      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>B 2 ISATK MEN McDOUGALL's Paterit SELF-RAISING FLOUR The Examiner, Saturday, April 29, 1905.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Easter Holidays.—Not a single member of the Royal Family spent any portion of the Easter holidays in London. The King and Queen were absent on their yachting cruise in the Alediterranian, and the Prince and Princess of Wales with their children have been at Sandringham. Their Royal Highnesses had arranged to go to Frogmore House, near Windsor, but a case of ill- ness their caused them to alter their plans, at the last moment, and the Prince and Princess have been spend- ing the holidays in Norfolk. The Duchess of Fife has been at Brighton, the Duke and Duchess of Connaught at Bagshot Park, whilst Prince and Princess Christian have been passing Eastertide at Cumberland Lodge. Princess Henry of Battenberg, and Princess Louise (Duchess of Argyll) have both been on the continent, but with the advent of May there will be a return of Royalties to London for the season, which really begins on the first of that month, with the opening of the Royal Academy, and the commencement of the Opera</p>
        <p>zeason.</p>
        <p>The Royal Academy.—To art lovers, the opening of the Royal Academy is the epoch of the year. The critics have not yet decided which is to be regarded as "the picture of the year," but it is certain that Mr. John Collier's "Bridge " painting entitled "The Cheat" will attract a good deal of attention, as it suggests two thoughts which are just now much before the public. In the portrait group, Lady Warwick—by Mr. Sargent—will be the principal figure. Last year it as her step-sister, the Duchess of Sutherland—also by this distinguished artist—who was the reigning beauty on the Academy walls. Lady Warwick, with her little son, is represented as standing upon a fight of steps in the open air' and few ss ould gather the inspiration from the picture that her ladyship is accustomed to address Socialist meetings in the great working-class centres. In the miniatures, Mr. Alyn Williams' portrait of the King will occupy the principal place. In last year's Academy there was a similar painting of Queen! Alexandra by the same artist. Holidays in the Open Air.—When Browning expressed , his longing to be in England because April had come, he must have been thinking of the joys of May,the flowers, I the sunshine, the songbirds, and the warm west wind. Such are the promises of spring, but it does not do to rely upon them,and the weather at Easter was anything but kindly for holiday makers, especially for the little band of men and women, whose idea of enjoyment was to spend the time in a cycle camp near Chesham in Buck- inghamshire.The party consisted of about thirty courage- 1 ous members of the Association of Cycle Campers, but I as the entire eqnipment has to be carried on the machines, it is all of the highest description. The so- called tents are not even canvas, but simply cotton cloth stretched on frail bamboo. On the ground, straw is laid, over which a waterproof sheet is placed, the occupants passing the night in "sleeping bags," whilst the cooking apparatus and other necessaries are marvels of lightness and compactness. The climatic conditions however bad, are not allowed to depress the spirits of the party, and the women are said to be more enthusias- tic than the men in braving such hardships. If that is so they must have enjoyed their Easter holiday im- mensely, whilst ninny of us preferred to stay indoors and indulge in fireside comforts, A Week of Weddings.—One of the most interesting of the many society weddings, followed upon Lent, was that of Lord Herbert Douglas Scott and Miss Maree Edwards, which took place in London. The bridegroom is a son of the Duke and Duchess of Buceleuch, and served in the South African campaign as A.D.C., to Lord Roberts. Elvetham, in Hampshire was the scene of another important wedding, the marriage of the Earl of Malmesbury to the Hon. Dorothy Calthorpe— the youngest daughter of Lord Calthorpe. The Earl of Malmesbury's family seat is at Heron Court, Christ- church, but the bride's family though settled in Hampshire, has large interests in the counties of Staffordshire, and Warwickshire. The prejudice against May marriages is responsible for the great rush of weddings since the end of Lent, but this antipathy is perhaps not so strong as it was and an important May wedding will be that of the Hon.' Stella Ridley, daughter of the late Lord Ridley, with Mr. Rupert Gwynne. This is to take place on the 3rd May, in the Speakers' Church of St. Margarets, Westminster, and the Parlia- mentary element will be largely represented at the wedding. A Society Engagement.—The engagement has just been announced of Lady Margaret Compton, the only daughter of the Marquis of Northampton. She is to marry Lord Loch, son of the first Baron, Sir Henry Loch one time High Commissioner for South Africa. Lord Loch who belongs to the Grenadier Guards, was badly wounded in the Boer war, but has quite recovered from a mishap which might have lamed him for life. He is fortunate in winning the hand of Lady Margaret Compton, who is a very important personage among the debutantbs of the past season or two. Not only is the Marquis of Northampton, who is a widower, immensely wealthy, but Lady Margaret inherits great riches from her grandmother, and she has been much sought after in Society. Though quite young she is a great sports- woman, and a dead shot with both gun and rifle. The Dangers of the Hat Pin.—The feminine hat pin is this week the subject of an attack by the "Lancet," which is always on the outlook to expose dangerous habits and practices. No one can deny that as a weapon of offence the hat pin is effective, and it has been fre- quently used in this sense by people who are prone to adopt violent methods of adjusting their quarrels. Nor is it free from the dangers of accident, as was shewn at a matinee performance in London, when a lady pinning ' her headgear to the seat in front, thrust the long hat pin through into the back of its startled occupant. But such accidents as are caused by hat pins are very few and far between, and it is at any rate to be feared that the ' Lancet's " appeal to milliners to devise some other means of fastening, will not prove very effectual. A Delightful Innovation.—All skirts gathered at the waist and about t's. hips, must be weighted at the hem, either with silk, velvet, or cloth, which should take the form of very open appliques. Voile skirts frequently I show appliqués of cloth about the hem, and an innova- tion is the smart cloth bodice to match such a skirt.! Then we have the cashmere bodice, and the crepe-de- chine, silk muslin, or voile skirt edged with bands or appliqués of cashmere. Both fabrics must match per- fectly or the scheme will spell failure. Evening skirts of tulle, net, chiffon, and other airy-fairy materials are also appearing with appliqués of cloth, or velvet from hem to knee, and undoubtedly this fashion makes for gracefulness and comfort, but the homeworker must ' see to it that the appliques are not too heavy, or they will tear away from the delicate fabrics upon which they are laid. Good Lace.—ln these days of inexpensive hand made lace, every woman should endeavour to possess a jabot, yoke, or even a vest of it. The value of a touch of good lace about the blouse or bodice is incalculable and provided it is washed and treated kindly such a possession may be said to last for a generation, at least. Delicate laces should be tacked lightly together, before being immersed in warm soapy water, in which they should be squeezed and shaken about until clean. They should then be doused well in several basins of clear cold water, squeezed and wrung in a cloth. The final process is that of gently pulling them out and pinning them on to a clear white cloth, that has been stretched over a table. Quantities of pins are necessary and the table should be a plain wooden ono so that the pins can be stuck into it when necessary.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>Are you getting full value out of your cocoa?</p>
        <p>Some cocoas are difficult of</p>
        <p>digestion.</p>
        <p>Van Houten's Cocoa can be</p>
        <p>assimilated by the weakest digestion. Try it.</p>
        <p>Van Houten's</p>
        <p>the easily digested cocoa.</p>
