      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>WOMAN'S REALM.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>MATRIMONY :w TRIBET.</p>
        <p>Courtship and matrimony are by no means periods of unalloyed bliss to the Thibetan woman, whose affairs of the heart are settled for her without any reference to her wishes. For several weeks the father of the prospective bride and the would-be suitor carry on a system of bargaining, before the necessary sum is paid down, and the bride is secured. She is then led to the house of her husband, and her spirit humbled by a severe beat- ing, after which she is forced to run round the village proclaiming the merits and valour of her lord and master.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>PORTRAITS OF FAIR WO3lE3r.</head>
        <p>PORTRAITS OF FAIR WO3lE3r.</p>
        <p>Every beautiful woman nowadays has her portrait painted. The pictures of fair women of a bygone generation have risen enormously in value of late years, and command a much' higher price than those of mere men by the same artist. Our descendants will be well off. Nearly all the interesting women of to-day will be represented in this year's Academy or the New Gallery. Amongst many others, Sargent is sending his pictures of the Countess of Warwick, the Marl- borough family, and also that of Lady Helen Vincent to the Academy, whilst Lady (lan) Hamilton and the Countess of Selbome, by Harris Browne, will adorn the walls of the New Gallery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>VISITING TOILET.</head>
        <p>VISITING TOILET.</p>
        <p>Our sketch shews a visiting toilet of line biscuit-coloured chiffon broadcloth. The bodice is draped to the figure, and opens in front over a little vest of white silk embroidered with silver. The turn-back revers are also of white silk, and are edged with black velvet and silver braid. Leg-or- mutton sleeves draped over the arm terminate in little cuffs and tight undersleeves. The skirt is</p>
        <p>... ( V , / 1 t/l ~ • \ ! : I 0 , 1 ,010 11 witn/tr.iiii•or w Jo—, ; ' 1 I' .0 It , _ I • ' woo -,,-.,), u ;;;•-ip ... . ,11 11'11 : --- I \,</p>
        <p>novel in snape, ana consists of three deep flounces, the top one being set into a shaped hip-yoke. A plain panel of cloth forms the front of the skirt, preventing the flounces from having too full an effect. The hat which accompanies this visiting costume is of white satin straw, trimmed with black velvet and bunches of La France roses.</p>
        <p>fl 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Gossip.</head>
        <p>Gossip.</p>
        <p>A resident in Marylebone says he can testify that there are to be found in his parish nursing homes the lady superintendents of which never had any recognised medical training, and whose "nursing staff" consists of the kitchenmaid, the parlour. maid, and the general servant, each of whom is ready to don a nurse's uniform when the occasion , requires it. It is the increasing vanity of women, the rage for dress, declares Lady Violet Greville, which has ' produced the race of white slaves, the women who toil on scanty food, herded together in stifling rooms for fourteen hours a day to earn a shilling, to produce the cheap goods in. which smartly. dressed women revel. The Grand Duchess Marie and Princess Beatrice of Saxe-Coburg will be the guests of the King and Queen at Windsor Castle during the Whitsuntide holidays, after which they will pay a short visit to Princess Henry of Battenberg at Kensington Palace.</p>
        <p>The Countess of Gosford, whose daughters are all golf enthusiasts, has presented a silver cup recently to the Co. Armagh Golf Club for foursome com- petition. The cup is to become the property of nny lady winning it two years in succession.</p>
        <p>A USEFUL WRAP.</p>
        <p>An extra wrap is always a useful addition to It wardrobe, especially during the treacherous weather of spring and early summer. Our illustration shews a wrap that would be equally serviceable for walking or driving. The shape is quite simple, consisting of a sack coat with wide sleeves. These new surplice sleeves give all its distinction to the</p>
        <p>coat, and are very graceful and becoming. Two wide bands of embroidery edged with velvet and tiny knife-pleated frills of silk form the only trimming. The wrap fastens down the front with two rows cf small fancy buttons. JAPANESE CHILDREN'S GOLDEN RULE.</p>
        <p>The mothers of Japan teach their children accord- ing to the book called "Teaching of Children," which is regarded as the embodiment of the Golden Rule. This book teaches that children should be severely punished for lying or deceit. A boy should be brave, but tender. Should he run home, chased by some superior antagonist, the door will be closed to him by the mother. "Do not fear the loss of life when it is for a righteous cause; but you should shew a tender heart even to your enemy when he is at your mercy," is the teaching of this book, which is most carefully in- culcated by the mothers of Japan.</p>
        <p>To-nay's RECIPE.</p>
        <p>- BOUCHEES DE PIGEONS. —Remove the flesh from , two pigeons, pound in a mortar, and pass through a wire sieve. Make a panacla of two tablespoon. I fuls of flour, loz. of butter, and a little milk. ' When the panada is quite cold (it must he rather thick) mix it with the pigeon, add a gill of cream, and season well with pepper and salt. Have little flat ornamental moulds well greased and sprinkled 1 with hard-boiled egg (grated) and a little fresh, inely-chopped parsley. Fill the moulds quite full, and steam for about half-an-hour. Serve in a silver dish, and pour round a white sauce, to which . has been added a little of the pigeon stock, the ; yolk of an egg, a tablespoonful of cream, and a few &amp;owed ...v.shrooms.</p>
