      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>HELEN MATHERS</head>
        <p>HELEN MATHERS</p>
        <p>(Author of" Co' thro' the Rye, ''ac.)</p>
        <p>The woman was tired. She bowed her head on ller hands, and looked back. _ _ _</p>
        <p>A happy childhood, sometimes ruffled, but not marred, by the old impossibility of bridging the gap between youth and its teachers and masters; a capacity for happiness so intenem as to border on pain ; a power of suffering that plunged her often into an outer darkness to which no glimmer of light could pierce; a quick intelligence that took microscopic note of everything that passed around her; and, lastly, a heart charged with pas- sion that was bound to blunder often before it found its mats—thus did her story unroll itself to her closed eyes, just as if it were another woman's life, and she but a careless and unwilling spec- tator of it. The child hail done no harm. It had grown and thriven, and stored up health and energy against the fight that had to be fought in womanhood ; afterwards she never ceased to thank God for those years of incalcula- ble value, for the stout armour that her parents had buckled on her when she was little, the ar- mour of health, and courage, and sincerity, and good principles, and that stood her in better stead than if they had weighted her liartds with gold. And the girl, what of her? When the freight is precious, and 1111 ignorant young captain sails without a compass on the open sea, shall he not drift more than once into the wrong port, and ex- change his rare merchandise to cruel disadvant- age? Yet will he again mail away, taking heart of grace, and thinking himself grown the wiser for his lesson, but if imagination have sealed his eyes, he may bring his galleon at last to a barren Shore where treasure is not, nor health, nor love, nor anything with which to pacify a starving, passionate body and soul. - " I was only a child," she said_ to ' herself, " when I fell in love—and being the first, it was probably the most ardent love of my life. He was my spiritual master—he treated me as a child, yet he would lay a flower, or e, bit of sweet briar on My Shakespeare as he passed to the tabl where he gave us our lesson .. . he was always good to me till we went our different ways, and made my school life entirely happy. " He was then about eight and twenty, and of very good birth : he was also without exception the handsomest, the best bred, and I think the proudest man (in its finest sense) I ever knew in my life, and lie must have possessed rare self - control, and been of tough moral fibre also, to walk unmoved through that garden of many of them lovely, and all worshipping, yet never by word or look, show that lie regarded them otherwise as young people whom he desired to see happy, and for whom he procured all the indulgences in his power. " In that tiny community (perched on the brow of a hill, and surrounded by other hills and val- leys that were beautiful at almost every season of the year), consisting only as it did of the Schools, the Rectory, the Church, together with the one great house of the neighbourhood, our girls' focus of life was very narrow, very intense, and he stood in the very eye of it, and when the tall slender figure, and the clean-shaven brown face with its wonderful eyes disappeared from our ken, the picturesque charm of life there, to many of us, was over. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Somehow I knew he had a genuine liking for the little wayward, original child, who was always in mischief, and that he hated to part with me, later. Years afterwards I found that he had pre- served every scrap of anything I had • worked or given him. I knew that my picture hung over the writing table in his study, a ?deg Merrilies, with a mane of curly hair that he used gravely i to assure me I was too lazy to do up into the curls he liked. "Sometimes he took us all for a walk, and he would take my hand (I barely reached his elbow) and we would lead the rest, going over hedges and ditches like racers and lie would stop and laugh, and look back to the rueful left-behinds who panted after us, and who sometimes made him furious by rafusing to climb gates before him, for mock-modesty was the thing he most abhorred in women, and like most men of his type he had a rare temper of his own, though he seldom deigned to shew it. "And when that distinguished presence passed out of our exquisite little chapel for ever, and as I supposed also out of my life, my love for him, absent, only grew in strength and persistency ; I could not foresee the day when I should love himn less, and he kept my heart absolutely closed to all other men, from the age of fourteen to twenty. "One day in that new school where they were trying to polish me into a counter that would pass muster decently in the world's currency, I was walking at the head of the girls, when look- ing up, I saw him on the other side of the wide road, walking as he often did, his arms behind his back, his dark head a little bent, vivid and perfect as a flawless black and white etching against the spring tints behind him. I stopped short, rooted to the ground, incapable of crying out, of moving a finger to attract his attention, overwhelmed by the reality and force of my proved love, for I was seventeen then, and a woman. He passed on with his long, graceful step, and the girls thought me raving mad, be- cause, though I would not tell them the truth, I could not force back the incoherent words that came to my lips,' and I would sooner have died than called him to me, or moved a step to follow him. " When and how did that love cease? It was warm and living when I refused the men who loved me, when I tentatively accepted others, hoping that they might drive out that old pas- sion, ... but they could not, and, in the grown woman's heart, as in the child's, he was king. " Yet one day I found that love stone-cold and dead, knew that if he and I came face to face, I should know no tremor, feel no thrill of awe or gladness, and it was only thus I became aware that often as I had fiercely vowed I could never love but once, I had loved again and I was ashamed of the inconstancy. I even doubted the sincerity of those six years of devotion—yet they were very real, very true, and looking back, it seems to me that this was the best and purest love of my life. For imagination ruled it, and I had a fine ideal—be had not wronged the least of those little ones over whom he had been set as pastor and master, in look or word, and every one of those girls must to her life's end have thought better of men for his sake, even if she compared them unfavourably with him. " And when, just before my marriage, we met accidentally at a house in Town, 1 was able to say to myself calmly: " How handsome he is !" hit were not his eyes wistful as they followed ins about—did his entire absorption in what I said and did and looked, not prove that be had as vivid• ly remembered me (only in a different way) as I had remembered him ? I sighed and was silent on going away. Afterwards we met again. and his mother asked me to his home, and looked at me so earnestly, so jealously that sometimes it seemed to me that I had just missed a great hap- piness ... all my life I have ply 4 :rod in love up to my lips' yet just missed it and what of love is there beyond? " Full and brimming I brought my own cup of love to the feast, but like many another foolish woman who puts her faith in one almoner, and he the careless, selfish man bent on filling his own purse, I came at last to go often hungry, and marriage, the irreconcilable foe to the ideal, thrust Love's mask completely awry, and 41)e w ell him human, pathetic, even grotesque at times in his weakness, and with no illusions at all about him. Gradually I came to pity this poor reality of a glorious dream, and hid its nakedness from the world, and my great capacity for a great love turned at last into that protective mother- instinct towards the weak, which endures the longest of any, and is perhaps the most pro- foundly starved of all the baulked desires that ravage the human heart. " He loved me in his own fashion, but be went the way that is the way of most men, RTIII I ICTIOW it, and he knew that I knew it, and it was like :t wall between us, and often in my misery I ar- raiwnes, 51011- fa'ilinn men -v%o were at once so weak and so good that one could neither hate and cast them out as utterly evil, nor yet wholly respect them, so that one f2lWlllig always between contempt of them, and a pity more than half akin to affection. And after many years of that life, in which I warred nu- teasingly between the smart of degradation, and his necessity for mercy and forgiveness, the third man, and the last, came into my life, and I plucked my standard of manhood out of the dust and purified it, and raised it higher than before —for him. .</p>
        <p>nu. um, .k wallie Inilf) that the miracle of spring leul beeu re-enacted in my heart, for this was neither the young priest's love for the child at school, nor that of the experi- enced man of the world who coveted, and took, and passed on his way to other fruit:, but one that be- ,4ltrei.o6 1)11pm:el nttraethm. p1e3,04 Iwo kmeu intellectual comradeakip and sympathy, in which we could sit silent, whether alone or in oompany, perfectly comprehending one another. " Stevenson has luminously put sal love as thr ito cmpressiou when he says: " • in the closest of all relations that ofe:ove well founded mid equally shared—speech is half discarded . and the two communicate directly hy their presence, and with few looks, and fewer words, continue to share their good and evil, and uphold each other's hearts in joy.' So with us, whatsoever place each might be in, was light or dark, according to the presence or absence of the other, for we were affinities, in soul and heart and body, and we had suet too late. All that we had both missed in our lives, showed a vivid palpitating reality when we two stood face to face—and we had the grace to know it, and the strength to realise that each of us must go hungry to his life's end. 4" You will never be happy in marriage, for you idealise too much,' a soothsayer said to me once, but it taught me no lesson, for a t once began to idealise this last lover in my life, and gradu- ally he came to know it, and tried to live up to it, and when those first years of fever and steeple in him ;were over, we settled down as devoted comrades, and he !less openly rebelled at the fate that had separated us. " It is not the quantity of a woman's lovers that do her honour, but the quality of them, and I lcould never quite understand why a man so Len- ient, so resolute, and of so great genius, gave such unstinted devotion to such a woman as I, alio was lavish in displaying to him my faults, while my heart I hid trots him. And through all the years, and they were many, hiitm love grew, and afterwards I knew that he had never spoketi my name but to praise me —so richly will it great soul cloak the poverty of what it loves. I look back now upon those years t f life and colour, and ask myself, " was I mad that I did not enjoy them more—did not count greedily every hour of a spiritual friendship more precious far than any perishable lover's tie? Under other circumstances, such splendour of love might surely lame had it' glorious ending, but as it was, each was able to throw sunlight, not shadow, on the other's path, ' and it never occurred to me that the light wound ; not always fall on mine, that one day I must walk alone in darkness to the end, since no other love ; could ever be possible to me again. " And before I realised that he was ill even, j and, without any last farewell to me, he died. Ile ' died, and my life, that had flowed so strong and ;ell when his supplemented it, like a river divert- ing forcibly from its bed, spread in a desolation that for a time almost swept use away. " It was winter, and I went to the seashore, and for long days roamed beside it : the roar' of the surf, the keening of the wind, and the sul- lenness of the scene was akin to my mood, and insensibly soothed it . and there mingled with them some lines he loved, and often repeated to me, dear for the inner zneaniug they held for us both.</p>
        <p>Since you noo mwore boat my side In walks, in zuminer bet, I'll goo along where mist do ride Through trees a-dripping wet. Below the rain-wet bough, ray love, Where thou did'st never come, An' I don't grieve to miss ye now As I do grieve at whome.' " I came back at last, to take up my life with- out him, but every day brought more home to inc the truth that the only part of my life in which I had really lived, would return no more. For I had thrown my last stake for love, and I bad lost, and I knew that so it was to be : that I who had loved deeply and sincerely three times in my life, was never to be happy in love, was never to know the sweetness of being all things to any one man, but only to be a child to this one, a toy to another' and nothing but a friend to him who would have made of my. life a thing homogene- ous and perfect, as God intended that our lives ; should be, and not of shred and patches as fashioned by the laws of man. " Greyly the future spread out before me, and tub one who had departed, methought, was in- finitely better off than I. And as the years passed, he receded from me, and the first sharp agony at his loss, constantly recurring, diminished little by little ; and I was able to think about him, to talk of him, to realise the great factor for good that his love had been in my life, how it had even influenced my attitude towards my care- less, uncomprehending husband, for it had taught me the universal love, and not the merely per- sonal, selfish one that loves only what ministers to its own gratification. " But lately, since I have been ill—for to the emotionally worn-out heart, sometimes physical failure comes mercifully to still it—, I seem to be drifting away from the living man, with his too- late remorseful, tender care of me, towards the one who loved me better than any one ever loved me before, or than any one will over love me again. "If there be a Hereafter, he waits at the door for me,—if there be none, our dust will still bold the fragrance of a great passion that we strove, and not vainly, to keep free of earthly stain. And as in life we never bade each other a last fare- well, so we never put into words the hope that what was denied us here, might be granted to us in another world .. . we only wanted to be to- gethet, whether as spirits or those finer humans that the Final Cause may have elected that we should be .. I wonder is he changed, and have I too lost the brightness that he loved ? Who can tell? _ _ .</p>
        <p>"Perhaps the barriers will fall at last —perhaps for him and for ine there shall cease to be that empty place where tbor«lid' st never come,' and we will tell each other all that has happened in .between, during these half-dozen years in which we have been parted. " I will tell him of how much of the fine work lie did in life, still goes forward, of how his memory waxes with every year, so that even his enemies who hated him. cannot deny the splendour of his achievements. I will tell him how I took pattern by his example, and tried to be brave, and make the best of life, though often I faltered, for no one understood ; only to myself sometimes I said : He knows .0 And indeed I have often that feel- ing that lie is watching me, that it is my fault that I do not catch his whisper of encouragement . . somehow he seems very near to me to-night not as spirit —it is that personal sense of him I always had when he entered a loom .. and I am tired, so tired. • • - -</p>
        <p>- , 0 ! that t' were possible, after long grief and pain, To feel the arms of my true love, round me once again.' " • • * or • * * 4, " Come !" said his masterful voice in her ear, and her heart leaped this was true, this was real : " You drove me away from you—you tried to live alone, to make the best of Item .it was no sp,a —come ! At last we will be together—none can hinder us now—sve did our duty by those shallow souls to whom fate bound us, and now we belong to them no longer, but to one another. All that was best of us, the soul, the essence remains— all that in moments of exaltation we saw beyond the earthly veil we will enjoy together : for me, Heavers was no Heaven, where thou did'st never come !' " A great storm of breathless joy thrilled her— through her locked hands she saw the strenuous dark face, felt his possessive touch on her arm . . often thus he had come to her in her sleep, cud looked in her eyes for welcome, and she bad wakened in eestacy only to remember that he was dead, so that now she feared to move, to speak, lest be should depart from her yet Aomelmw she knew hat this was his very self ... that to her, at the very last. love bad come, to stau . . . in a rapture i la:L.111M sip: telt et, arms close liko steel about her, ond with a long deep sigh of con- tent, as one who sinks into a sweet, deep slum- ber, She turned her face into the haven of his breast, and swiftly lie bore her away. • • I * * *</p>
        <p>„ She is „leap,” snid one who entered on tip- toe the room into which was shining the morning sun, while yet the lamps burned brightly that had been lit overnight. „ shei dead," suid another weeping, as she tried to unlock the clasped hands that hid the glory of the woman's face. She did not suffer, she died happy," said her husband, presently, as through blinding tears he stood and looked down upon her. She looked then us she laid done before he took her life into his :muds, rind slowly extinguished the light of El.a did not - know that another mon's love had u.r7;:gi.t back all its lost splendour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] TIM WISE MEN OF GOTHAM",</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] TIM WISE MEN OF GOTHAM",</p>
        <p>h. BARING-GOULD (Author of " .Ifehalah," " Cheap Jack Zi(a," ie.).</p>
        <p>Why an obscure village of about nine hundred people in Nottinghamshire should have acquired the credit of producing the greatest fools in England, is bard to discover, for the popular tale to account for it is absolutely unhistorical, and attaches itself to Gotham s a tentative ex. planation of a generally received opinion. " As wise as a fool of Gotham" is a proverb, and the nursery rhyme concerning the Got. hamites runs</p>
        <p>Three wise men of Gotham Went to sea in a bowl. If that bowl had been slronger, My story had been longer. Gotham is not by the sea, it is very far inland. Three Gothamites, wandering on adventure bent, on reaching the margin ot the ocean, out of mere ignorance or stupidity, got into a bowl as an effective and serviceable boat—and that was the end of them.</p>
        <p>The popular story to account for Gotham breeding fools is this. King John was marching towards Nottingham and intended passing through Gotham meadows. The villagers were led to suppose that the ground over which a King passed became for •ver afterwards a public road, and not being minded to part with their meadows so cheaply, sent a deputation to the King to say that there was no way through Gotham to Nottingham or, anywhere else. According to another version of -the thief Kiug. john sent to requisition men and money to assist lAimin his wars, and the Gotham people replied that there were no men among --Abcm, and certainly no money. Highly incensed at their proceedings, King John sent to enquire the reason of this in- civility, with the intention of chastising them. The Gothamites, made aware of the approach of the commissioners, were as anxious to escape the conseqtences of the monarch's displeasure as they had been to evade having him pass through their lands or obtain supplies of them. A COUNCIL OF FOOLS.</p>
        <p>• We are not told how they deliberated, but consult they did, and agreed on a course of action ,calculated to exculpate them in the King's eyes. When the royal commissioners arrived at, Gotham they found some of the inhabitants labouring to drown an eel in a pond ; some :were dragging their carts and wains, laden with tiles' to the top of a bani, because the barn had to be roofed, and it would save trouble —by this means all the tiles would be brought up at once instead of in instalments. Some Gothamites were rolling cheeses down hill in expectation that they would find their own way to Nottingham market. Some, again, were en- gaged in hedging in a cuckoo, which had perch?d on a bush, because they desired to re- tain he bird with them throughout the year. And, indeed, about a mile south of Gotham stands a bush to this day, that goes by the name of " Cuckoo Bush," which is supposed to have been the very bush in question, or one planted on its site. _ _</p>
        <p>When the King's officers arrived, and saw the G'othenntes thus occupied, they were so satisfied that they were fools, that they returned to King John and told bim that they were un- worthy of his notice, incapable of rendering him any service, and beneath his, resentment. ' They, of course, WEI outwitted the King, *and though pronounced to be fools they were wise in their fooling. • _ _</p>
        <p>The story is very old. In the reign of Henry VIII. was published The, Merry Tales of the Mad Men of Gotham." Old Fuller, comment- ing on the story says : " Gotham doth breed as tc;lBs people as any which causelessly laugh at their But they hiive had other defenders beside Fuller. A poet wrote : Tell me no more of Gotham fools, Or of their eels, in little pools, Which they, were told, were drowning; Nor of their carts drawn up on high, When King John's men were standing by, To keep a wood from browning. Nor of their cheese shov'd down a hill, Nor of the cuckoo sitting still, While it they hedged around ; Such tales of those have long been told, By prating boobies young and old, In drunken circles crowned. The fools are those who thither go • • To see the Cuckoo Bush, 'I trow, The wood, the barn, the pools ; For such are seen both here and there, And passed by without a sneer, By all but arrant fobls.' But, according to Mr. Mark Antony, Lowes Sussex was, the county of the Gothamites. Gotham is a little place, not a parish, near Pevensey. and many traditionary anecdotes are still current hi Sussex respecting the stupidity of its people. On one occasion the Mayor, having received a letter, was reading it upside down ' • the messenger, very respectfully, sug- gested that he wouls sooner arrive at the mean- ing of its contents by reversing its position. " Hold your tongue, sir," replied the chief magistrate ; " for Wl:il4 I am Mayor of Pevensey I'll hold the letter which end upp'ards I like." Surely Shak?.speara amst have had the Got- hamites in his mind's eye when he represented Dogberry, Verges • and the Watchmen, those ' shallow and incredible blunderers, who yet with. their folly diseove: '.hat was not seen by the eves of wise men. " Pont thou not ,suspect .my place? Post thou not suspect my years? 0 that he were here to wr:te me down—an ass ! But, mnstecr, remember that I am an ass ! though it be not *ritten down, ye.; forget not that I am en ass."</p>
        <p>DEA:sZ SWIFT'S WISE ILEN.</p>
        <p>Surely also Swift must have had a reminiscence of the story of the Wise Fools of Gotham when, in " A Voyage to Laputa," he describes an island materially fertile rendered desolate be- cause made the field for the experiments of the Professors of the Academy of Sciences of Laputa. Gulliver is represented as visiting the Academy. " I was very kindly received by the warden. Every room hall in it one or more projectors ; and I believe I could not be in fewer than fire hundred rooms. The first man I saw had been eight years upon a project for extracting sun- beams out of cucumbers, which were to be put in vials hermetically sealed, and let out to warm the air in raw, inclement summers. He told me he did not doubt that in eight years more be would be able to supply the governor's. garden with sunshine at a reasonable rate. . " I saw another at work to calcine ice into gunpowder, who likewise showed me a treatise he had written on the malleability of fire, which he intended to publish. " There was a most ingenious architect, whe bad contrived a new method of building houses by beginning at the roof, and working down- wards to the foundation.</p>
        <p>" Then was a man born blind, who had several appientices in his own condition ; their employment was to mix colours for painters, which their master taught them to distinguish by feeling and smelling. This artist is much encouraged und esteemed by the whole frater- nity. "In another apartment I was highly pleased with a projector, who had found a device ol ploughing, the ground with hogs, to save the charges of ploughs, male and labour. The method was this :—ln an acre of ground you bury at six inches distance and eight deep a quantity of acorns. Then you drive six hundred of these imimals into the field, when in a few days they will root up the whole ground in search of their food, and make it fit for sowing, " I went in to another room where the walls and ceilings were hung round with cobwebs. The projector here lamented the fatal mistake the world had been so long in of using silk- worms, while we had such a plenty of domestic tweets who infinitely er.celled the former, be- came they understood how to weave as well as spin. " There was an astronomer who had under- taken to place a sundial upon the great weather_ cock on the town house, by adjusting the annual and diurnal motions of the earth and sun, so as to answer and coincide with all accidental turnings of the wind."</p>
        <p>THE GoTHAMITES OF OTHER LANDS. But, the story of the Wise Men of Gotham does not pertain to Gotham alone, or only to England. It is found elsewhere. The Greeks considered the Boeotian as fools, and their philosopher lieiroeles wrote a collection of atnus- ing stories makin.s.7 fun of pedants, and telling tales that are now " chestnuts," as that of tho</p>
        <p>1dt1.14. Vh, (ll) (1.13 •At. It. I I et al“, 0. a,. • •• ,•• would not go near water twain till lie haci learned to swim ; and of anotlTer; who desiring to sell his houae, carried about a brick from it In his pocket, to exultut MAI A :ap‘clrueu ot utak&amp; his house was.</p>
        <p>The French regard the Gaseous as both tools and braggarts, and have many tales concerning them. So have the Germans about the Swabians. lint the nearest approach to the story ot the Wise Men of Gotham is that told of the Schild- burger. The men of Schildburn• were so wise that they were in demand throughout Europe, to act as ministers to the kings and princes, and to oc- cupy all government offices. This led to only women being left at borne, and these wrote a a petition to their husbands to return, and then persuaded them to cultivate their own fields and town politics, and not go forth to direct the affairs of great states. This they agreed to do ; and having re- solved that they inust have a town hall, in order to build it, they went to a hill to cut down timber. As a good many logs rolled to the bottom, they went down and rolled them up again, so that all the felled trees might roll to- gether down. Thal, built their town hall, but when it was complete found that it was so dark inside that they could not read their protocols and minutes. So they met in consaltation and resolved that as the roof excluded the light, they must lake it off again. , This they did, but the winter rains came on and they were fain to replace the roof. They again consulted, and determined to catch sunbeams and force them into the council house. Some attempted to do this by holding bushels, or sacks in the sun, and then tanning into the hall and emptying them there, whilst otliers.sgaias•ttett' mouse-traps wherewith to catclt the beains,•:. and others again • attempted to shovel the daylight in. A traveller passing through suggested win- dows. Accordingly they set to work to knock Lulea' through the walls, and as each member of the Town Council Would require light, each was furnished with a window.</p>
        <p>As the Town Hall proved cold, the Schild- burgers passed a resolution nem. eon. to have a large stove. This was procured, but as it would take too much room within, it was erected outside, and a trapper was employed to set his nets before the stove door to catch the heat as it came out and deliver it tci the city council within.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>FINE CROP OF SALT.</head>
        <p>A FINE CROP OF SALT.</p>
        <p>On account of the wars, salt proved a scarce and expensive commodity, so the Town Council ordered that a field should be sown with salt that they might have a crop next year. In spring all that sprang up was, sting nettles ; and a depu- tation from the corporation went to see the crop and report on it. They tasted the herbs and stung their lips and tongues, and returned to dnuounce that the crop was one of extraordinary strength and excellent quality. As at Gotham so at Schildburg, the King announced his attention of paying the place a visit. A most loyal welcome was drawn up, hi rhyme, which the Mayor was to pronounce. But when the King arrived and the corpora- tion in their robes went forth to, receive him, the Mayor deemed it his duty to take off his hat, and as he required both hands for applause, beheld his hat in his mouth. •</p>
        <p>The King said : • " Yon Schildburgers, as through your town we pass—" The Mayor ought to have responded in rhyme, but could not as his mouth was full of the rim of his hat, but a chimney sweep yelled out : You' Majesty—the Mayor's an ass an ass !" and all the, councillors and the Mayor himself applauded, some shouting " Hear ! Hear !" and others clapping their hands. The,sovereign was so satisfied that the Schild- burgers were all fools that he - exempted them from paying all dues and taxes. The German story. is much fuller than that of the Men of Gotham, but it is obvious that both derive from a common original. But why the credit ,of being shrewd feols should have adhered to one little place in Nottinghamshire must remain a puzzle. That the Gotham folk are shrewd fools is a jest never forgotten in the neighbourhood. The other day a man was riding into Nottingham, on a saddle but without stir- rups. Helm !" said a farmer, who encountered him. " Saddle and no stirrups ! you must be a Gothanl man." •</p>
        <p>" That's just where I've come from," was the rejoinder, " and I have come to show you that we Gotbamites can do without things that the &amp;els outside regard as necessaries."</p>
      </div>
