      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>UNDERGROUND TELEGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>UNDERGROUND TELEGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Complete From London to Warrington.</p>
        <p>The engineering department of the General Post Office report that good progress has been made during the past three months in laying the underground tele- graph line to the North, and it is expected that the permanent cable will be ready for opening towards the end of May, although no date can at present be fixed. What is called the "pipe" line from London to Glasgow has been completed for some time with the exception of a short section between Bothwell and Hamilton, the construction of which bes been delayed in consequence of differences with the highway authorities. The permanent cable has been completed between London andWarrington Warrington and Liverpool, and Warrington and Man- chester. A temporary cable has been drawn into the pipes between lianchester. Leeds, and Bradford. The permanent cable has also been drawn in between War- rington and Carlisle, but the work is not yet completed on that portion of the line.</p>
        <p>-a- TO SUCCEED lAN MACLABEN.</p>
        <p>It is stated that the Rev. George H. Morrison, of Wellington United Free Church, Glasgow—the leading church of the body—is likely to be the successor to the Rev. Dr. Watson, of Sefton Park Presbyterian Church, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
