      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>. STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>. STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>BArTISMAL Snavrcc.—On Sunday evening, before a crowded congregation, the ordinance of baptism was administered at Hill Cliffe Baptist Church. The The Pastor (the Rev. J. S. Hughes) took as his theme "The witness of baptism." He claimed that baptism, as observed by them, and in accordance with the practice of the ancient church, bore a witness which no other form of baptism could give. It was a personal act of consecration, and contended strongly that the Church had suffered great loss by the change from the §criptural method of baptism to sprinkling and pouring, neither of which had the same power of witnessing as Immersion. He quoted from Dr. Rathdall's book" Christus Ecclesia," in which the writer said " When we look at what baptism was in primitive times, and what it is now, we may well ask ourselves whether the Church has done wisely to change this solemn pro- fession of personal self-dedication into a ceremony per- formed as a matter of course over every unconscious i infant. Of nfant baptism in the New Testament, or in immediately post-apostolic times, there is not a single trace. We hear nothing of it till the latter half of the second century, and then it is a moot point whether children should be baptised and at what age? These, said Mr. Hughes, were the words of an Anglican pro- fessor of high attainments, and he would commend them not only to his fellow-Christians of the Anglican community, but also to the Free Churches, with whom he delighted to co-operate, and would ask them if it was not time for them to give up a practice which haa no authority in scripture, and was still a barrier to union amongst the churches of Christ. The candidates —three men and four women—were then immersed. This is the second baptism within six weeks and there are others awaiting submission to the ordinance.</p>
        <p>.1</p>
        <p>. N; . I '1!</p>
      </div>
