      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>7'HE BLAC,Ir MOTOR CAR. By J. B. HARRIS HURLAND.</head>
        <p>7'HE BLAC,Ir MOTOR CAR. By J. B. HARRIS HURLAND.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VI. MR. ARTHUR HOLMC. . 7,tronr.tv after 6 o'clock most of the guests had departed, and Lady Agnes, released from Dart rd her social duties, cut the rest and slipped away unobserved to the bottom of garden. , As she reached the gate. which opened out through a thick yew hedge into the park beyond, there was a rustling in the bushes by the edge of the path, and her favrurite Irish . terrier came sheepishly to her side. His nose ' was covered with mould, and there was little 'doubt he had been engaged in unlawful , pursuits. Oh, Ginger," she said, in a tone of re- proach. The dog looked up at her witt pathetic brown eyes and wagged his tail :slowly and doubtfully. She stooped down And patted his rough brown coat. , "Come along, you old villain," she said, ; holding the gate open for him to pass through, . and then the two went together down the sloping grasslands of the park, under the great spreading oaks and chestnuts, and through a narrow copse which fringed this side of the Heatherstone estate for two miles. They emerged into a small open space thickly overgrown with bracken. Beyond it • lay the stone wall, which bordered on the road. Everywhere else the -copse touched the wall, and the explanation of this open space was not far to seek. In the centre of it lay the ruins of a small cottage, a mere heap of grey stones, almost hidden by the ferns, which sprung up rouud it. That which was now a piece of waste land had evidently been once a garden. Lady Agnes brushed her way through , the bracken, and climbing up to the highest part ,_ of the ruins, looked out over the wall. The ,7 view - that lay before her eyes presented as k strange contrast to the wooded slopes of the, I- park behind her. On the other side of the road flowed a sluggish brown stream several yards in width. The tide was coming in, but on either side of the water there was still a narrow belt of mud, brown on the surface but , slack as coal underneath. Two small boats lay on the mud, and a ~' couple of oyster smacks were anchored out in , mid-stream. A --thin curl of blue smoke rose from the stove pipe .of one of them. Deep , black holes in the mud showed where the fishermen had walked ashore from their , stranded dingeys. ' Beyond the creek the flat marshes, seamed - with little gulleys and dykes, ran for miles, till they reached the sea. To the south the , creek itself narrowed down into a mere ditch. To the north it widened and widened, until it turned round a bend and. went eastward, to tistally join a broad estuary that swept through square miles of sand and mud into the ocean. Here and there a small cottage showed up from the waste around it, the home of some lonely fisherman, or of one of the - v.-atehmen of the oyster fisheries. These habitations seemed only to accentuate the , loneliness of the desolate marshes. It was a familiar scene to Lady Agnes Cliffe, but it was •. one that had never lost its fascination for her. - To some people the waste places of the earth, s the marshes, the deserts, the lonely fields of :, ice, and snow, are only barren and uninterest- ing. But the imaginative mind finds more , room for its luxuriant fancy iu such spots than . in the most beautiful landscapes. To Lady • Agnes, as she looked out from the border of her father's splendid park, these dreary i., marshes represented the unknown, the unex- plored, the edge of the world. , • As children, she and Lord Harry had sat secure in their .broken stronghold of grey stones and looked out towards the east, watch- in' for the enemy that never came though - they firmly believed that he lay hidden some- where beyond the muddy creek. Often of an afternoon they would spy his movements in the white sail of a distant boat, in the stir of • some rushes, in the rustling of a bush, in the sudden flight' of a heron os some wild duck. - But they never saw him, though they knew well where he lived. To the north-east, and distant some eight miles as the crow flew, a small stony hillock rose up against the sky. On the side of it stood a large red brick house. It was almost invisible from their fortress, but both of them had explored the long road which ran to it between two creeks. the house had always, as far as they could remember, been unoccupied. It was a gaunt pile of red brick with no glass in the shuttered windows. Just the hiding place for a secret 'foe Neither of the children had ever had any doubt that this was the actual home of their unseen enemy. Lady Agnes looked at it on 'this particular afternoon, and smiled at the remembrance of her childish fancies. Yet she `vas still child enough to regard the place with k some suspicion. It was odd that it had not ss • been let for so many years. Her thoughts were interrupted by the dog, who hurriedly scrambled from her side and , brushed his way through the bracken, barking s cheerfully. In a few moments Lord Harry appeared. She rose and waved her hand to •,,, him. He strode through the bracken and smiled. But for all that there was an un- '• svonted look of anxiety on his brown face. "I am sorry, Agnes, to keep you waiting," lllie said, as he climbed up the heap of stones to her side, "but really, these politicians are most s' insistent people. My head is going round." lie sat down by her side and looked so earnest- ly at her that she turned her eyes away to the ~- north. ' "I've been all right, Harry," she said, "1 have been looking at the red castle, the home of our ancient foe." The young man laughed. "It's been let," he said. "No more room 'for imagination in that quarter. I rode out there the other day. There was glass in the windows, and the garden is being cleared up With a scythe." "Who's taken it ? " she asked. " I don't know," he replied. "A stranger to these parts, I should say. It is eight miles from a shop, you know." "Thus end the dreams of our childhood," the girl said with a 'smile. "I have no doubt ue is a worthy man and will come to our garden parties. Do you recollect, Harry, how we pictured him a tall red-bearded scoundrel, with yellow teeth and rusty armour? If I , remember right, he always crept, or slunk, or crawled." " Yes, I remember," he replied gravely, " but I had spotless armour inlaid with gold, t ,nd I stood straight up to him and slew him Oefore your eyes. I think your favourite was crimson velvet trimmed with . ermine ? " ..,t "It was, Harry, it was. Very hot in . ti.,'.lllMer, and most unfashionable in colour. ' "(As' funny these dreams of one's childhood are, Harry; how unpractical and how seldom realised." • " . . .. That's true, Agnes," he replied, slowly, s __Yet there's one dream of my childhood that ‘:.. ;,'•-as very practical, and which could yet be ,alised. You. were, if I remember right, Ways a princess and generally in distress." " " Well, Harry ? " ss; . ~ , ell,a he said, in a low voice, " kou are ' t till a Princess to me." I " But not- in distress, Harry," she faltered, , incTolcihnrs awayey from him,' for she dazred not look 'NO, • . ns, not exactly ,in distress," he said, " but , ,syot too happy. A princess with a step- s 'o ther—a sort of 'Cinderella—neglected— ~ With no ugly sister," she broke in. ' so:, True," he replied. "but with a brother s, /t4.° wants the nonsense kicked out of him. . to 's a pity your father did not send Overcliffe a . public school." , , Poor Overcliffe. " - sies..,Well." 'the V.OlCe, young man continued in a s Thugut_ 'La • " : oI am not exactly a prince, ani.,, but—well, Agnes, you know what I loe - a , .but 2:,,,pohtician, 'I believe," she said archly, sas_?tilt she did not venture to look him in the tva--; Her woman's instinct told her what Thri a 9 ing next. , 41,ce",am n politics,,, the young man cried sseni'v• "T be your nartloss Armes. but you 413Pearto thi' nl- polities are everything. It to 's i ..., .be a family failing." . , ,eir7„,.gnes had no reply to make to this ssnr awnc A statement, and for a few moments ere silent. The girl was rapidly cast-</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>#</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
