      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>SPRING* ERUPTIONS.</head>
        <p>SPRING* ERUPTIONS.</p>
        <p>Bile Beans a Certain Cure.</p>
        <p>MAGAZINE CUTTINGS.</p>
        <p>SEA GIPSIES.</p>
        <p>A writer in the " Madras Mail " tells a delightful story of a new life "absolutely without parallel in Europe. The inventors of it are known as Sea Gipsies, and the scene of their happy wanderings is the archi- pelago Mergui. Instead of carts they owned covered boats, in which, with their families,dogs, cats, chickens, and pets, they float about on the sea and wander from island to island. By day they fish or harpoon turtle, or dive for oysters ; but every night they put back to the shore. If the weather is bad at sea,they land with their dogs, and then poach, catching porcupines, squirrels, arrnadilloes, hog-deer, and the like, of which they make savoury stews, like our gipsies. It sounds an ideal life. with mild sport, no work, no hours, no fixed abode, no rates and taxes. As a " rest cure "its adoption might be recommended by nerve specialists.—" Country Life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>FARMS IN WEST AUSTRALIA.</head>
        <p>FARMS IN WEST AUSTRALIA.</p>
        <p>Recognising the necessity on the part of settlers, particularly young men, of gaining Colonial experience, and of acquiring the technical knowledge needed to en- sure success in an agricultural career, the authorities in Western Australia have established two experimental farms, one of about 1,000 acres, and another of about 2,000 acres. Both are in the south-west division, but 1 they represent, to some extent, different classes of soil. Entrance to these farms is open to youths of sixteen years of age and over on most generous terms. For a fee of £2 2s. per annum, payable quarterly, students are maintained and taught for two years. Any young fel- lows going from the home country to these farms might get a thorough practical knowledge of farm work, and, at the end of the period, would be able to take land for themselves. Should anyone like to have further infor- mation, it can, no doubt, be obtained from the Agent- General for Western Australia, 15, Victoria street, London, S.W.—" The Garden." SOME QUEER CUP TIES.</p>
        <p>The final match for the blue riband of the Association football world has occasionally been played under much worse meteorological conditions than those which obtained recently. Indeed, matches have more than once been played in a thick mist, and the ridiculous position has arisen of a goal being shot quite unknown to anyone on the ground, including the man who had scored it! On one occasion, too, in 187 G, in a final tie between the Old Etonians and the Wanderers, what should have been the deciding goal of the match was disallowed, because the goal-posts, being very insecure, had been knocked over in the scrimmage which led up to the goal being shot. Snow has been another spoil sport, and so seriously did it handicap the players in the semi-final between West Bromwich Albion and Small Heath Alliance in 188 G, that the spectators, becoming weary of the slow progress of the game, started a snowball fight on their own account, and were soon swarming all over the ground.—" The King."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>SCIENCE OF DIAMOND CUTTING.</head>
        <p>THE SCIENCE OF DIAMOND CUTTING.</p>
        <p>Three patterns are followed in diamond-cutting. One the brilliant, invented perhaps barely two centuries ago takes the general form of two cones united by their bases. The upper one is so truncated as to present a rather large terminal plane (the table) surrounded nor- mally by thirty-two facets, the lower one is but slightly truncated and has only twenty-four facets. Much depends on the ratio between the axes of these two con es,and this is just right in the famous "Regent" of France. It is not so in the Rob-i-nor, which on that account should have been left with its original irregular and very ancient cutting. The second and older pattern is the Rose, in which the base is flat, and the facets, normally twenty-four in number, converge so as to form ultimately a blunt point—something like the top-half of a common form of a glass decanter stopper. The Orloff of Russia is the largest of this type certainly known to exist. The third , pattern is the Table, the chief ch,racteristic of which, 'so far as it can be defined, is a broad, flat surface above and below, with little more than a ring of facets , enclosing each, so that the stone is always a compara- tively thin one.—From "A Home of Diamonds," in the " Cornh ill Magazine" for May.</p>
        <p>THE GOLFING SOCIETIES. Apparently this is to be tho age for the formation of Golfing Societies (writes A. J. Robertson in 'The King "). Though the movement which has often been advocated to form a large and comprehensive Associa- tion for the purposes of golf is no nearer being realised than it was ten or a dozen years ago, golfers seem inclined unconsciously to be attacking the problem in detail. The liberal professions and important com- mercial interests in our varied industrial life are rapidly forming themselves into- golfing societies embracing the playing members connected with them. Thus we have societies representative of the Bar, the Universities, the Press, the Solicitors, the Musicians, the Actors, the Stock Exchange, and some other bodies. The latest society to be formed is the Cricketers. Golfing Society, which is to be opened only tolmembers of first , and second class county cricket teams and those players in the two principal Universities who have had the dis- tinction of having a" Blue" conferred upon them. The intention is to play inter-club and inter-county golf matches ; and considering the large number of very excellent cricketers who almost stand in the front rank as golfers, this commendable movement to organize their play on the links ought to meet with a great deal of hearty encouragement. Mr. Alfred Lytelton, the Colonial Secretary, himself one of the best cricketers in his University days, and to-day one of the best players in Parliament, will be president of the new society, while Mr. H. W. Bainbridge will.be captain, and Mr. G. L. Jessop, hon. secretary. A COMMUNARD'S ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>Whilst I was with her the husband came in and told his story. He had held, I heard afterwards, high com- mand in the French army, was a man of character and ability, with extraordinary linguistic acquirements ;he could speak some twenty European languages and dialects. He had thrown himself into the outbreak of the Commune, from what point of view Ido not now recall ; but, as a solder, had been given an important point to defend on a barricade. The fight was long and terrible, and when nearly all the defenders were killed or woupded, and the ammunition was exhausted, the few suivivors escaped as best they could. The 'General" managed to get on to some roofs and escape down a chimney into. a private apartment. There he found him- self in the bedroom of a lady who was dressing. Sauvez-moi, Madame ! " he cried. She, poor soul, terrified at this strange apparition, who with torn clothes, covered with soot, and with darker stains yet upon him stood suppliant before her, had but an instant to deoide,foi the tramp of soldiers rang upon the stairs.' and a thunoering knock at the outer door summoned her to open. She bade him get into the bed, and taking the skirt of her dress, a jacket, and some other feminine, garments, covered him with these. And then there followed a long parley between the soldier outside and the quick-witted Frenchwoman inside the door. " She was not dressed; what could they want ; what an abominable thing that in those evil days the very bedrooms of women were not to be res- pected." Of course, the soldiers had their way, and entered the room, the lady, scolding, fuming, protesting. The men looked in the cupboards and wardrobe, and under the bed; into the bed they did not look ; and after many apologies, with a military salute they depar- ted. The poor woman sank into a chair, and slowly the General raised himself. "Look, Madame." said he. and he took from a little table beside the bed his tobacco pouch, which unconsciously he had laid down. "If they had found this !" e cried.—From " French Refugees to England in 1871-72," in the " Cornhill Magazine" for May.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>ADMIRAL ROJESTVENSKY.</head>
        <p>ADMIRAL ROJESTVENSKY.</p>
        <p>I have had the following interesting little account of the Russian Admiral from "One Who Knows Him": Admiral Rojestvensky managed to spend some very happy days in this country, a few years ago, when he served as naval attache to the Russian Embassy. There are a couple of stories he is fond of relating concerning his experiences in the English capital. On his first arrival in England, he was invited to a hill in his official capacity. Knowing nothing of his prospective host and hostess, he was recommended to keep a sharp look-out for the Russian Ambassador, who was also to be present. When the evening arrived, Admiral (then Captain) Rojestvensky discovered that he had com- pletely forgotton both the name of the family and the number of the house in question; all that he could remember was the name of the square in which it stood. Nothing daunted, he told the cabby to drive up to any house in that locality that was brightly illumined. What was his coasternation, however, when the cabman suddenly shouted through the trap-door: "I say, Moosoo, there's two houses lit up; what's to be done?" Rojestvensky was for the moment nonplusse, ,then he decided to enter the nearest house and take le risks. A glance round, however, quickly convinced 1 •na that he had invaded the wrong ball-room, and he N{ _s about to beat a retreat when he was spoken to by a young lady, who imagined that she had met him before. The Admiral was convinced that she had not, but he thought it a shame to disillusion one so fair, so he accepted the character she imposed on him. So pleasantly did the time slip by in her company that it was not until many hours later that Admiral Rojestvensky reluctantly bade her farewell and crossed over to the other house. The first person he met on entering was his Ambassor, whose amusement on hearing the Admiral's story may be imagined. The key-note of Admiral Rojestvensky's life is work. He was once asked by the present writer." what his favourite pastime wax." " Work," replied the Admiral with his customary brevity. "Then what do you consider your hardest work ? " "Being idle," came the response.—" M.A.P."</p>
