      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>JOHN WALKER AND CO.</head>
        <p>JOHN WALKER AND CO.</p>
        <p>EKA3IINER PRINTING WORKS SICK, RrEriCVdPTl,3ouilliidd liSuUmßbSe rCe Till OrNer fßa Kt S order. ACCOUNT BOOKS, Sze TRADE PRINTING of every description. POSTERS A SPROIALITZ.</p>
        <p>EXAMINER OFFICE,</p>
        <p>jrrtarbilitrib</p>
        <p>BY most people in this THE TRANSVAAL country the new Trans- EXPERIMENT. veal Constitution is re- garded as purely experi- mental. Nobody supposes that it indicates any permanent arrangement for the administration of the Colony. A Legislative Assembly which is elective and an Executive which is nominated cannot represent the last word uf political wisdom. Such a Constitution may work very fairly for a time, and there is no lack of disposition anywhere to give it a trial. But the natural tendency of the elective Assembly will be to assert its prerogative over the non-elective Government. Either the Executive will agree with tho majority, in which event it ought to be responsible to that majority, or it will disagree, in which event the whole machinery will be in danger of getting dislocated. Mr. LierrzLrox might have taken a bolder step by establishing responsible government outright, instead of rely- ing on the precedent of Canada, for, instance, in the interest of caution. He pleads that direct party government in the Transvaal just now would represent a racial division. But the answer to that is put very well by a journal which supports Mr. LYTYELTON. The racial division, says the " Standard," can scarcely be pleaded eff6ictually in the face of the compact between the Boer leaders and a section of the British settlers. That com- pact has been criticised as a surrender to the Boors; but it may prove to be the beginning of a party system which is not based on racial lines. It is clear, at any rate, that, if the new party in the Transvaal should eventually exercise authority it cannot revert to all the old anomalies which are summarised under the name of Krugerism. To some provisions of the now Constitution the Boor politicians are likely to object,simply because they are based on English and not on Dutch tradition. Krugerisin naturally favoured a dis- tribution of seats which gave dominance to the electoral districts in whith the rural Boer popula- tion was ascendant. Thus Lydenburg, with a population of little over 3,000, had two members, and Johannesburg, with a population of 76,000, had one member. The new Constitution does not sanction this principle. It provides, moreover, for an automatic redistribution of seats every four years, so as to meet the condition set up by constant migration. This is manifestly just according to English notions, but it does not suit the Boers. They did not like the redistribution in Cape Colony, which gave more electoral power to the towns and less to the country districts. Their jealousy of the towns recalls a similar animus which used to inspire the old Tory party in England. Here the progressive movements of our times have sprung more trom the town than from the country; and it is very likely that the same rule will be observed in the future development of the Transvaal. We have seen our arty system grow out of that distinotion between town and country; but South Africa has its own conditions, and tho farming class there is I likely to maintain a strong and separate identity in politics. For this and other reasons the party system in the Transvaal must differ considerably from ours. The two white races cannot be expected to forget all at once the conflicts of the past ; but we may hope that the common interests of the Colony, especially the development of its material resources by a modern spirit unknown to Krugerisrn, will mitigate the purely racial animus as time goes on.</p>
        <p>The ordinary monthly meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday evening. So far, everything points to a brief and brotherly meet- ing. The agenda is uncommonly thin, and a really contentious subject would be difficult to find unless it be Councillor Wright's motion in favour of the Education (provision of meals) Bill, 1905. Having got one Bill through the Council at the last meeting, the Labour members are apparently greatly encouraged. But a resolution of this kind every month will probably end in "killing the goose, &amp;c." Our Town Councillors are not noted for their knowledge of Governmental and private legislative proposals, and in the past they have shown a strong disinclination to consider matters here which have to be dealt with at Westminster. The taxation of ground values for instance, though a topic upon which they might reasonably air their views as ratepayers' representatives bathe municipal parliament, is one which they have always shunned like the plague. It savours some- what of politics, and our Labour Councillors will find, we fear, ere long that the Council will class other matters under the same head rather than discuss them pro and con.</p>
        <p>Even the apathetic and supercilious people who decry British love a sport will, we venture to think, feel a thrill of pride in the fact that they are Warringtonians if the local football team fulfils expectations and wins the Northern Union Cup at Leeds to-day. It will be no small honour, fot the</p>
        <p>owners of the Cup are justly entitled to consider themselves the " Cocks et the North" so far as Rugby football is concerned+. Twice before have the Warrington men fought their way to the final and then fallen. No true townmnan will admit for a moment that they may do the same again to-day Skill and pluck are bound to eventually meet with a due reward, and it is the general belief in South Lancashire that the moment has arrived for War- rington to annex that shining trophy which has evaded them in the past. Then will they get a true Warrington welcome if they succeed ! The town is hungering for the opportunity of claiming the cup-holders as its own.</p>
        <p>Opinion in favour of "blacking out" betting news from the newspapers provided in Free Libraries is gaining force. The Liverpool City Council has set a worthy example in the matter and it seems likely to be followed by other authorities in th 3 district. No notice that at Thursday's meeting of the Wallasey Free Public Libraries Committee the subject was under discussion and, although no definite decision was arrived at, several members expressed themselves very strongly for the tuppression of betting intelligence. Apart from the ethics of the subject, there is no questioning the inconvenience ' created by men who stick most tenaciously to newspapers in order to study the latest "odds " and "selections.' Those who have a more legitimate claim upon the newspapers are often compelled to endure tedious waiting until the betting men have satisfied themselves. As we announced last week, the Free Church Council has requested the Warrington Town Council to act as Liverpool and St. Helens have already done, and even at the risk of displeasing some patrons of the Reading Room, the authorities should be willing to consult the convenience of the large body of genuine newspaper readers by blacking out the betting columns.</p>
        <p>That the now silent highways of the air are not likely in any reasonable time to be crowded with traffic, whatever achievements in aerial flight our inventors may put to their credit, is plain enough. The cost is prohibitive. Motoring has been. described as a sport for millionaires, but its expense is a mere matter of pocket-money as compared with that of ballooning. The silk envelope itself, as a writer in the "World's Work" points out, would be cheap at £3OO. But this is only the beginning of the ceitlay. If one's ambition contemplates no more than a captive balloon, the necessary machinery for ascents and descents will run into anything from £660 to £750. But this initial cost is as nothing compared with the sums which must continually be expended on !workmen to take charge of the engines, on storage and repairs—remembering always that an unfavourable descent may mean complbte and irretrievable ruin to the best- equipped balloon—and on gas. This latter item is certainly a serious one. If any person has a right to grumble at the length of his gas-bills it is the aeronaut. To fill an ordinary balloon means at least £35, which is rather a high price to pay for a trip in the air occupying a few flours only; while pure hydrogen costs eight times as much. Assuredly prices must fall before an airship omm- ' bus service is feasible at popular rates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Democracy: The word Democracy is'now pronounced every day, every hour, and in every place; and it is surprising how many meanings it is credited with. What we understand by Democracy is "a form of Government in which the supreme power is vested in the people collectively." Let us not forget that human individuals are not isolated. Mankind is not merely a series of individuals called men ; it is a race, which has a common life, and a general and progressive destiny. * * *. _ _</p>
        <p>Unlike some places, Warrington has had no "lively" vestry meetings this week. There has been almost perfect harmony all round, but perhaps the most peaceful was that in connection with St. Ann's Church, Here in addition'to the Vicar,andyestry clerk,only two gentlemen assembled to transact the business of the parish. The small attendance was attributed to a change in the time of meeting. Needless to say, the business was conducted with great expedition and the time occupied—about twenty minutes—was said to be , a record. * *</p>
        <p>The 'brevity of this meeting is, however, quite thrown into the shade by the remarkably short pro- ceedings at a vestry meeting at Shnttleworth, near Rarusbottom. The meeting was also extraordinary by reason of the fact that it was held in the pouring rain outside the vestry door, against which the Vicar, the Rev. E. Dyson, placed his -back and appointed as his warden, his niece, Miss Frances Stringer. Mr. T. Moore was re-elected people's warden and endeavoured to make some remarks about the rights of the people. The Vicar, however, from his commanding position on the door-step, applied the closure half-way through Mr. Moore's opening sentence. * *</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Pierpoint M,P. does not always bear in mind the axiom that it is more gracious to give than to re- ceive. In response to a request for a subscription in aid of the family of James Baxter, one of the heroes of the sewer fatality, the Member forwarded a modest ten shillings, accompanied by the somewhat tart comment that" everyone appears to think that the member for the borough has a fund upon which everyone may draw." We are afraid " everyone " will think the member is getting soured by the exigencies of Parlia- mentary life under a discredited Government. * * Pwllbeli revivalists are much exercise 4 in mind about football. They had almost stamd out the wicked pastime, but it " revived" itself and aroused feelings of horror by almost securing the Mayor to kick-off at a match in aid of the Nursing Association. The revivalists exhorted His Worship and finally so wrought upon him that he resisted the temptation of the oval Evil One. Probably the Nursing Association was the loser by the action of the revivalists. They obviously need a lesson in Pwllheli on" charity as a Christian virtue ". We offer the text to Mr. R. A. Naylor. *** . •</p>
        <p>The" Warrington Union Operatic Company" is the latest addition to our looal entertaining organizations. As the title suggests, it consists of members of the staff at'the Workhouse and Infirmary, and already it has been the means of unearthing quite a fine array of talent. The other evening a performance of the musical comedy. "An Adamless Eden" was given in the dining hall at the house before a large assembly and passed off with unqualified success. The inmates of the House had also been entertained by the com- pany and, needless to say, enjoyed the treat immensely Mr. Alfred G. Turpin,the moving spirit in the venture who acted as stage manager and played a leading part is to be complimented upon the happy results of his enterprise.</p>
        <p>* * * Break the news gently! The widening of Warring- ton Bridge, is, we are told, to be commenced in two months' time. A notice of Vice-Admiral G. S. Nares, Acting Conservator of the Mersey, with regard to thescheme appeared in last week's London Gazette." It is intended to widen the bridge from 28 feet to 501 feet by means of cantilevers, constructed of iron built in and secured to the existing buttresses of the bridge. The centre width of the existing bridge will be utilized for vehicular traffio. The work, it is stated, will not exclude, impede, or interrupt the tidal or other waters of the river Mersey. Warrington ratepayers will rejoice that there is at last a prospect of this very necessary improvement being undertaken. Alderman Smethurst will probably not need to call his public meeting after all!</p>
        <p>* * * The growth of the Free Churches is strikingly evidenced by the latest statistics. Last year the Free Churches provided 8,208,774 sittings as compared 3f7 the Free Churches or with 8,172,116 in 1903. The Anglican Church pro- vides, With regard to tahtei ofing forure 87 stand, 2,045,544—an increase of 4,710 on the previous year; and the estimated number of communicants in the Anglican Church is 2,123,551. The comparative inter- national statistics show that whilst Evangelical Free communicants,ac mn Churches number over 21,000,000 communicants— an increase of nearly 2,000,000 over the previous year, the Anglican Church for 1904 numbers 3,662,906. The Methodists alone have more than double the number ci communicants in the Anglican Cliurch,</p>
        <p>After chafllny the Mayor for his =gallantry to the fair ser, 'the crime we committed last week looks posi- tively heirme. In reporting the recent motor acci- dent in Brialm,street, we cold-bloodedly and in cold type remarked):: "Some ladies were travelling in the car, but unfortunatoly they escaped injury." There is something quite fiendish about the suggestion;and we therefore place full blame upon that wicked person the "printer's devil."</p>
        <p>* * * Play up Warrington! Be not "downhearted !" Fully 5,000 people are expected to follow the local football team to Leeds to-day, and certainly the ten special trains will not take many short of this number. It is interesting to calculate what an expendithre of money this football "final " will involve for War- rington people. Each excursionist will have to expend at least ss. to see the match and return home—in fact, the estimate is probably below the average. Five thousand five-shillings represent X. 1,250. Warring- ton will therefore be considerably poorer to-night, and the Railway Companies in particular, will be largely enriched. Tradesmen and rent-collectors will have a difficulty in obtaining cash next week, we imagine. But let each say " What care I" if the Cup comes home.</p>
        <p>* * The fact that it is bettel to be born lucky than rich must have been borne forcibly upon the parents of a. small Latchford child this weele. Along with other , youngsters, the child in question was playing on the embankment of the Ship Canal, near the Railway bridge at Latchford, when she fell and disappeared over the edge. Witnesses of the occurrence imagined that the little girl would be speedily drowned, and so she would have been had not a good, kind Providence thoughtfully provided a pontoon at this particular spot, on to which the girl fell. Her escape can only be ascribed to Good Fortune, and we should imagine that this particular little girl is not destined to die by drowning.</p>
        <p>* * All honour to Coroner Brighouse for his persistency in reiterating the necessity for the compulsory pro- vision of fire-guards as a means of protecting the lives of young children. When we realize that, on an average, 700 children are burnt to death in a year and many others agonised and disfigured by burns which get better, it makes us shudder at the callous indiffer- ences displayed by those who are responsible. Yes, are responsible,though we are afraid only theoretically. For what can we think of the maternal instinct which allows a woman to lock up young children in a house— clothed principally in an inflammable material,—while she gossips with a neighbour or spends the last copper in a" drink." The thought is so appalling that it almost fills one with despair. * * Will the compulsory provision of fire-guards act as a preventative? In some cases no doubt it will, where the mother is one of the better sOrt ; but if she is one of the indifferent, shiftless, disgrace to the nmnet women, who seem to be the more numerous among the poorer class,is it likely that she will take the trouble to put up with anything which inconveniences her or hampers her movements ? And when we consider that one fire has to do all the cooking(such as it is) boil the kettle heat all the water, dry the clothes, and warm the dace as well as the children,a " guard" must be Imore or less of an inconvenience. No ! To some women it will make no difference —they will "muddle and make-believe " to the end of their days,--but-- God help the little ones! We understand that the child burnt to death at Penketh last week, four years old, and left in the house alone, managed to lift the latch of the door,which he couldn't reach, with a brush, while his clothes were blazing round him. Oh ! the pity of it !</p>
        <p>_ * * * We shall have to go deeper and further back for the remedy. Girls will have to be taught and made to realize the responsibilities of motherhood, before it is too late, while they are young and impressionable_ It is of far more Importance to them than many things they have tof learn, and if, as they say, it is toc. easy to make a small family into a large one, (anct everyone knows that an abundance of anything detracts from its value) then small families say we for those who cannot afford large ones, well looked after, cared for and brought up—not draggedup. If we were to save and keep all the children that are born there would be no need to deplore a decreasing birth-rate.</p>
        <p>* * * Coroner Brighouse has said that representations have been made on the subject to the County Council, and statistics submitted in order that some legislation might be promoted, but Sir J. T, Hilbert, who was in the chair, summarily dismissed the recommendation, and characterised it as grandmotherly legislation, Well, where there are young children, nothing is more useful than a grandmother. She can sit by the fire and see that they don't get burnt to death if she can do nothing else, and if the State could supply every needy family with a grandmother she would probably be better than some mothers—she couldn't be worse— and we can quite see that she would be an improve- ment on a fire-guard. But "guards "before nothing by all means.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION.</head>
        <p>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION.</p>
        <p>Sale of Work In Aid of the Organ Fund.</p>
        <p>The members of the congregation of the Working- men's Mission, Bank Quay, are making an effort by means of a sale of work, to liquidate the debt incurred by the erection of a new organ in the mission. It will be remembered that in February the instrument was formally opened, the total cost being £205. Towards that sum LilOhas been raised entirely by the efforts of the congregation, many members having guaranteed amounts which they are paying by regular instalments. In order to enlist outside support, it was decided to hold a sale of work in the schoolroom and the pro- ject has been entered into with enthusiasm. The open- ing ceremony was performed on Friday afternoon' by Miss G.Crosfield and thesale will be continued to-dav -- (Satnrday).Thestalls,draped in heliotrope,white,and red, are laden with a variety of wares, andpresent a bright and inviting appearance. The following is a list of the stall- holders:—Congregational stall Mesdames J. Hassall, S. Hill,F.Atkinson,and A.Tilling,jun.;young ladies'class and choir stall,Mrs. Tanner Mrs.F.Gray,Misses Oakley,Dunn, ancllG.Hough; Sunday school andChristianEndeavour stall Mrs. Bowyer, Mrs. Jones, Misses E. Dobbins, A. Cooke, and Backhouse ; flower stall, Misses E. Dale,A.Thomas, F. Hassall, E. Hill, and M. Blunt ; refreshment stall, Mrs. T.Taylor,Misses Vane,C.Taylor, N.Hill, E.Tilling, and S. Blunt; advertisement stall, Messrs. G. Hough, J. Hampson, and S. Hassall. It is interesting to note that the Sunday school children have handsomely supported the fund. On ri. recent Sunday a collection was made, each child con- tributing a sum in proportion to its age—one halfpenny ' for each year. £6 2s. Gd. was realized and at Friday's opening ceremony the offering was formally handed over by one of the scholars. An excellent programme of entertainments has beers arranged, including selection bytheauvenile Harmonic: Society, under the conductorship of Mr. T. Brought and Vane's orchestra. An amusing "side show" has been. , provided by the men's class. To Mr. A. Tilling (hon. secretary) and Mr. F. aArtrkainnsgeomne(nhtosn.. treasurer) praise is due for the excellent '</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Inquiry at</p>
        <p>The George Hotel License. A public meeting of the Warrington 'Compensation Authority will be held in the police court on Thursday to consider the question of compensation to be paid for the lapsing of the George Hotel license. Cornet Soloist's Success. At Rhyl Eisteddfod on Mondakr, Mr. W. Rutter, of Warrington, obtained first prize in the- cornet solo competition,' for a rendering of the selection " Jennie Jones."</p>
        <p>Local Dog Fancier's Success. Mr. Jones, a member of the Warrington and Dis- trict Canine Society, was successful at the Preston Dog Show, securing second prize in the open class, with his St. Bernard dog, Major, being beaten by that well known dog champion, Tannhauser. A Handsome Easter Offering. There were large congregations at the Parish Church; Lymm,on Easter Sunday. The collections took the form of the usual Easter offering from the conk gregation to the Rector, the handsome sum of .£loq being contributed. "PREMIER" CYCLES at 7 guineas startling value.— J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.— Advt. St. Austin's Ward Vacancy. The vacancy in this ward has been formally declared and Monday next is the last day for receiving nomina- tions. Withdrawals must be handed in by two o'clock on Tuesday afternoon. The election will take place on Monday, May Bth. SD far the only candidate nominated is Mr. E. Horrocks (L) but Dr. Joseph has consented to contest the ward on behalf of the Conservatives. r ASK for CIINIIINOHA.M.S:HIGII-CLA-SS MINERAL ATHRS.—Advt. Concert at Latchford. ; There was a crowded attendance at the Parish ; room, Latchford on Wednesday evening,at a ; concert which was held with the object of raisin.. a ; testimonial to Miss Atkinson,who has for years worked hard on behalf Christ Church. An excellent pi-o- gramme was submitted in which song's were given by Mr. Aukland and Miss Mabel Henshall, violin solo by Miss M. Fairclough, cello solo by Mr. Jackson, two humorous readings by Mr. H. M. Ashton and humorous items by Mr. A. Grounds. The entertainment was concluded by a laughable farce " Petticoat Perfidy " given by the Mise Ad uns and Miss E. Roberts. Animated Pictures at Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>The reputation of the New Century Company has been sufficient to attract large audiences to the Parr Hall, where an excellent series of animated pictures has been exhibited during the week. Many new fea- tures have been introduced since the Company's first visit to Warrington, and advantage is taken of all the latest developments in animated photography. Incidents of the war in the Far East are depicted, and the audiences are able to witness a review of Russian troops by the Czar, prior to their departure for the seat of the war. The story of the Prodigal son is illustrated by an admirable series of coloured photo- graphs, and of the other films some of the most inter- esting picture the varied incidents of a trip from Liverpool to Vancouver. • There is no lack of humour in the entertainment, and the adventures Of Mrs. Brown in the motor car create great mei-riment. The programme is further varied by musical items, and altogether the entertainment is a most enjoyable one and well repays a visit. There will be a matinee to- day (Saturday) and the concluding exhibition will be given this evening. - -</p>
        <p>Another Success by the Apollo Choir. Our readers will be pleased to hear of another success by the Warrington Apollo Male Voice Choir. Competing at Rhyl Eisteddfod on Monday, they carried off the first prize of £23 in the chief choral competition, the test piece being "Crossing the plains" (Price). Mr. Wilfred Jones R A.M.; and Mr. C. Moreton Bailey, Mos. Bac., F. 11.0.0. were the adjudicators. The other choirs taking part in the contest were Wigan Harmonic, Crewe Apollo :hid Tregynon and District Male ;Voice Choir. In their decision the adjudicators said they had no hesitation in awarding the first prize to Warrington Apollo. They spoke in very eulogistic terms of the singing of the choir and specially commented upon the brilliance of trine produced by the tenors. Crewe Apollo were 'placed second and Wigan Harmonic third. Mr. Harryl Berrey conducted the winning choir. It is gratifying to note that this latest success makes a total of over £lOO in prize money gained by t4lts Apollo choir at various eisteddfodau. Earlier in the day the choir took part in the chief choral competition at Chester Crown Eisteddfod. The test piece was Jenkins's "The Son of God," and the prizes, £2O and a silver crown for the conductOr, and gl to the conductor of each unsuccessful choir. Five choirs competed, viz. : Wigan Harmonic (conductor, Mr. R. A. Moss) ; Man- chester Orpheus Glee Society (Mr. W. S. Nesbitt); Tregynon and District Male Voice Choir (Mr. B. H. IPhillips); Birmingham Male Voice Choir (Mr. W. ' Sewell) ; and Warrington Apollo Male Voice Choir , (Mr. H. Berrey.) Dr. Rogers,the adjudicator, awarded the marks as follows :—Wigan Harmonic, 55 marks ; Manchester Orphens, 58 ; Tregynon, 57 • Birmingham. 55 ; Warrington, 53. At this Eisteddfod only one choir turned up for the juvenile choir competition, this being the Warrington Harmonic Juvenile Choir, and they were awarded half the prize (13 35.) The Vagrant Problem. To-day a meeting is being held in Manchester in , connection with the No. 11 District Association of Workhouse Masters and Matrons (of which Mr. J. F. Awbery,`master of the Warrington Workhouse, is bon. secretary), to discuss two important phases of the vagrant problem. The first phase, states the notice convening the meeting, is " the desirability of urging upon all Boards in Mr. Jenner Fust's district to com- bine for the purposes of pursuing a uniform treatment of the casual, especially in matters relating to diet and tasks, and thus avoid the inequality of vagrant admis- sions now prevailing through the absence of the principle of uniformity. Secondly, to respectfully sub- mit this expediency of Boards of Guardians being urged to arouse the State to intervene with remedies of a National character for arrest- ing the growing traffic in child vagrancy. Many ,of our Boards have recently expressed their uneasi- ness at the great increases of vagrancy reported to them, and we have ventured to suggest that if only co-operative deliberations between our Boards and ourselves could be arranged, two very important feature i vor this question could be elucidated n this district, act as an incentive to other areas to follow , our lead, and thereby be the means of creating a universal desire on the part of all public authorities to combine for a comprehensive and national measure of vagrant reform. We recognize that many conferences upon this subject have been convened, and no results have trancpired, but this is the first recorded instance of a 'body of Workhouse Masters taking the initiatory steps and appealing for their respective Boards to join lhands With them in an earnest endeavour to do some- thing, if only in the two respects outlined."</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>index to Inside Pages. . PAGE. \ 2. Women's Chat and Fashions. " Where Thou Did'st Never Come," by Helen . Mathers. "The Vise Men of Gotham" by S. Baring- Gould, 3. "The Black Motor-Car." Football. Local Churches. 6. Wesleyan Foreign Missionary Anniversary. Police News. . Cricket. The Free Churches. Electoral Anomalies. . "Phyllis the Elusive." 7. Friendly Society Securities: Warrington. The Homes and Haunts of Sir Walter . Scott," by Sir Herbert Maxwell Bart., M.P. Association Football. Billiards. r' Am for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASb MINERAL WAritus.—Advt. f• Coming Events. / . A secular lecture will be delivered next Tuesday evening in the Druid's Hall, Sankey-street, by Mr. • Percy Ward, Lecturer of the Liverpool Branch of the ; above Society. The main object of the meeting is to i form a branch of the Secular Society in Warrington. The Sunday school anniversary in connection with i ;Wycliffe Church will be cetebrated to-morrow, when the preacher at the special services which will be held will be the Rev. F. W. Adeney, principal of 1 t Lancashire Independent College. A meeting of the Warrington Anti-Vivisection gociety will be held in the Cairo street schools on ' 0 Taesday afternoon when Miss Lind of Hageby will f? : give an address. , Accident at Naylor's Works. Whilst following his employment at Messrs. R. A. i r Xaylor's work, on Tuesday afternoon, William Banner (38), 7, Villars-street, fell from a ladder and broke his .; leg. He Ids conveyed to the Infirmary where his - ... injuries were dressed. '.. PUBLIC BE ER NEFIT.—UND THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. ' Free Breakfasts Discontinued. The free breakfasts at the Ragged *School have been discontinued, no further response having been made to the appeal for help. On Good Friday, Saturday, and Easter Monday, parties of 40 poor girls were taken for a day's outing to Southport. It is intended to continue these health-giving excursions at intervals during the summer months. , Death of a Prominent Congregationalist. The Rev. J. A. Mitchell; secretary of the Congrega- tional Union of England and Wales, died at his residence at Highgate, London, on Wednesday night. Mr. Mitchell, who was 54 years of age, was operated ninon on Monday for appendicitis. The deceased from ..-1897 to 1903 was principal of the Congregational Institute at Nottingham, and prior to that was minister at the Congregational Church at St, Helens for four . Years. ' Warrington Artist's Success. • . Mr. Oswald CI arside, for the fourth year in succes- sion, has had his work accepted and hung at the Royal Academy. This year he exhibits a very largo water colour painting, beech trees at Chauctonbury, sussex. The space allotted at the Academy to water colours ng restricted, comparatively few. and those , naturally only the very b6st of the pictures sent in, can be hung, which speaks well for the quality of this .. artist's work. He is also well represented at nearly : all the leading provincial, and foreign exhibitions. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER ,•;LOCK. BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. : tr. Lloyd George's Holiday. Mr. Lloyd George is spending the Easter holiday in .' reland, accompanied on different stages of a motor- : I,ng tour by leaders of various sectionsbf the Irish -Nationalists, including Messrs. Healy, J. Redmond, William O'Brien. Having regard to Mr. T. P. - O'Connor's forecast of Mr. Lloyd George's position in !Ile Liberal• Cabinet, and Mr. George's recent negotia- tions with the Roman Catholic authorities respecting d.lei administration of .. the Education Act, political . I;4•nificance is in certain quarters attached to the , Present visit. PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST .... .23430 TS IN LANcesHlßE.—Advt. The Cemetery. Mr. It. Young, the Registrar, has presented his . Ttiliial report of the work at the .Warrington Cemetery .er the year ending March 31st, in which he states that the receipts amount to £1,409 2s. 3d.; ' corres- r)ialing period last year, £1,461 Os. sd. There have een interred during the past year 1,376 adults and calinors, and 115 stillborn children and premature; 17tesponding last year, 1,501 adults and minors, and i-P.. stillborn and premature, showing a decrease of e'•-' burials and a decrease of 5 stillborns, and a lerease in receipts of .S5l 18s. 2d. There have been interments during the past year from the County Ilium, Winwick. R. M. ASHTON'S Lays Town is the KING or , IN .."CS.-63. SA.NKEY-STRE.ET, Warrington.—Advt. urowning Mystery. , atl.ll-• Fisher, deputy district coroner, held an inquest . tini'lle Stag Inn, Walton on Saturday on the body of , l'auanknown man which was recovered from the , . •••• :' 1 th chester Ship Canal on Thursday. The evidencel ivecl that the body was found by a man nameill half on the bank of the canal and half in the I tonter. It was recovered by the police and conveyed 1 the stag ' Inn The police were of opinion that the i kan h ad only been in the water a day or two. A . , ~ .., ,141:11.tictturnefing" F:enyd, frdeesand "e 0 Imvpaisexierleltuarmniedd.—saTridhye .3a reription of the body is: ' height 5 ft. .9 inches, dark , , 44 44 stacbe, The clothing consisted of a black suit ' grey overcoat,, , Tll. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING or , it,, o NICS.--43, SANKEY ST., WARRINGTON.--Advt. 4.1110 In Crossley-street. f"rtAs, fire broke out in a stable owned bv Mr. Ralph ' NttisultlY; retail coal dealer, Back Crossley-etreet, off. ~,Zal-brow, on Monday afternoon. Information was A- ii eyed to the fire station and the Brigade, t„,"ria the command of Superintendent Turner, at once I ‹i:d out with the steam engine "Contain." On the arrival they, found the stables well alight. 'With i ik nittsgv, eates. t difficulty a horse inside the stable was got bee_nut it had been badly burned, all its hair having] lite,:`,, ainged off. The sheds of the chemical works 14,,,,u, also in flames, but in an hour's time the brigade cotate`cacceeded in extinguishing the 'outbreak. The Citkocil t:1 of the stable were totally destroyed. Mr. 126,74 8 premises were not insured. Co. It THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. "Mentions Objector's Unsuccessful Appli-. At th cation. Borough Police Court on , Thursday, Mr. °er I'd ' Twiss, jun. i Thelwall Lane, applied for an e • itt xempting his child from vaccination.—The lieki rates who heard the application were Mr. John eallt .culgh (in the chair),and Mi. W. Roberts.—Appli- ipit ,:t. in an appearance at the Court on Wednesday Jeatil, ,there was only one magistrate on the 'to‘v ,tat'lls objection could not be. heard. He ion to ect that he had a strong conscientious objec- la the ' vaccination and believed it would be injurious 1- ex,.. . tealth of the child. The chairman: What rka ~ ri__ :40 yoti have you that leads you to believe it would ais b child harm ?—Applicant instanced the case of after t°ther's child who, be said, became ill aicl thav,accination, and died.—The chairman ePlirh aps the child would have died in any i 4 utare . had a strong opinion that vaccination did :olated 2,?ed. than harm, although there might lie 'at slit: ;,stie es in ,which it did harm. The Bench were . th, : d • with the applicant's conscientious objec- ' -111,11.1}ta the application would he refused. 111113111.41317,11", CYCLES at 9 guineas for Best Grade, 1, ,arriagt,;:rd- S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., n Advt. ,</p>
