      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL</p>
        <p>EASTER DAY SERVICES.—There were good congre- gations at the services on Easter Sunday. The church was prettily decorated with flowers, and the singing bright and cheerful. The anthem "Arise thou that sleepest " was rendered in an effective manner by the The Rector (the Rev. R. Greenall) preached in the morning, whiß) the Rev. E. E. Farrar occupied the pulpit in the evening. THE VESTRY MEETING.—This was held on Tuesday, when Messrs. A. Dakin, L. Leyland (wardens), 11: S. Layland, and Joseph Warburton . (sidemen) were re-elected. At the close of the meeting the annual dis- tribution of cloth took place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Extension of St. George's Mission.</head>
        <p>Extension of St. George's Mission.</p>
        <p>Efforts are being made to provide for the physical as well as the spiritual welfare of the residents in the neighbourhood of the St. George's Mission. On Tuesday a building which has been added to the Mission premises and which will be used for the purpose of a gymnasium and recreation room, was opened by the Lord Bishop of Liverpool, in the presence of a large gathering.— The Rector presided and remarked that St. George's Mission served a district comprising about 4,000 people and now had a good congregation, with a large number of communicants ; a Sunday, school of about GOO ; a large mothers' meeting ; a men's club and other institu- tions connected with it. The work there had prospered and been greatly blessed. It was the great desire of ,fr. Thurston and afterwards of Mr. White to acquirethe pro- perty which had now boon converted into that extension. It was purchased at a cost of .2500,filthough it had been recently valued .tt £OOO, the money to be paid in yearly instalments of £lOO. He thought they could quite see their way to pay that. In addition the many necessary alterations had cost another £5OO or .£6OO. and towards that sum they had about £250 or £5OO. One object in 'their having obtained the property was so that they could look after the bodies as well as the souls of their people. The room they were now in—a spacious one —would be used as a gymnasium and for all kinds of recreation while the room above would be an addition to the Sunday school. (Applause.)—The St. Clement's Church Lads Brigade attended as a guard of honour, under the direction of Captain Gandy, Lieut. Lapage, and the Rev. T. W. Barber, assistant chaplain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Examinet, Saturday, April 29, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examinet, Saturday, April 29, 1905.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. Tea and Public Meeting.</p>
        <p>Inaugurated only a few months ago the Stockton Heath Women's Liberal Association has already a good membership, and promises to become no inconsiderable power in the political life of the village. Oa Thursday evening a tea and public meeting were held under the auspices of the Association, the success of which will encourage the Liberal ladies of the village in their politi- cal endeavours, and will no doubt act as a fillip to the organization. 'lea was provided in the Providence schools to which about 30 sat down and at the meeting which followed there was a good attendance. Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes presided and was supported by Miss Wubbe, Liverpool, Miss L. Gratton, Miss Bessie Dutton, Mesdames H. Roberts, E. Lloyd Jones, J. S. Hughes and E. England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>ADDRESS-BY AIISS WITEBE._</head>
        <p>ADDRESS-BY AIISS WITEBE._</p>
        <p>Miss Wubbe delivered a highly interesting and educa- tional address upon current politics, dealing with the manner in which the present Government had closured debates and rendered free discussion in the House of Commons almost impossible. ShB specially emphasised extravagance of the Conservative administration pointing out how the national expendi. tare had increased out of all reason. She was particu- larly strong in her denunciation of the Governments. Alteration of the rules of the House of Cornmons,which permitted of the unquestioned spending of large sums of money, and which prevented the House of Commons exercising the power which rightly belonged to them in regard to financial matter. The Conservative party were attempting to interfere with our free trade. Mr. Chamberlain had succeeded in raising the price of sugar from £6 to £l6 per ton. It was impossible at present to alter the condition in the sugar trade but when the convention had expired it would be the duty of the Liberal Government to go into the matter. Another question was that of Chinese , slavery and that would have to be re-considered by a Liberal Government when the indentures which had been entered into had expired. (Applause.) She was ' not going to discuss that question but she thought it was a great going back on the part of England, after having set an example to the world tO allow the introduction of forced labour in South . Africa. (Applause.) It was the duty of the Liberal party to sink all petty differences and unite and form one great party throughout the land and return a Liberal Government in the place of the present adminis- tration who were dead to a sense both private and public honour. (Applause.) Mrs. England, the hon. secretary, presented a report of the work of the Association which was of a satisfac- tory nature. In her remarks she dealt with the question of womens suffrage and thought the time had arrived when women should have the vote—(Applause)---in face of the fact that women paid taxes both directly and indirectly as well as the men.</p>
        <p>....... Miss Bessie Dutton said she could not properly express her indignation against those who had attempted to tamper with our free trade system, and who had robbed us of a great del/ of our liberty by the Educa- tion Act. The excuses for protection were flimsy in the extreme and they were not the simple simons that they could net see through them. She was sure everyone must be convinced that if we had protection a great deal of money would come out of the pocket of the poor man and go into the pockets of the rich. (Applause.) Liberalism did not legislate for the rich but also for the poor, and those who were placed at disadvantage. In temperance • matters we could not expect anything from the present Government, and though we did not get all we wanted from a Liberal Government, we certainly got more than from a Con- servative administration. (Applause). Something must be done in regard to the education question. We did not want to have money which we subscribed, spent by people over whom we had no control, and it is when, as under the present Education Act, representation did not follow with the raisins of funds, that we realized the hardship and injustice of the Act. Miss Dutton concluded an able address by a strong appeal to the ladies to do all they could in the coming election to help forward the Liberal cause. _ _</p>
        <p>—.-„. On the motion of Mrs. Henry Roberts, seconded by Mrs. Jones and supported by Miss Gratton the speakers were heartily thanked for their addresses. In answer to Miss Gratton, Mr. E. Lloyd-Jones stated that Mr. King the prospective Liberal candidate for the Knutsford division was in favour of women's suffrage. Miss Vcrubbe briefly returned thanks, and upon her proposition Mrs. Holmes was thanked for her services in the chair, and the meeting concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. A. H. DUNN.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. A. H. DUNN.</p>
        <p>It is with regret that We announce the death of Mr. Alfred Henry Dunn, Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath, who passed away on Wednesday morning after a brief illness. The deceased gentleman, who was not of robust constitution, had been in indifferent health for some time, but his death came as a painful shock. He was seized with a stroke on Wednesday of last week, but appeared to progress favourably until Tuesday morning when he hid a serious relapse and, despite medial attention, died ab3ut nine o'clock the following morning. The late Mr. Dunn, .who was fifty-three years of age, was a native of Andover, Hampshire, but came to Warrington at an early age. His father, the late Mr. Frederick Dunn, carried on business for a considerable period in Bridge-street as a boot and shoe dealer. At the age of sixteen Mr. A. H. Dunn was alipi enticed to the late Mr. Edwin Richardson, grocer, Bridge-street, and by his devotion to duty earned the confidence of his employer. Twenty-five years ago he was appointed to the position of cashier and traveller and acted in that capacity up to the time of his death. He was well-known by the members of the trade in the district,and was also a familiar figure on the! Manchester and Liverpool wholesale markets: Of determined character and high- principle, yet un- assuming in disposition, he commanded the esteem of all with whom he came into contact. He was promin- ently identified with the Wesleyan Methodist denomination and WAS a regular worshipper at Bold- street Church. He had acted as circuit chapel secretary for many years and also took a great interest in the work of the Free Church Council. He was a staunch Liberal and rendered viluable service to the : party organization in Stockton Heath. As an energetic temperance worker he did much to further the pro- gress of the Band of Hipe movement in the village. !He was a particnlarly good organiser and despite the 'heavy calls which business made upon his time and abilities,he was ever ready to assist in promoting worthy causes, particularly in connection with the church to which he was so much attached. He was always to the fore in the arrangement of social functions. It was probably his strenuous life which over-taxed his strength and brought about his death so un- expectedly. His loss will be deplored by a wide circle and deep sympathy is extended to the widow and family of three in their bereavement.</p>
        <p>THE FUNERAL. The funeral took place yesterday (Friday) afternoon at Hill Cliffe, the large attendance at the graveside testifying to the esteem in which the deceased gentle- man was hell The officiating minister was the Rev. G. H. Carnson. The Wesleyan body was largely represented.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES. Warrington Juvenile District No, 4.</head>
        <p>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF RECHABITES. Warrington Juvenile District No, 4.</p>
        <p>The quarterly District Council was held in the new schoolroom of the Independent Methodist Church, Gars- wood, on Saturday, April 22nd. Bro. H. Blacker, D.s.J.T., presided, and the whole of the district officers and 13 representatives, and also a number of visitors from the adult tents were present. The D.S., Bro. T. Massey. reported that one new branch had been opened at the Wycliffe Congregational Church, Warrington, with 10 new members, and up to March 31st returned a membership of 33. The past quarter had been the most successful in the experience of the District, nearly every branch showing an increase of membership ; the Hope of Sankey Tent again leading the way by an increase of 33 members during the term. Three juvenile members had been successful in obtaining two Legion of Honour Certificates and one Collar, presented by the Board of Directors for introducing new members in their tents ' for the year ending 1901. The report shows a total of 16 juvenile tents, with a membership of 999, this being an increase of 123 members in the quarter, besides trans- ferring 11 juvenile members to the adult tents, and 11 left by clearance and 2 deaths. The income for the quarter amounted to £3O 2s. 931, and the expenditure £25 18s., which includ3d the funeral claims amounting to £l3, leaving a net gain on the quarter of £1 4a. 91d. The total funds amounting to .6360 7s. 63d. In the evening a public; meeting was held in the Schoolroom, and was well attended. Bro. H. Blacker, D.S.J.T., occupied the chair, supported by Bro. W. A., Platt, 11.13.13., Bro. T. Massey, D.S., Bro. W.Baxeridale, D.T., and other officers. A capital programme, consist- ing of songs. recitations, selections on the concertina,. and a dialogue entitled "'Uncle Benjamin's Legacies" was given during the evening, Bro. J. Hill being the accompanist. Bro. W. A. Platt, 111.8. D., gave a splendid address on the principles and position morally and financially of the ltechabite Order, and afterwards on 1 behalf of the Juvenile District made a presentation to 'throe retiring District officers, viz.: a cruet stand to Bro. T. Hughes and a silver watch to Bro, W Hulme, retiring Trustees; and a marble time piece and bronzes to Bro. W. Wright, P.D.S.J.T.—Brethren Hulme and Wright suitably responded, Bro. Hughes being absent through sickness.—The prizes gave every sttisfaction' and were supplied by Bro. S. Fernley,Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS:</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS:</p>
        <p>Wright—Birtles. ! There was a pretty wedding at Christ Church. Padgate on Wednesday, the contracting parties being Mr. Arthur Stanley Wright, architect, Warrington, son of the late Alderman J. E. Wright, J.P., and Miss Florence Birtles, daughter of Mr. Thomas Birtles, Brantwood, Fearnhead, and Legh House, Warrington. In consequence of the recent death of Mrs. Birtles, the wedding was a very quiet one, the party consisting of the relatives of the two families. The church was filled with friends of the bride and bridegroom. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. Howard Pitt, of Liverpool, assisted by the Rev.,W.Pollock Hill,vicar of Padgate.Mr. Blackwell presided at the organ and played appropriate selections. Later in the day the newly-married couple left for Scotland where the honeymoon is being spent. They were the recipients of very numerous presents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Robinson—Richardson.</head>
        <p>Robinson—Richardson.</p>
        <p>Considerable interest was manifested in the marriage at St. Thomas's Church, Stockton Heath, on Thursday morning, of Mr. John Cormack Robinson, younger son of Mr. Thomas Robinson, Nab Hill, Leek, and Miss Gertrude Richardson, eldest daughter of Mr. W. Richardson,J.P., Holly Bank, Walton. Both the bride and bridegro )m are very well known in the district,and a large congregation assembled to witness the ceremony The altar and chancel of the church had been very beautifully decorated for the occasion by Mrs. Henry arsenal,. The officiation-' clergy were the Rev. H. V. Pigot, vicar of StocktonEleath,and the Rev.W.Carleton Nicholls, of Mere Brook (brother-in-law of the bride- groom). Mr. F. Rose presided at the organ and played a choice selection of wedding music. The bride who was given away by her father, was charmingly attired , in a dress of cream cloth with silk and gold trimmings, I with a hat of cream tulle. She carried a lovely shower bouquet of lilies of the valley and wore a pearl pendant ' and chain, the gifts of the bridegroom. She was attended by three bridesmaids, Miss Dora Richardson and Miss Maud Richardson (sisters of the bride), and Miss Charlotte Robinson (sister of the bridegroom). Their dresses were of heliotrope chiffon taffeta, trimmed with .old lace, and they wore hats to match. The brides- maids carried beautiful shower bouquets of palest pink roses, and their ornaments were gold signet rings, the gifts of the bridegroom. Mr. Harry H. Evans, of Warrington, discharged the duties of best man. After the wedding, breakfast was served at Holly Bank, and during the afternoon a reception was held. The newly- married couple departed for London and the South of England where the honeymon is being spent. The bride's travelling cossume was of meth cloth, with travelling coat of the same, and with hat to match. Numerous and handsome presents were received. Steele— -Mortimer.</p>
        <p>At Frodsham Parish Church on Wednesday, the marriage was solemnized of Mr. Matthew Steele, son of the late Dr. Steele' of Frodsham and Runcorn (at one time a partner in the firm of Messrs. Wigg Brothers and Steele,chernical works, Runcorn), and Miss Mary Muriel Mortimer, daughter of the late Mr. William Mortimer, of Beaconhurst, Frodsham, head of the well-known Orford Tannery, Warrington. Great interest was taken in the event in the village and surrounding district. A large congregation assembled, there being upwards of 150 guests. Dr. Bridge of Chester, presided at the organ, the service, which was choral, being under the direction of Mr. C. H. Hibbert, L.L.C.M., organist. The officiating clergy were the Rev. Cannon Willis and the Rev. H. B. Blogg. The bride who was given away by her brother, Mr. H. P. Mortimer, of Crofton Lodge, Kingsley, was attired in,a handsome costume of white crepe de Ohine,inserted with tambour lace arranged with white satin ribbon flowers. The Court train of tambour lace, lined with white chiffon, was suspended from both shoulders and trimmed with satin ribbons and sprays of orange blossoms, with tulle veil over a wreath of orange blossoms. The bride carried a lovely bouquet of white tea roses,and her only cirnament was a diamond wood- cock brooch, the gift of the bridegroom. Two charm- ing little maids Misses Doris Steele and Maud Pilkington,dressed in pretty white muslin frocks, with white chip hats trimmed with white chiffon and pink roses acted as train-bearers. The four bridesmaids, Misses Norah Mortimer, Kathleen Brassey, Lilias Douglas and Maddocks were daintily costumed in lovely gowns of azure silk taffetas, trimmed and arranged with white Valenciennes lace with white crinoline hats trimmed with white lilacs and wore pearl/and gold safety pin brooches, gifts of the bride- groom. A reception was held at Iles,conhurst by Mrs. Mortimer in large marquees on the lawn, there being close on 200 guests, and the happy pair subsequently left for the Golden Grove, Llanasa, Flint, lent for the honeymoon by the bride's brother, Mr. Frank Mortimer The bride's travelling dress was a tailor-rade costume of brown cloth, coat and skirt, with hat to match. The marquees, grounds, and church had lavish floral decorations, the motto " Health and happiness to bride and bridegroom" being displayied. The village was en fete and during the day the church bells rang out merry peals. The newly married couple were the recipients of many beautiful presents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL OPERATIC PERFORMANCES.</head>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL OPERATIC PERFORMANCES.</p>
        <p>Praductiou by the Memben oi St. Pauls' Church Choir.</p>
        <p>jSo successful were the productions on Tuesday and Wednesday night by the members of St. Pauls' Church Choir, of the burlesque opera "All Ba-ba and the black sheep" that the suggestion is unhesitatingly made that they should form a permanent operatic society. The primary intention of the prompters of the venture was to assist the choir fund connected with St. Paul's ' Church and while in this direction they succeeded in their object they also proved their worth as entertainers, their efforts, considering their inexperi- ence. being remarkably successful. The opera, the tar:.tto of which was writt( n by Mr. Fred Edmonds,and the music by Mr. C. T. West, is an adaptation of the story "The forty thieves," and ma graces many catchy airs and pretty numbers, and affords opportunities for displays of humour of which the the artistes were not slow.to take advantage. A serious handicap arose from the smallness of the stage but otherwise the production was excellent, the performers, bath collectively and individually, acquitting themselves admirably. The lady members of the chorus were particularly sprightly I and their singing harmonized well. The "robbers,'" however, were a little stiff in their actions though their singing left little to be desired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>DRAMATIS PERSO).LE.</head>
        <p>DRAMATIS PERSO).LE.</p>
        <p>AU Ba-ba, Mr.C.GeldrechlMongianNiliss N.Armstrong; Aldullah, Mr. A. Cotterill ; Hassarao, Mr. E. Walker Illrabim, Mr. W. Flood ; Mustapha, Mr•W. Davies; Mrs' Cassim, .vliss E. Miller; Fatima, Miss Wilcox ; Zuteika, Miss J. Lawless; Polly, Miss N. Gardner ; Donkey, Mr. J. Gibson.</p>
        <p>Thieves: Messrs. Bibby, A. Cox, E. Downing, 3. Gibson, J. Morris, W. Pennington, G. Ratcliffe, W. Richardson, W. Wilkes and 1. Wilcox. College students : Airs. E. Wilkes, Misses. R.Bebbing- ton, S. Broadhurst, C. Downing, C. Jennings, G. Bayne, E. Wilcox, G. Wilkinson and P. Williams. Taking the players in order of merit Miss E. Miller perhaps leads the list. Her interpretation'of the part was bright and charming.. Her singing also was excel- lent specially in the solo "In old Bagdad." The other litsly principals had comparatively little to do but each entered heartily into the spirit of the piece. Mr. Cotterill was well placed as the captain of the thieves, his robust voice admirably suiting thS.character. Mr. C. Geldred sang and acted well as did also his slave (Miss Armstrong) whose playing was characterized with a pleasing freedom. The song " Poverty " by Ali Ba-ba, could hardry have been improved upon. Mention must be made of Mr. J. Gibson as the Donkey, for, by the eccentricities of which he made that animal guilty, he showed an excellent appreciation of the humorous possibilities of the part. The opening selec- tion by the lady members of the chorus " Merry little ladies " and the, Parasol drill" by the same performers I were prominent features of the production, while the humorous chorus by the thieves "Twinkle Twinkle " was also a most successful item. Taken altogether, the opera, as above stated, was produced with pronounced success, and a suggestion which was made that an addi- tional performance in aid of the Infirmary, should certainly be acted upon. Praise must be given to Mr. R. R. Lonsdale, who made a most efficient stage manager, and Mr.E. Wilkes for his services as Conductor, and also in training the performers, while thanks are duo to Mr. J. Potter for the loan of the scenery.</p>
        <p>APPLETON V. GRAPPENIIALL. At Appleton on Good Friday. The home team won the toss, Gramm-Mall commencing operations against a strong wind. The visitors had much the best of matters, although beaten in the pack, tries being scored by Shakeshaft (2) and Fairhurst (1), while S'mkeshaft and Davenport each added goals. Result : Grappenhall,l3 points, Appleton, nil.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S GOLDEN BUTTER</p>
        <p>BISCUITS,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>THE N.U. CUP FINAL. Warrington's Prospects.</p>
        <p>(By " Ovalist.",</p>
        <p>Warrington will win the Cup to-day! This is my firm opinion. They have an infinitely better record in the League than Hull Kingston Rovers, they have had more cup-tie experience, and this is their third appearance in the final tie. Dame Fortune has stood by them right through the tournament, and I cannot imagine that she will desert them at the eleventh hour. Previously, Warrington have lost,bothin Yorkshire and Lancashire, so that the choice of grounds cannot be worth much. In fact Warrington should be more at home at Headingley than the Rovers, and their nerves ought to be, by this, Steeled to the criticisms of a 30,000 crowd. '</p>
        <p>Having' expressed ray confidence in the Cup coming to ;Warrington, I must add that I am sure it will wit be won without a hard struggle. There are no novices , among the Rovers, and as a team they have always played a prominent part in Yorkshire football. In their meetings with Warrington they have probably a sub- stantial balance of points, but they have rarely met the " Wirepullers " at full strength, and have certainly never seen Warrington's best form. lam hoping that , to-day they will go home with a chastened feeling as the result of the prowess of Hallam's brigade.</p>
        <p>Warrington's team to-day is quite the strongest which has represented the Club iit the N.U. final. On the two previbus occasions, there has been a want of • solidity in the pack which has been a considerable handicap on the team. In fact, last April, it was Warrington's inability to hold the powerful Halifax forwards which proved their undoing. The introduction of Preston, Shugars, and Belton this season has strengthened the side very much, and rarely have the Warrington eight had to play second fiddle. They will meat a redoubtable lot in the lovers' pack, but they ought to succeed in affording the backs eriough opportunities to win by. There are more unlikely things than a purely forward try by Warrington. Preston, Thomas, Boardman, Swift and Cook have been-showing fine form in the loose recently and are generally up with the ball.</p>
        <p>The Rovers are somewhat similar to Warrington in that they are an aggressive combination. At h ill- hack they have two excellent players in Barry and Gordon, while the three-quarters are a good lot. In West, they have one of the cleverest and speediest wings in Yorkshire, while Robinson, at centre, is an unknown quantity to Warrington men. That, he possesses both pace ancrcleverness recent perLrmances have proved. Warrington will require to be as accurate in !heir spotting as against Bradford. Broughton Rangers found to their cost that it does not do to run alongside the matt with the ball. Hogg lot West in with the winning try entirely through this bad fault.</p>
        <p>Just. a word as to the referes. Mr. Bruckshaw is not a popular official, but he is generally considered to be both fair and firm. His deficiencies are well known and it behoves Warrington to "play the whistle" all through. If they stop to appeal they will be let down as they were against Oldham at Wilders- pool. Every man must play on until he is pulled up.,</p>
        <p>By winning to-clay, Warrington will be the first Lancashire club to capture the Cup on Yorkshire soil.</p>
        <p>The tabalar history of-the finals is 'as follows: Winners. Runners-up. 1893-7 Batley St. Helens 1897-8 Batley Bradford 1898-9 Oldham Hunslet 1899-00 Swinton Salford 1900-1 Batley Warrington 1901-2 Broughton Rangers Salford 1902-3 Halifax Salford 1903-4 Halifax Warrington</p>
        <p>The comparative records of to-day's finalists are as follow:</p>
        <p>For Asst. G. T. P. G. T. P. Warrington v Morecambe v Keigbley A Keighley v Wigan -' 313 ... 00 0 v Bradford</p>
        <p>12 12 GO 1 1 5 For Aust. G. T, P. QT.P. Hull Ringstori Rover v Brooklaud Rovers lll7 7..; 1 5 v Leeds 0 1 3 ... d d 0 v Hunslot 1 2 8 ... 1 0 2 v Broughton Rangers</p>
        <p>14 22 91 ... 2313</p>
        <p>When Warrington were last at Leeds for the final tie the gate receipts constituted a record, 29,563 people being present and paying in £164 16s. 6d. Even these figures were boxtten, however, two years later, when Halifax and • Salford played at Headingly. FhiS match drew a crowd of 32,509 people, the pro- ceeds being £1834 Se. 6d. A great amount of interest is being taken in to-day's final all over Lancashire and Yorkshire and it is expected that a fresh record will be made.</p>
        <p>The exodus from Warrington will probably be such as will make the town appear deserted. No less than ten special trains are due to leave Bank Quay and Central Stations during the morning, and some 15,000 people are expected to make the journey to Leeds. A feature of the London and North Western service is that dining saloons are being provided,whereby the hungry excursionist may indulge in a cold lunch on the out- ward journey and a hot dinner on returning, at a charge, inclusive of railway fare, of less than 10s. The Cheshire Lines and Midland Railways have made special arrangements to ensure a clear line over their long route, the trains following closely upon one another.</p>
        <p>Admission to the gmund will be Is. with extra charges of t?s, and Is. for the large stands. An end- less supply of trams will be available to take the crowd up to I:leadingley.</p>
        <p>ton in the semi-final with Batley at Huddersfield in 1897. He has also played in the two finals since, and is now playing as well as ever. The team in 1897 consisted of Heesom; Moores, Cross, Barber and Isher- wood; &amp;rack Bate and Houghton; Taylor, Dakin, Hill, Barratt, Saholtze, Fairhurst, J. Swift and Nevins.</p>
        <p>Of to-day's team, Hallam, Fish, Isherwood, Dickin- son, Boardman and Morrison, played at Leeds in 190 I, while all but Kenyon, Brooks, Shugars, Swift and Belton played at Salford last April, so that there is plenty of experience in the team,</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield is expected to be present at the match, and if tlis team wins, Sir Gilbert Greenall will entertain the players and officials to dinner at Walton Hall. •</p>
        <p>NOTES FROM HULL-THE ROVERS TEAM.</p>
        <p>A Hull correspondent writes : —The success of the junior football club at Hull in reaching the Final round of the Northern Union Cup has aroused tre- mendous interest at the Yorkshire seaport. Kingston Rovers are about the youngest team in the Northern Union, but they have excelled the performances during the past few years of many of the old clubs. Eight seasons ago they carried off the Yorkshire Challenge Cup at the first time of reaching the final, whereas the Hull Senior Club has been struggling ever since the competition was .first originated over a decade ago to annex the trophy. The Premier Club at Hull has never reached the final tie for the Northern Union trophy, and the Rovers have again had to show the way to the old club in this respect. Naturally the !younger organization is the most popular club in Hull to-day, and their supporters possess a calm confidence in the ability of the team to overthrow Warrington at Headingley.</p>
        <p>The team will be emstituted exactly as against Broughton Rangers in the semi-final at Wakefield, and although the Rovers have indulged in a couple of league games, beating Wakefield and Salford this week, the majority of the leading players were resting, in fact, against' Salford not a single man who will be out to-day against Warrington was playing. Notwith- standing this the Rovers defeated a full side represent- ing Salford by 14 points to 2. Such a substantial victory against a Lancashire organisation has put the winners on good terms with themselves in view of the Headingley carnival.</p>
        <p>The .11ull representatives profess t &amp;wtys Lace Warrington with no little confidence, and it is remark- able that the wirepullers were the first team the Rovers ever defeated in Lancashire. This was on February 12th, 1898, in a friendly match, the Rovers winning by no fewer than 5 goal S 3 tries, 21 points to nil. This was in the first season the Craven-street team joined the Northern Union and was only their second appearance in the County Palatine. The Rovers still talk abut the performance with pride, and the score still stands as their record achievement outside Hull. .</p>
        <p>Anthony Starks and his colleagues are not too con- fident of success at the first time of reaching the Northern Union Cup final,hut the popular Rovers' and Yorkshire County skipper said the other 'day, " We are all triers and shall make the best of 'em 'hike.' If Warrington beat us they'll be a good team." Which being interpreted medns that the old International forward is vet.), well satisfied with his men. It is fully expected that between 6,000 and 8,000 people w,ll visit Leeds from gull, and great will be the diS- appointmeat if it's not a Yorkshire victory.</p>
