        <p>Toe above figures from my Hull correspondent make the Itovers a much heavier team than Warring- ton. From my knowledge of the players should say the weights are in ordinary clothes, while Warrington's are " stripped " weights. The difference between the two is about 10Ib, per man.</p>
        <p>• Since the last issue of the " Examiner ", Warrington have played two 'League matches. On Good Friday they put a weak team in the field against St. -Helens, the presence of Fish alone turning the cale in *your of the home side by 5 points to nil. The following an fiven weaker side went to Wakefield and was deleatia by r gs33b3 to pi), The Wakefield fowards had all their own way, but the dice ha War- rington backs was good enough to Leer; th.! line from being crossed.</p>
        <p>The Leave latld epEo,clate is as follows : —Against— P. W. L. V, Ti Pts. G. T. Pts Pts. Oldham 34...25... 8... 1..,118' 0529L..28 31-158...51 Bradford 33...23... 8... 2...3J 291 .21 35-153-48 Brou't'n R 34...22...10... 2...35 e 5-295-41 31-175-46 Leeds Waregt'n 34...20.. 12... 2...38 48-220-30 30--150-42 Salford Wigan 34...18...15... 1...34 54.-230_36 39-189...37 Hull 34. 15...15... 4...37 50-221...41 44-214...34 linnslet Leigh Halifax Hull K.R 34-15...19_ 0...43 38-200...35 50-220...30 Wakefield 31...13...19... 2...44 22-54..35 47-211...28 Batley 34 .12 ..19... 3...38 28-160-45 46-223...27 'Widnes Swinton 33...12...19... 2-22 36-15 38 78-196...26 St. Helens 33... 9...23... 1...32 31-166-47 78-328...19 IRuncorn... 34... 7...26... 1...26 27-133-47 69-301...15</p>
        <p>Wigan are the winners of the South-West Lanca- shire Cup. They made no mistake about, trouncing Leigh on Good Friday, and thus finish up with a clear lead of one point.</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League complete is as follows</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For. Agst. Pts. Wignn...lo ... S ... '2 ... 0 ... 101 ... 45 ... 16 Wgton 10 ... 7 ... 2 ... 1 So ... 37 ... 15 Leigh ...10 ... 7 ... 3 ... 0 61 ... 51 ... 14 StHelonslo ... 3 ... 6 ... 1 46 ... 59 ... 7 Widnes 10 ... S ... 7 ... 0.......40 ... 79 ... Runcorn]o ... 0 ... 10 ... 0 32 ... 79 ... 0</p>
        <p>Runcorn and St. Helens arenow both doomed to the second division. It will be seen what an important bearing the South-West Lancashire Competition has had on Runcorn's fate. They have. lost every one of the ten matches played, their neighbours thus proving their worst foes.</p>
        <p>' 'Warrington "A " ought soon to he hailed as champions of the Lancashire Combination. My tiguries of last week, which had been•supplied by Mr. Houghton, of -St. Helens, proved to be wrong, and the mistake was rectified when the "A " team went to St. Helens on Good Friday. • They scored a good win, and came borne with the knowledge that they had only to beat Widnes and Chadderton at Warrington to win the medals. Widnes were . easily vanquished on Easter Monday and Chadderton visit Wilderspool to-day.</p>
