      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WARRINGTON TEAM.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON TEAM.</p>
        <p>A great, misfortune has befallen the Warriugtonians, tor Jack Preston, the crack forward and goal-kicker, finds himself unable to torn out in the final. The injury be received to the knee on Good Friday has given him considerable pain, and though he spent the week-end in bed he is still under medical supervision and Oita unfit to play. Everybody will sympathise with Preston in his great disappointment for he was vew keen On helping Warrington to lift the Cup. The prominent part be has played in the Cup-ties up-to- date, coupled with his weight and cleverness make his loss one which is generally felt by the team and their supporters. Preston is the heaviest of the Warrington scrummagers, scaling 14 stone 71b in his playing clothes-.</p>
        <p>, Owing to Preston's injury, and the-fact that Board- . man, Fish, and D. Davies are .all suffering from sprains, the team was not picked on Thursday even- ing, but the probability is that all the \ men, with the ' exception bf Preston, will be fit and well for the match. The fifteen will probably be Ilalloin, full back ; Fish, Isherwood, Dickinson, and Kenyon, threequarters ; D. Davies and Brooks,. half backs ; Boardman, Shugars, Thomas, Jolley, Naylor, Swift, ' Cook, Belton, or Morrison.</p>
        <p>I have been at some-trouble in obtaining the ages, heights, and weights of the Warrington players, and they afford an interesting study, viz. : " Age. Height. Weight. . ft. ins. at. lb. Hallam (back) Fish Isherwood 1 ‘ 23 ... 5 fq ... 11— 0 The' boisterous, unsettled weather of Eastertide upset three-quarters 23 ... Dickenson i 5-1(g ... 11— G i the calculations of many who had counted on success- Kenyon - ... 11— 0 ful tours, and caused them to seek enjoyment nearer D. Davies i half-backs 25 ... 5 71 ... 10— 8 home. When the members of the Warrington Cycling l Brooks 23 ... 5 8 ... 11-- 7 Club make up their minds to go on tour, it takes a great ' deal to deter them, and several parties spent the week- 13oardmau 1 end in North' Wales. It must not be assumed, however, Thomas 1 Shugars I 23 ... 5-11- ... 13_10 that all journeyed per "bike." Several' contingents Jolley ... r 9 ... Ig_ 6 chose the motor car as a more rapid and comfortable ... 6._ il ... 12_12 means, while others did the journey by train. Belton forwards Navlor Swift selves with visiting the usual resorts in that picturesque Cook neighbourhood. Of late years the Warrington wheelers' Morrison i Easter visi • 23 ... 5-11 ... 12— 0 tto Llangollen has been signalized by a fall of snow. Last week-etid's expedition was no exception, ----- 1 interesting tact that Dan Isherivood is the and on Sunday snow lay several inches deep on the It is an eerwsiis. A large section of the club made Llanfair- ' only player in to-day's team who played for Warring-</p>
        <p>fechan their headquarters, but I hear that the party of twelve could only muster two bicycles. Two motor car parties toured North Wales, and perhaps on the whole they had the best of the bargain. As cycling tours, the events of last week-end were decidedly dis- appointing.</p>
        <p>To-day's fixture is an inter-club run to Hoo Green with the Manchester Wheelers, but as on this occasion "all roads will lead to Leeds," it is rather too much to expect a large turnout.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington opened their season disarpointingly on Saturday, tutving to admit defeat at the hands of Bolton. In face of the fact that the team was only nine strong, the result was not surprising, in fact those wielders of the willow who did respond to the call made a brave fight and succeeded in keeping the adverse margin down to ten. When the town has recovered from football cup tie fever, enthusiasm in the summer sport may be expected and there is every indication that Warrington intend to make the best of the season when once the club has got into its usual swing. The league fixtures should be a great attraction and when they are com- menced we may hope for encouraging gates.</p>
        <p>Bolton placed a strong eleven in the field on Saturday. No fewer than four professionals did duty, including the famous Albert Ward. Warrington lacked the ser- vices of their captain, E. A. Connor, P. B. Hawtin, J. Thompson, and T. Houghton. Batting first, the Boltondans placed 71 to their credit. The bowling of Mason, Warrington's new professional, and Thewlis gave the batsmen a great deal of trouble,and the highest scores attained were 15 by Thwaites and Pacev, and 13 by Hassall. Mason captured 5 wickets for 26 ruus—a promising start—while Thewlis did himself credit by accounting for 4 wickets at a cost of 20 runs.</p>
        <p>In response the Warrington men played gamely, and compiled 61, Thewlis rendering very acceptable service by knocking up H. Hampson was also in good form and scored 10. The bowling of Silcock on the Bolton side was most effective, his capture being 5 wickets for 120 runs. Redford was responsible for 3 wickets at a cost of 16 runs.</p>
        <p>, Bowling. Bowling will not be seriously considered in Warring- ton until after to-day, for being sportsmen local trundlers are following the fortunes of the Warringtott Rugby Football Club, with interest mainly of an active character. It therefore follows that until football has given place to summer pastimes there will be little to record as having transpired on Warrington greens. In consequence of the expected general exodus from Warrington to-day two League engagements have been postponecl—Fearn- head v. Penketh Park and Bulls Tlead v. Horse and Jockey. Apparently however, the Cemetery and Sankey Chapel House clubs are unaffected by the wave of enthusiasm as their fixture will be completed.</p>
        <p>The Liberal Club green was opened for the season on Saturday by a four hundred main. The turf was in excellent condition—a fact which reflects credit upon the manager of the club (Mr. Randall)—and some excel- lent trundling was witnessed. The prize winners were Messrs. IL Marrow and C. Jordan, F. Plumnton and W. Leigh,aml J. Gibson and E. A. Sanders. Another main will be played next Saturday and the week following the Liberals will be entertained by the Saracen's Head players.</p>
        <p>There was a fair number of entries for an open main which was hold on the King and Queen Green on Mon- day, many players of undoubted ability being included in the list of competitors. The final lay between J. Shaw and J. W. ‘vorsley, both of whom figured pro- minently List season in *league engagements. Shaw last year was also in the running in the works competition main and on Monday he proved a littlo too strong for Worsley' though he did not carry about the prize without having to fight for it.</p>
        <p>The opening main in connection with the Cemetery Club was also decided on Monday the prize winners being Messrs. W. Po;my, J. Wood and T. Healey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EASTER HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>THE EASTER HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>; The lateness of Easter this year proved of no great ladvantageas far as the weather conditions were con-, corned. Keen winds and occasional rain made it 'unpleasant to be out of doors on Sunday and although there was an improvement on Monday, a chilliness in the atmosphere militated somewhat against the enjoy- ment of holiday-makers, except in the afternoon when the sunshine was more powerful. Easter Day is celebrated as the greatest of festivals in the Anglican full Roman Catholic Churches in fact at most places of worship the note of rejoicing dominated on Sunday. Many churches were beautifully decorated with choice sprino., flowers, the altars, pulpits and fonts being embellished with particular taste. At the celebrations of the Holy Communion there were very large numbers of communicants.</p>
        <p>During the week-enrmany townspeople availed them- selves of the apecial facilities provided by the railway companies for visiting sea-side and rural resorts. The following figures give an idea of the heavy bookings : Central Station : Saturday, Liverpool and New Brighton, GOO ; Manchester, 500 ; Southport, 50. Monday, Manchester, 1,000; Liverpool and New Brighton, 1,200; Sonthpnrt,2so. Bank Quay : Saturday Birminghan and Wolverhampton, 100 ; Frodsham, Chestor and North Wales, 200; Blackpool, 150. Monday IBlackpool, 150 ; Birmingham and Wolverhampton, 200; Frodslaarn, Chester and North Wales, 400. For those who remained at home football matches , were the principal attractions, while in the evening the places of amusement were well patroni%ed. A large audience assembled in the Parr Hall to witness the axaellent exhibition of animated photographs by that-. NeW Century Company.</p>
        <p>.ot TRIP TO CLRETRORPRS. A party organized br Mr. Buckley, of School..bro, had an enjoyable trip to GritUSby and Cleethorpes on Good Friday. The journey to Sheffield was tedious, but from the cutlery town to Grimsby a splendid run was experienced. On arrival at Grimsby at 11.25 the docks and other places of interest were visited and after- wards the piny proceeded by train to Cleethorpes. A drive round the suburbs served to pleasantly while away the afternoon, and on returning to Grimsby refresh- ments were served and a start was made for home. Warrington was reached at 10.20. Mr. W. Smalley (hon. secretary) and Mr. Buckley had charge of the arrangements. THE TRAFFIC ON THE TRAMS.</p>
        <p>_ .. Traffic on the Corporation tramways is always exceptionally heavy at holiday times. Below we give the total returns of passengers and receipts for the tour days, Friday, Saturday, Sunday and Monday, together with the returns for .the corresponding days last Easter:—</p>
