      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>243 4 1 229 10 11</head>
        <p>243 4 1 229 10 11</p>
        <p>PENtETH.</p>
        <p>Tnr, VICAR AS A CYCLIST.—The Vicar, the nay. J. R. Jones, M.A., has recently completed his 2,000 th mile on his cycle since the sth of May last year. He uses the cycle for his visiting, Sm., and the mileage is eloquent of his activity in performing his parish functions and duties. EASTERTIDE.—The holidays were 'observed in the customary manner, work being generally stispended. The Friday morning service at St. Mary's Church was well attended, and the annual tea meeting and scholars' prize distribution at the Wesleyan school in the evening was also well patronised. The tea had been prepared under the supervision of Misses Wilkinson and Booth. The usual concert was afterwards held, the chair being taken by Mr. J. T. Ambler, and Miss E. Hughes of Bootie. presented the prizes to the scholars for attend- ance. The Communion services at the St. Mary's Church and Mission room on Sunday morning were very well / attended, over 200 communicants taking part. Monday was quietly spent, many going away for the day. Dnarrf or MR. W. J. WIGFIELD.—His many friends will learn with regret of the death of Mr. W. J. Wig. field, who died at his residence at Wallasey after several months illness at the ago of 69 years. Up till about IS months ago Mr. Wiglield resided at Home Lea,Penketh, and for 16 years had occupied the post of secretary to the Penketh Tanning Company, Limited, but failing health compelled him to relinquish his post. Mr. Wig- field was a native of Rotherham, and his wife is the daughter of the founder of the well-known firm of William Walker and Sons, Tanners,Bolton. Mr. Wigfield was through his life a loyal supporter of the Wesleyan Church, and during his residence in Penketh he took an active part in the work, filling the post of Organist and Choirmaster for many years at the Wesleyan Church. His cleparturo from Penketh was greatly re- gretted, as he was much loved and respected for his kind and gentle nature. He was a Liberal by conviction but never took an active part in party strife. flis only son, William,is manager of a tannery in Scotland,and his eldest daughter Edith, Was married to Mr.C.H.Mitchell who is connected with this town. His second daughter Gertrude,has been for some years a missionary in India, in connection with the Wesleyan Missionapy Society, but lately returned on furlough, and was able to assist her younger sister Hilda in nursing her father during his la`ST illness. Much sympathy is expressed generally in the village, with Mrs. Wigfield and her family in their bereavement.</p>
