      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WORD TO THE WISE.</head>
        <p>A WORD TO THE WISE.</p>
        <p>The fickle nature of the climate of this country often gives rise to a variety of ailments and complaints, which assuming at first the form of only a slight indisposition, if neglected and unheeded may become the germ of serious diseases, which will undermine the constitution, and finally result in a long and lingering illness, and perhaps terminate fatally. The variable temperature and the changeable weather of the last month or so, makes it incumbent on all of us to exercise wise and prudent precautions to counteract the evil effects which the weather may have had upon our health and comfort. Already we frequently hear such complaints as " No appetite," "These frequent headaches," " This languid feeling," and dozens ot other expressions which all point to the urgent need of a good Tonic. Now there are several tonic mixtures offered to the Public, but none which have been so uniformly successful as Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. This Preparation is acknowledged to be The Best Remedy of The Ago for Nervousness, Weakness. Chest Affections, Palpitation of the Heart, Indigestion, Liver Complaint, and Influenza. The numerous cases of marvellous cures effected, and the restoration to perfect health, of those who had long suffered and had vainly tried other remedies, can be accounted for by simply stating the effect which Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has upon the System. Ist. It strikes at the source of the disease, and by removing the causes of disease, the evil effects soon vanish. .</p>
        <p>2nd. It strengthens that part of the system which is weakest, and therefore, most liable to the attacks of colds and all diseases. 3rd. It purifies the blood, and thus gives new life and force to all parts of the body, for disease cannot exist where there is a free circulation of pure blood.</p>
        <p>4th. It gives healthy action to the digestive organs and to the liver, thus aiding to keep away Indigestion and the host of different forms of disease which re-</p>
        <p>ult from it, sth. By removing impurities, strengthening the weak parts of the system, and purifying the blood,the human frame is well fortified to withstand the attacks of disease. It is repelled, and its place taken by health and strength, through the effective action of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. Sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d. and -Is. Gd. each, or will be sent carriage free for these prices, direct from the Sole Proprietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Com- pany, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. Beware of Imitations. See that you get the genuine Preparation with the name," Gwilym Evans "on Label, Stamp, and Bottle, without which none is genuine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A ROUGH CUSTOMER SURPRISED. At the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday Albert Foster, Rodney-street, was charged with being drunk and disorderley and was assaulting P.C. Turner.—The Chief Constable said that the case possessed some extra- ordinary features. Late the previous night P.C. Turner was on duty in Parr-street, a lonely neighbourhood when he net the prisoners who he knew very well. Prisoner asked the way to Church-street and Turner having given him the necessary directions prisoner bade the officer "good-night." Turner walked away but presently became aware that he was being followed, and on looking round saw the prisoner. Foster called out "You are alone aren't you "to which Turner replied" Yes." Prisoner then said " I will serve you same as I served your com- rade " and made a rush at the officer. A struggle,which lasted for twenty minutes, followed in which the prisoner got a severe thrashing. Turner blew his whistle and the brother of the prisoner appeared on the scene and assisted the officer to lock Forster up.—Mr. Steel, who appeared for the prisoner, said his client had a very hazy recollection of what had taken place and it was no use his endeavouring to contravene Turner's statement. The case was one of the unfortunate results of men of the class of the prisoner having holidays. Prisoner had been visiting some relatives and had had too much drink. He had a wife and eight children, and considering the thrashing he had received he (Mr. Steel ) asked the magistrates to deal as leniently as they possibly could with the prisoner.—Prisoner who had been previously convicted for an assault upon the police was fined 20s. and cost.—The Chief Constable thanked the brother of the prisoner for the assistance which he had given to Turner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Dr.. Fox _.(in the chair) and Mr. C</p>
        <p>Jackson Lfolmes.</p>
        <p>" THE WORST WOMAN IN WARRING TON."—Mary Broderick, 4, Hallam-lane, made her 40th appearance before the Warrington magistrates, being ctarged with using obscene language. She was described by the Chief Constable as the worst and most obscene-tongued woman in Warrington.—A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed. GAMBLING IN THE gTREETS.—John Dodd, 9, Cart- wright-street, Walter Pritchard, 17, Cartwright-street, and Alfred Barlow, 11, Manley-street, were each lined 3s. 6d. for tossing money in a public street. THROW ENG STONES AT A LAM PLIGHTER.—Edwin Hughes, 83, Plumpton-street, Henry Fisher, 11, Dale- street and James Smith, 37, Plumpton-street, were charged with throwing stones in Plumpton-street.—The Chief Constable said the prosecution was taken at the instance of the Lighting Committee of the Corporation. The facts of the case were that early one morn- ing a corporation lamplighter was extinguishing the gas lamps in Plumpton-street, when the defendants who were returning home began to throw stones at him. —Harry Manln, the lamplighter, described the incident. He had a little trouble with the defendants and had to threaten them. They then commenced to throw stones at him, hitting him in several places.—Hughes denied that the, threw stones. They, he said, only threw " taters.' —A fine of 48. and costs was imposed in each case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
