      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>JCIENCE NOTES AND</head>
        <p>JCIENCE NOTES AND</p>
        <p>GLE ANINGS.</p>
        <p>Avcoranso to Dr. Holland, an X-ray examination is an excellent way of 'Ha:musing a •I'oracio aneurism, and it is also useiul iu„'.vatchiur: ',regress of consumption.</p>
        <p>MESSES. WILLOWS and PECK, in a paper to the Royal Society, have shewa that radium beta rays extinguish the spark from a IVimshurst electrical machine. Lenard rays produce a similar effect, but not Röntgen rays.</p>
        <p>IT is stated by an American journal that holo- phane globes for electric lamps cause a loss of 14 per cent, of the light, while prismatic globes lose 19 per cent., white opal 55, yellow opal 60, and green opal as much as 78 per cent, of the light.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>REAGENTS THAT PREVENT RUST.</head>
        <p>REAGENTS THAT PREVENT RUST.</p>
        <p>According to a paper to the Chemical Society carbonate of sodium or ammonium, borax, bichro- mate or ferrocyanide of potassium, nitrate of sodium, carbonate of potassium, and hydroxide of calcium prevent rusting of sheet iron more or less effectively. In general the reagents preventing rust are those in presence of which peroxide of hydrogen decomposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MOUNDS OF LOUISIANA.</head>
        <p>THE MOUNDS OF LOUISIANA.</p>
        <p>The natural mounds of Louisiana are ascribed to! three causes: (1) Gas from decaying vegetable matter underground and water associated with it have built up cones of sand on the surface; (2) dunes of sand drifted about clumps of low vegeta- tion; (3) old hills of the leaf-cutting or the white ant. In Western Texas are anthills 40ft. to 501 t. in diameter and lft. to 2ft. high. Hills of the Atta leaf-cutting ant in Cuba are as much as 101 t. or 12ft. high, and a diameter several times as great. White ants, though tropical, may have existed in Louisiana in past ages. According to Mr. Veatch in a paper to the Geological Society of Washington, the two last theories are the best supported.</p>
        <p>WHERE THE GLOW-WORM GETS ITS LIGHT. , To the statement that absolutely nothing is known of the light of the glow-worm, Dr. T. L. Phipson takes exception, shewing that the phos- phorescent organs of the glow-worm and the fire- By have been the subject of numerous inquiries. The source of the light proves to be not the organs themselves but a white unctuous substance known as "noctilucine," and, as Dr. Phipson himself shewed thirty years ago that this substance is a special product of decaying fish, it is regarded as the probable light-giving material in all animal and vegetable phosphorescence. Luminous bacteria, the tiny organisms that give light to large areas of the sea's surface, certain fungi and other living forms seem to produce noctilucine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>COLD WATER AND POISON.</head>
        <p>COLD WATER AND POISON.</p>
        <p>In connection with the subject of water, there is one peculiar property of that liquid with which everyone should be made acquainted, and that is its capacity for absorbing impurities, which increases proportionately the colder it gets. Hence, water that has stood in an insufficiently ventilated sleep- ing-chamber all night is not only unpleasant, but positively injurious to drink, since it readily absorbs the poisonous gases given oil by respiration and the action of the skin. An ordinary pitcher of water, under such conditions, at a temperature of 60deg. will be found to have absorbed during the night from a pint to a pint and a-half of carbonic acid gas, and an increase of ammonia. Ice water is an objectionable drink at all times, but if it is indulged in, the vessel containing it should never be left uncovered in sleeping or sitting rooms, because at freezing point its capacity for absorbing these deleterious substances is nearly doubled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>KENTUCKY AEROLITE.</head>
        <p>A KENTUCKY AEROLITE.</p>
        <p>On the evening of November 15th, 1902 after a luminous course across the sky over Ohio and Kentucky, an aerolite or stony (not metallic) meteorite exploded near Bath Furnace, Salt Lake, Kentucky, and pieces were afterwards picked up. One of these, found by a squirrel hunter in a hole at the root of a tree, is now in the American Museum of Natural History, and, according to Professor Ward, of Chicago, is of triangular shape, with a pitted and furrowed surface. It consists of fine compact olivine and enstalite (silicates of magnesia), with specks of nickel iron. Its composition is like that of volcanic rocks, but has not the slaggy structure of lava. Stony meteorites are evidence that terrestrial matter also exists in space. Reichenbach shrics that a meteorite falling forty miles a second through the atmo- sphere woald, by a compression of air on its surface, attain a slMace temperature of 7,200de,g. Fah. Hence the melted surface.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WHAT BECOMES OF .THE MICROBES?</head>
        <p>WHAT BECOMES OF .THE MICROBES?</p>
        <p>As most, if not all, of the more important diseases are to-day being ascribed to microbes, the question naturally arises, when a person dies of a con- tagious disease, as to what becomes of the germs that caused his death,and that abound in his body? The subject has been largely investigated by Dr. Klein, of Germany, who has buried the bodies of infected animals for stated periods, and then examined them for germ‘life. The doctor con- cludes as follows: The bacillus prodigiosus and the staphylococcus aureus are found living in dead bodies twenty-eight days after interment. Longer stay under ground kills them; after six weeks no culture develops. The bacillus of cholera lives nineteen days, but does not preserve its reproductive powers after eighteen days. The resistance of Eberth's bacillus (typhoid) is nearly the same. The germ of plague is always alive after seventeen days of burial, but not after three weeks. The bacillus of consumption does not survive the person it has killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>SUN SPOTS.</head>
        <p>SUN SPOTS.</p>
        <p>The following interesting extract is taken from an address delivered by Professor W. Campbell at the International Congress of Arts and Sciences at St. Louis:</p>
        <p>The study of our sun forms the principal basis of I astrophysical research. The sun is an ordinary star, comparable in size and condition with millions of other stars, but it is the only one near enough to I shew a disc. The point image of a distant star must be studied as an integrated whole; whereas the sun may be observed in considerable geometrical detail. We are unable to study the body of the sun, except by indirect methods. The interior is invisible. The spherical body which we popularly speak of as the sun is hidden from view by the opaque photosphere. This photospheric veil, includ- ing the sun spots; the brilliant faculm and flocculi, projecting upward from the photosphere; the reversing layer, in effect immediately overlying the photosphere; the chromosphere, a stratum associated with and overlying the reversing layer; the promi- nences apparently ejected from the chromosphere; and the corona, extending outward from the sun in all directions to enormous distances; these super- latively interesting features of the sun constitute the only portions accessible for direct observation; and they are an insignificant part of its mass. They are literally the sun's outcasts. Our know- ledge of the sun is based almost exclusively upon the study of these outcasts. Nevertheless we are able to formulate a fairly simple and satisfactory theory of its constitution. The surface strata are radiating heat into sur- rounding space. To maintain the supply, it is imperative that convection currents should carry the cooled masses down into the interior, and bring corresponding hot masses up to the surface. These , currents make the sun a very tempestuous body. Further, the outrushing materials must acquire the higher rotational speeds of the surface strata and the inrushing must lose their tangential momentum; and these can scarcely be ineffective factors in the sun's circulatory system. The sun spots are the most extensively studied and the least understood of all solar phenomena. That they are large-scale interruptions in the photosphere, and at the same time the most striking evidence of atmospheric circulation, there can be no doubt. Observations made near the sun's limb, to determine whether the spots are elevations or depressions with reference to the photosphere, seem not to be reliable, perhaps because of abnormal refractions in the strata overlying and surrounding the spots. In the earth's atmosphere, a high barometer is the indication of descending currents, which generate heat by compression and prevent cloud formation. Is not the umbra of a spot an area of high pressure, which forces the solar atmosphere slowly downward, prevent' g ; cloud formation in that area, but favouring the 1 growth of brilliant pacuhe and flocculi in the regions of uprush surrounding the spot—a theory first suggested by Secchi ? The visible spots are not the sole evidences of circulation. The surface is covered with a network of interstices, or vents between clouds, which pro- bably exercise all the functions of the visible spate, Out on a smalkac scale.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday April 29, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday April 29, 1905.</p>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES AND LABOUR. During the month there has been considerable discussion on the relation of the Free Churches to labour. The Rev. J. E. Rattenbury's speech at Manchester on "The Institutional Church," wherein he said that the Churches were run for the benefit of the middle classes, was strongly resented by some ministers and others. An inte- resting symposium appears in this month's Free Church Chronicle on the question, "Are the Middle-class Churches to Blame for the Aliena- tion of the Working Classes ?" The replies from representative Free Churchmen are very interesting. Mr. H. R. Mansfield, M.P., for instance, writes: "I agree with Mr. flatten bury. We have allowed religion to develop too much upon one side of life. 'We have allowed our chapels to become too much places of spiritual entertainment. ,We have treated the religion of our Lord too much as though men were only souls, and have neglected too much the human needs of life. The teach- ing of Christ, as I understand it, is for all the needs of life—bodily, mental, social, spiritual. If we are to reach the people, we must g 6 back to Christ and learn and enforce the lesson of the marriage-feast, the feeding of the hungry, the healing of the sick, and the raising of the fallen."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>FREE CHURCH COUNCIL ON CIRCUIT.</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCH COUNCIL ON CIRCUIT.</p>
        <p>Directly after Easter the National Free Church Council commences what might be called a circuit of the Churches. In order to • reach the whole country during his presidential 'Torton has arranged to hold wen or eight Coo which all the Free Church Councils in each respective area are to send representatives. These Conventions are not held with the pur- pose of denouncing the Government or propos- ing Education legislation, they ate not to emphasise the rights of Free Churchmen; they have for their intention the deepening of spiritual life. The Conferences to be held in the mornings deal with such subjects as "Con- fession ot Sin," " Consecration," and "The Work of the Holy Spirit." There will also be Sunrise Prayer Meetings, Conferences over tea, and public meetings in the evening. The first of those, held at the end of April at Plymouth, includes representatives from the whole of Devon and Cornwall. The Rev. J. H. Jowett preaches the Convention Sermon, and among those taking part are—in addition to Dr. Horton—Rev. Thomas Phillips, 8.A., Dr. J. Rendel Harris, Rev. Thomas Law, Rev. J. E. Rattenbury, Rev. Silas K. Hocking, and Rev. R. Wardlaw Thompson. The Mayor and Mayoress of Plymouth are giving the reception.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>FUTURE FIXTURES.</head>
        <p>FUTURE FIXTURES.</p>
        <p>After the visit to Plymouth these Conventions are to be held at Llandrindod Wells, May 22 to 2.1; Lowestoft, June 19 to 22; Sunderland, July 17 to 20; Oxford, September 18 to 21; London, October 23 to 26 ; Harrogate, 3;ovembor 20 to 23.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>VAST ORGANISATION.</head>
        <p>A VAST ORGANISATION.</p>
        <p>In spite of the few years of the life of the National Free Church Council, its organisation ht s grown tremendously. The General Com- mittee elected at Manchester has just met and appointed the following Sub-Committees: (1) Finance, (2) Publication, (3) Organising, (4 Temperance, (5) Legal, (6) Social Questions, (7) Girls' Guild, (8) Education, (9) Social Purity, ( 10) Continental, (11) Election, (12) Young Free Churchmen's Auxiliary, (13) Missionary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>HISTORY OF THE QUAKERS.</head>
        <p>HISTORY OF THE QUAKERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Udmund Harvey, 8.A., L.C.C., has just completed a volume dealing with the rise and history of the Quakers. The book contains an admirable survey, and has for its frontis- piece a reproduction of the famous Lely portrait of Fox now hanging in Swanmore College, U.S.A. The volume is the eighth issued in the "Eras of Nonconformity" series edited by Rev. C. Silveqter Horne., M.A., and pub- libhed by the National Free Church Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>VIE CONSCIENCE CLAUSE.</head>
        <p>VIE CONSCIENCE CLAUSE.</p>
        <p>An authoritative circular has been issued by the National Free Church Council on the rights of parents under the Conscience Clause, and under the new bye-law. The writer is the Rev. J. Hirst Hollowell. As confusion exists with regard to this matter, Mr. Hollowell's leaflet should be very welcome. Copies can be obtained on application to the National Free Church Council, Memorial Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>FREE CHURCH DEVELOPMENTS:</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCH DEVELOPMENTS:</p>
        <p>To be fully abreast of the times with regard to Free Church matters, the Free Church Year Book (to be published in a day or two) is in- valuable. The volume for 1905 has been re- arranged, and many new features have been added. In view of the attention being given to missionary work at the present time, an account of the Free Churches on the Mission Field should be very interesting. Then there is the Legal Information for Free Churchmen, which deals with procedure entailed by marriages and burials, and also gives informa- tion on the conscience clause, registration of buildings, gifts to charities, &amp;e. It is important for every Free Churchman to know what his denomination stands for, and a series of articles describing the distinctive features of the various bodies should be valu- abie. In view of the probability of a General Election in the near future a list of Free Church- men in Parliament will be scanned with interest. The list shows that there are Free Churchmen in all parties—Liberal, Conservative, Liberal Unionist, and Nationalist. In addition to these fresh articles there are the usual features dealing with Free Church Statistics for the year, &amp;c. The official account of the Manchester Meetings is given, also a resum6 of the year's work. In spite of the new additions, the price of the Free Church Year Book remains the same, 2s. 6d., and it is issued by the National Free Church Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES AND FOREIGN MISSIONS._</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCHES AND FOREIGN MISSIONS._</p>
        <p>The National Free Church Council has entered upon a campaign for arousing interest in Foreign Mission Work. In view of this de- parture, the publishing of a volume on Foreign Missions," by Rev. G. Currie Martin, M.A., B D.. is very timely. Those interested in the Mission Field are often in danger of concentrat- ing their thoughts upon a part of the work, and a brief survey of the field of operations is useful for extending the view and getting the proper prospective of the Mission Field. Mr. Martin's book would admirably serve asla primer for the study of Foreign Missions. It is issued by the National Free Church Council at is. 6d.</p>
        <p>THE MISSIONARY OIITLOOK.</p>
        <p>"The Outlook on the Mission Field" should be very timely just now. The Free Churchman for this month contains the first of a series under this title. The Rev. A. P. Begg, MA., contributes an article on "Work among Edu- cated Hindoos," which throws an interesting sidelight on the problems of our gseat depend- ency.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL. FAlLCRES.—According to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, April 21st, was 178, viz. :—New bankruptcy proceedings published in the London Gazette, 96 ; deeds of arrangement registered, 82. The TRUSSRS.—The new Water Pad Truss, never gets Hard.— respective numbers in the corresponding week of last The new Washable Waterproof Truss is alwa, s clean, cool, year were: Bankruptcies, 91 ; deeds of arrangement 95—total 186; being a decrease of 8* The total and comfortable.—The Trianifular Fluid Pad Truss Is number of commercial failures recorded during the 16 effectual In most difficult CaSeg. Tresses of every known weeks of the present year is 3,032: the total number description.—W HIT'S &amp; WRIGHT. Surgical Instrament and recorded in the corresponding 16 weeks of last year was Artificial Limb Makers, 98, Renshaw at,, Li7erpool. Wirt 2) 2.760, showing an increase of 272.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>PHYLLIS THE ELUSIVE.</head>
        <p>PHYLLIS THE ELUSIVE.</p>
        <p>Lovely Woman.</p>
        <p>[By Godfrey Dean.]</p>
        <p>Phyllis had been reading a book by an author who apparently,z.passes his time an distributing delica. ' hnmonr and aromatic tobacco smoke.</p>
        <p>She described it to me as "lovely " a woman'A adjective, of course ; but Phyllis appreciated the spirit of the work. •</p>
        <p>"There are not many humorists 0.. e. his kind," she said.</p>
        <p>I agreed. "If a man is not a humorist," I said, "he may be a disciple of the Apostles of humour. Few— extraordinarily few—are without a sense of humour. But I have heard of one, and his was a very, very sad case." ' Really," she said. "And he was a writer, too ; which made it worse." " What of him?" Phyllis asked. "He wrote a book about the men of a certain nationality which was amusing; that is, if one did not realize that he was serious. Many people, in fact, credited him with a certain sense of humour; heavy, perhaps, but yet passable." "And what became of him ? "</p>
        <p>"Eventually he set ont to bludgeon a rainbow." "I suppose he didn't achieve his object," she said, "but you might tell me the story in full." "To begin with," I said, "he (metaphorically) took a seat on a high pedestal, for which most men have an aversion. If one must mount a pedestal, there is a clumsy and a graceful way of doing it. Moreover, taking a little thought, one may almost hide the pedestal ;at least antistically drape the crudity. But this man, not having any artistic drapery—or, indeed, drapery of any kind—for the pedestal, which he laboriously climbed, had perforce tp do without." Rhylis smiled. "He couldn't have borro xed the material then</p>
        <p>" Of course not," I said (though aware she would not for a moment Ibink such a proceeding proper) " it would have been bad form—nay, unpardonable. " Yes ?—go on," she begged. • "Having attained a most commanding position the man proceeded to bellow forth to the multitude. His story was most exhaustive—in length and effect." " What was it all about ?" "Need you ask? Woman was his subject. And s obsession had destroyed an sense of proportion." " You've led me to think" she said, " such would be cause and effect." •</p>
        <p>"'Wis. But one may receive different effects from similar causes. All depends on the point of view." " I long to hear the story," she said—" his story." "Of its kind it was wonderful, not edifying. His audience thought at first that he was going to expound some new truth or new gospel. But it was neither. Betraying a sublime indifference to (or ignorance of) the elementary fatt that woman's • point of view in many respects is different—justifiably, necessarily— from man's, he set himself to relate in detail the enormity of her offence ; not in man's, but his eyes !" "At the outset, woman (he said in effect) had no place in the general scheme of things; but in any case she should only be tolerated, and except when her lord willed it hide her face from sight." "He declaimed that woman was a blight upon man's life, and appealed to the schoolboy as one who recog- nised mother, sister, or the wife as such. Were the schoolboy present his answer would have been, what rot ! '"</p>
        <p>"And then, reviewing woman in every walk of life, he. regarded her with _wondering_ contempt. ,</p>
        <p>"What do you think of it ali.? " I asked.</p>
        <p>" He was guilty of gross ingratitude," Phyllis said. "I would suggest," I said," that immediately he con- cluded.his longlarangue he should haN, e been charged, each separate court ,of the indictment bassed on the slurs he cast upon woman ; the Court to consist of a judge, who should be a high-minded matron in touch with public affairs, and a mixed jury, also of your sex, representative of all classes and professions.. , To the prisoner, of course, might be extended the 1, privilege of being defended by one or more of that sex whose fair characters he so wantonly aspersed." " Would any be willing, do you think ?' "I'm sure some would be chivalrous enough— though it would be a thankles: task—to undertake his defence, if a posOble line could be discovered. To give him every chance of vindicating, or even excus- ing, his conduct, they would use the knowledge they possessed of their own sex in his behalf. And, besides he might have the assistance of counsel from among his fellows—superior men—who, though shocked, would do their best. They, of course, would first advise a Ilea of guilty.", Doubtless, however, the prisoner, realising that no leniency would be extended by the justly indignant Court In this event, would attempt a defence." ,1 The trembling man would soon realise how easy it is to make groundless assertions and how hard to justify them. The trial would be strictly fair, though; every allowance made for the prisoner. ' "then when the lady counsel for the prosecution I first invited and then compelled the prisoner to state what kindnesses women had done for him, out of his own mouth would come his condemnation. From a baby when they kissed his tears away; through boy- hood, when sullenness was met and softened ; youth upwards—woman had taken his burden of sorrow on to her slim shoulders and turned a smiling face to hide a heart of grief." "And so, in the end, the prisoner would be found guilty on all counts of the indictment, and without an extenuating circumstance." "What do you think his sentence should be?" Phyllis asked. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- . ... "Punishment to fit the crime. Infinite pity." 1" But you did not tell me how his story ended," she said. "Pedestals," I said, "are unsafe—particularly the self-erected ones."</p>
        <p>(To be continued.) (These articles originally appeared in ths "London Echo.")</p>
        <p>HAVE VOIJ CATARRH 2 EVERY FOUTH PERSON YOU MEET HAS IT. AN INSIDIOUS AND DANGEROUS DISEASE. WORSE THAN BRONCHITIS. Cattarrh commences frequently by running at the nose; sets up inflammation of the mucus membrane of the nose and throat, followed by a stopped up feeling in the nose and dropping of mucus in the throat. It partially stops up the air vesicles of the lungs, inflames the bronchial surface, sets up catarrhal bronchitis or catarrhal asthma, accompanied by a dry or loose cough, difficulty in breathing, with unpleasant hawking and expectorating. The mucus thus secreted works its way down the alimentary canal to the stomach, catarrh of the stomach and intestines following. It then becomes a persistent constitutional disease, both dangerous and disagreeable, leaving the patient very susceptible to cold, and every additional cold aggravates the symptoms until it affects the hearing and gradually the sense of smell and taste. VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is the most efficient remedy for this disagreeable complaint, it acts directly upon the mucus surface, relieves the irritation, clears the bronchial tubes and air vesicles and relieves the stopped up feeling in the nose. It is infinitely superior to any local treatment. It acts constitutionally, clears catarrh thoroughly out of the system; you feel better the first ; day; you begin to breathe freely through the nose and rest comfortably at night. Ask for VENO'S LIGHTNING Couou CURE, 91d. Is. 11d. and 2s. 9d., at Chemist everywhere.</p>
        <p>Warrington Sailing Club. I The annual race in connection with the above club was held on Saturday, over a course from Widnes, round a buoy moored near Walton Arches, to the west end of Thendis's wall, Bank Quay. There were seven entrances for the race and the boats were handicapped half a minute per foot and the Pride one minute. The competing boats were :-1, Emmie; 14ft., captain, J. Gleave ; 2, Valeta, Sherratt ; 3, Peggy, 18ft., G. H. Brown; 4, Spray, 211 t., W. Rippen ; 5, Wildflower, 22 ft., H. Warburton ; 6, Gipsy, 301 t. J. Devison•, 7, Pride, 26 ft., W. Warburton, Jun. The starter was Mr. 'l'. White ' • the judge, Mr, J. Hatton ; the treasurer, Mr. W. W j arburton, un. ; the commodore, Mr. H. Warbuiston ; the vice-commodore, Mr. J. Devison ; and the secretary, Mr. G. H. Brown. The boats came in in the following order : 1, Pride ; 2, Gipsy ; 3, Wildflower ;4, Em mie ; 5, Peggy ; 6, Spray ; and 7, Valeta. Money prizes were given, the first prize being £2 10s, and a pipe and ease. 00 yards separated the second boats at the finish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>WESLEYAN . FOREIGN MISSIONARY ANNIVERSARY.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN . FOREIGN MISSIONARY ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>Mission Work in South Africa.</p>
        <p>A public meeting was held in Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Good Friday evening in connection with the foreign Missionary Anniversary. The principal features of the meeting were addresses which were delivered by the Revs. M. Limon and M. F. Crewdson,on the progress of missionary work in South Africa. both having for a number of years laboured on behalf of the Wesleyan body in that cbuntry. Mr. J. Upson presided over a good attendance and was supported by the Revs. G. H. Camson, M. Wilkin Rees, and J. Manning._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>SATISFACTORY ANNUAL REPO-RT.</head>
        <p>SATISFACTORY ANNUAL REPO-RT.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. WILKINS REES, in presenting the report, I stated that the total amount—£277 Os. 4d.—sent from the Warrington circuit during 1901 was by far the largest yearly contribution made by the circuit. From £232, the sum raised in 1900, the Foreign Missionary income had steadily risen to the amount as at present returned. Had there not' been several serious and I unavoidable losses the increase would have been very I much greater. The amount from Bold-street was £47 2s. Bd., an increase of £3 17s. Gd. The juvenile association of Bold-street Church, the interests of which were well looked after by Mr. George Ashton, raised £25 10s.' lid., of which one-third was devoted to foreign missions. From Bewsey-road, £45 Is. Gd., an increase of £2 155., had I been contributed; from Sankey Bridges, £9 9s. 6d., a slight increase ; Latchford, £l3 Bs. 6d., increase £3 Gs. 6d.; Ellesmere-street, £3 18s.,slight increase; Lymm, £l4 10s., decrease 16s. ; Penketh, £45 Os. 6d., decrease of five guineas (last year, however, Penketh showed an increase of about £11) ; Moore, £22 4s. id., increase, £4 7s. 6d.; Whitley, £ll 19s. 4d., increase £1 14s. 3d. ; Antrobus, £l2 Gs. ld., decrease 13s. 2d.; Appleton, £4 10s. ld., decrease £2 10s. 3d.; Padgate, £37 15s. 10d., in- crease £5 15s. 7d. ; Martinscroft, £1 Os. 3d., slight in- crease; Stockton Heath, £G 35., decrease £2 25., largely caused by a deficit at the public meeting. Mr. Rees ' stated that a fair amount was also sent from the cir- cuit to the Women's Foreign Missionary Auxiliary. Mr. Rees added that he was unable this year to supply any report with reference to the work in the general mission field, as the great meeting in London had not yet been held, but financially they were on the right side. (Applause.) Mr. J. UPSON, who had a hearty reception, said he was not present for the purpose of apologiziug for Foreign Missions. Ever since the Master said," Go into the world and preach the Gospel to every creature," the man who apologized for missions was wasting his time, to say the least of it. Nor was it necessary to justify the cause as it justified itself. The work of their missions was never so successful as it was at the present time, though it seemed to him that the greatest difficulty missionaries met with, especially in breaking new ground,was that they had no great body of believers to point to whose lives had been 'brightened by the doctrines they professed. They had no "living epistles," whose lives proclaimed the Gospel of Christ, and he conteaded that the fullest success would only come to missionary labour when not only did we ourselves at home live the lives of Christian men and women, not only when our own lives and hearts were right, but the fullest success would only come when our national policy was guided by mercy, righteousness and truth (Applause.) The Rev. MARSHALL LIMON, in an interesting address, dwelt upon his work among the Kaflirs in South Africa, At the outset he strongly denied the asserton that a Kaffir was a soulless and brainless animal. He had seen the Kaffir boy at school with the white boy, ' and he could assure them that the whit e boy had to keep a ' sharp look out if he kept pace with the Kaffir. The native children were intensely interested in their lessons, and he had seen them during the half-hour given for recreation running about the play ground in a circle hand in hand, singing the lessons they had just been taught. (Hear, hear, and applause). ' Mr. Limos; proceeded 4to quote incidents in the mission work, showing how converted Kaffirs, without any prompting, took upon themselves the mission work among members of their own race. The Rev. M. F. CREWDSON said that to appreciate theadvance of Mission work in South Africa we must understand the conditions which obtained. The beauti- ful climate had made the South Africans a playing people and had undermined the strenuousness and grit of character and taken away the splendid feel- ing that duty must be done no matter what happened. We could say whatever we liked about the gold mines in South Africa, but to him they afforded a splendid opportunity for the evangelization of the natives who worked in the mines in their thousands. In Johannesburg, where he had mainly: worked, there were, however, many difficulties in the way of the immediate success of Christian influences Every seventh man was a Jew and he prayed that in England we might never know of the corrosive influence of a Jew population an influence which eats its way in- to the very vitals of religion. For the success of our missions it was necessary that the whiteman should predominate and in Johannesburg the whitenaan was losing caste on account of the degrading occupations followed by the lower members of the Jewish com- munity, and it was necessary we should take up in earnest the work of stamping out the evil Jewish influence. We had, however, not grit and strength enough to carry our missionary operations amongst the Jews of Johannesburg, and he blushed to see that in that town there was not a single church which was doing one stroke of work for the Christianization of that race. But there was much promise in South Africa. South Africa was the most highly mineralized portion of the world's surface. and to him that meant a cosmo- politan population. Men of ambition, men of strong will, men of confidence in themselves would all flock there, and if they could only convert those men and win them over for Christ it would mean much. (Applause). The usual votes of thanks concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>The police had a thoroughly busy time at Eastertide. Not only wore elaborate traps preparei for the unwary motorist who, tempted by a stretch of down-grade, was letting his car " rip," but ho was also held up at frequent intervals on the main roads in order that the constables on duty might be duly satisfied that he was carrying his driving license, in accordance with the terms of the Motor Car Act.</p>
        <p>We do not consider that this latest move on the part of the police force is at all an unreasonable one. There is little doubt that a good many persons are in charge of a car who have not complied with the demands of the Act with reference to taking out a driving license, aad, in the event of an accident arising, it would be extremely difficult to trace the offender. The license, as we all know, is not a proof of efficiency or capability, but it does ensure a certain amount of protection to others, insomuch as it renders comparatively easy the identification of a calpable motorist, and the palice do well to show activity in this respect.</p>
        <p>Another matter to which they might well direct their attention is in connection with the brakes. The Act requires that every motor car shall be provided with two independent brakes; but, more often than not, cars are taken out with one or two of these brakes missing. It is the trade trial cars that are the worst offenders in this respect, and, if they were to be suddenly hauled up, it would frequently be found that in this matter the ruling of the Act had been .temporarily ignored. Unfortunately, tho polic,a are not sufficiently conversant with the mechanism of the cars to know whethe” the brakes are on or nOt, and a clever driver, so stopped, could easily hoodwink them by careful manipulation of the timing lever.</p>
        <p>Probably the reason why so many of the trial cars are so frequently seen out of adjustment lies in the fact that each one is driven, at various times, by three or four different drivers. Hence, when anything goes wt ong, the natural impulse of the man who has little interest in his work is to get back to the depot as best he can and leave the trouble of the adjustments or repairs to the next man who takes the car out. This one speedily finds that matters are wrong, but argues that he is not going to put things right for others, so I he, too, manages as best he can and trundles the car I out and back again by a series of makeshift devices. A I practical foreman wouldspeedily put a stop to such manceuvring; but, unfortunately,many of the men who I hold this responsible position are not practical, and so in this way business is lost, cars are spoilt and the reputation of the firm is ruined solely through the laziness and the incapacity of its employees.</p>
        <p>II We botice in the" Autocar "an editorial note .asking I what happens when the judges, retained by the Motor Car Co (1905) Ltd. for the benefit of their clients sug- gest different cars? To an expert, who has before him a plain and complete specification of a client's need together with the price limit he is prepared to pay, the nature of the country over which be proposes to use the car, and so on, the choice is not really a .particularly I wide one. For instance, suppose the enquirer did not I wish to pay more than £l5O for a two-seated car, to be. I used in a hilly district, and to be so simple in the matter of its mechanism that he would be able to look after it himself, the field of selection is limited. We , take it that the judges would review, as it were in their mind's eye, all the vehicles listed at this price, and would then delete those unsuitable for hilly roads. Accessibility and simplicity would probably be the next consideration' and by this time the list would be considerably reduced. Finally, as each judge gives his special reasons for the selection of the cars he suggests I when more than one make is then recommended to the ' customer we couclude,as the signed reports are forward- ed to him, that it rests with him to weigh the reasons and make the final choice himself. This would possibly be based on some quite minor Aetna such as carriage work, date of delivery, or some other practically unim- portant point.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ELECTORAL ANOMALIES.</head>
        <p>ELECTORAL ANOMALIES.</p>
        <p>Since the last Redistribution Act the electorate of the kingdom has increased from 5.707,531 in 1885-G to 7,191,974 in 1905. Lancashire and Cheshire have fully shared in this growth,as will be seen by the comparative figures which we are able to place before our readers :</p>
        <p>THE ELECTORATE 01` LANCASHIRE. Average Electors, per member. 1885-6 1892 1895 1900 1905</p>
        <p>In the kingdom as a whole the average electorate per member has advanced from 8,518 to 10,739, but in Lancashire it has increased from 9,342 t 3 12,486. It will be interesting to compare the electorate of the county with other great groups of constituencies :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>FIVE GROUPS OF CONSTITUENCIES.</head>
