        <p>Electors. Electors. Average. Seats. 1885. 1905. 1905. Scotland 72 574.358 735,764 10,219 Ireland 103 741,913 696,932 6,766 Londonßor's 62 507,881 683,081 11,017 Lancashire 57 532,511 711,732 12,486 Yorkshire The average number of electors per member in Lanca- shire and Yorkshire is much above the average for the Kingdom, and nearly double to Ireland. We next pro- ceed to give the comparative figures for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>LANCASHIRE- BOROUGHS</head>
        <p>THE LANCASHIRE- BOROUGHS</p>
        <p>No. of Electors Electors Present Seats. 1885. 1905. Average. Liverpool Manchester Salford Oldham 2 25,600 32,439 16,219 Blackburn ... 2 16,329 20,887 10,443 Bolton Preston , In the above list of boroughs returning more than one ! member the chief anomaly is the inadequate representa- tion of Oldham, which though possessed of a larger electorate than Salford, has one member less. Newcastle-on-Tyne, the City of London, and -Oldham are the greatest two-member constituencies of the' Kingdom, and are in striking contrast with Bath, which has two members for 7,807 electors. It may be noted ! that although Liverpool has two members more than Glasgow or Birmingham, its electorate is several thousand less. The city could spare one of its members to supply the deficiency of Oldham, and would still retain more than its shade of members. The remaining boroughs of Lancashire return one member each. They are as follows :</p>
      </div>
