        <p>manifest that this group of constituencies is entitled to a substantial increase of representation. We next give the . COUNTY DIVISIONS WITH FEWER TII iN 15,000 If ELEcTorts.—Southport 1885 8,437-1905 14,557, Middleton 11,718-14,186, Lancaster 8,961-13,948, Newton 9,344-13,632 Leigh 8,572-13,262, Chorley 9,881-13,012, Radcliffe-cum-Farnworth 10,433 12,987, Ince 91,57-12,828, Ormskirk 8,714-12427 Rossendale 11,450-12732, Widnes 8223-10,553, Heywood 9,269-10,460, and 9,780. Lancashire were represented upon the basis of its electorate it would have 60 members instead of 57 as at present, and it is evident that a large proportion of the increase would fall to the county divisions. We next turn to</p>
        <p>TO THE ALTAR BY TRAM. A remarkable wedding took place on Wednesday at Christ Church, Blackpool. Mr. Frank Williams, head- master of the private school at Cleveleys, was married to Miss Grace Cohen, niece of Mr. L. Cohen, of Norbreok and Blackpool. The bridal party and the guests travelled from Cleveleys to Blackpool by tram, three of the large Fleetwood cars being used for the pi</p>
        <p>lurpose.</p>
        <p>_ . A report of a remarkable cycling fatality comes from Ireland. Sergeant Bell, of the Royal Irish Constabu- lary, left Newtownstewart on a bicycle at an early hour on Tuesday to visit some friends in county Antrim. About three o'clock he was going down a steep hill at Crannog, and was thrown against a wall and precipitated into a river, where his body was found a few hours afterwards.</p>
