      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>TILE HOMES AND HAUNTS OF SIR WALTER SCOTT,</head>
        <p>TILE HOMES AND HAUNTS OF SIR WALTER SCOTT,</p>
        <p>CALL Riamexs RESERVED.I</p>
        <p>THE RT. HON. SIR HERBERT MAXWELL, BART., M.P. (Author of " Memories of the Months," to.).</p>
        <p>There have been many greater poets than 'Walter Scott, and certain critics of our own day speak with scant respect of his romances— "writing impromptu novels to buy farms with" is the phrase in which one of them summarizes Scott's life-work. Nevertheless, no man ever poked pen into inkhorn with such en- during effect upon the fortunes of his country- men. Practically, he discovered Scotland for people south of the Tweed and west of the Atlantic; who, had they written henceforth the name of that country with a double t, would merely have paid fitting tribute to their guide, seeing that in good sooth Scotland has become to them chiefly Scott's land—the land that Scott has given them. But it was far more than mere peneraft that prevailed to weave Scott'i memory into such close association with certain scenes in his native country—with the Borderland, for instance, of which it is not too much to say that no man can apprehend the spirit who has not drunk deeply of Scott's lore. Washington Irving has left a faithful picture of what that region would still be to the visitor, had the Lay of the Last Minstrel and Guy Mannering never been written. When he visited Abbotsford and Scott took him out to view Ettrick and Teviotdale, Torwoodlee and the Lammermuirs, Irving , could scarcely believe that 'his eyes really rested on the scenes whereof he had read—eyes accustomed to landscape on the grander scale of the New World "I gazed about me for a time with mute surprise, I may almost say with disappointment. I beheld a mere succession of grey, waving hills, line beyond line, as far as the eye could reach, monotonous in their aspect, and so destitute of trees that one could almost see a stout fly walking along their outline ; and the far-famed Tweed appeared a naked stream, between bare hills, without a tree or thicket on its banks. And yet, such had been the magic web of poetry and romance thrown over the whole, that it had a greater charm for me than the richest scenery I had ever beheld in England."</p>
        <p>THE SF.CRET OF SCOTT'S INFLCENCE.</p>
        <p>What is it that has stamped Scott—Scott— Scott upon all the Merse and Borderland ? First, it was the man. "It • can be said of Scott," wrote Thomas Carlyle, by no means an Uncritical admirer of his genius, " when he de- parted he took a man's life along with him. No sounder piece of British manhood was put together in that eighteenth century of time." Genius is a selfish power ; it is wont to con- centrate all the energy of a man upon some far- off goal; seldom but it drains the channels of neighbourly sympathy and routine of friend- ship. Men do not easily lose the impression made by that combination of warm heart and towering intellect,. which may be encountered scarcely, once in a generation. Second, it was the writer. Scott wrote not at all till he was ripe. He was thirty four when his first original poem—The Lay—was published : of his contemporaries, Byron died at thirty six, Shelley at thirty two, Keats ut six•and- twenty. Waverley was not written till its author Was forty-three, an age at which nine novelists • out of ten have written themselves a-dry. During all his youth and well into middle age, Scott had been accumulating historic lore, not for any conscious purpose, but out of sheer love of the Matter. " Out of the fulness of the heart the mouth speaketh." When the time caine for him to utter, Scott wrote with an abundance of knowledge that held his readers spell 'bound. Ile was, in the best sense of the word, an" eye- Opener ; " he showed his fellows what lay. hitherto unsuspected, behind and beneath the plain aspect of every-day scenes ; and it is in unconscious gratitude for that service that People still flock yearly in tens of thousands to the places hallowed by the memory of Scott, eager to set their feet where he once stood, and to see for themselves the landscapes upon which, I he gazed.</p>
        <p>HIS BIRTHPLACE AND YOUTH.</p>
        <p>The house in which Scott was born in 1771 no longer exists, having been demolished long before the most prescient of town councillors could have foreseen that it would ever possess the slightest interest for anybody. It stood at the end of the College Wynd, Edinburgh, just opposite the main entrance to the University, end was pulled down towards the close of the eighteenth ceptury in order to make way for a new street. Zdiuburgh Old Town can never be otherwise than picturesque, by reason of the nature of the ground whereon it stands ; but the Rand of the sanitary reformer has sorely changed its aspect. The eye that rests on tile insCription at No. 8 Chambers Street—" Near this spot stood the house in which Sir Walter Scott was horn "—can trace little else in the neighbourhood which was there a hundred years *go.</p>
        <p>- - Walter was a sickly child and suffered from lup disease, which lamed him for life. The aged poet Home—the same who in his youth had owed to his cat like activity Ins escape from a Jacobite prison in. Donne ' Castle—pitied the little cripple. "I quite grieve," wrote he, for that poor little fellow with the withered limb. What a painful sight to his anxious Parents' to witness a loved one so suddenly doomed to a life of inertness and mortification ! It will never be known how much the world awes to that misfortune, for it was the cause of the child being sent for country air to his grandfather's farm of Sandyknowe, close to the lonely tower of Smailholm, in sight of which the old. cow-bailie, Sandy Ormiston, unfolded to him the endless chronicle of border warfare and foray. It was the cause, also, of the boy, aa he grew stronger, being sent to Kelso Gram- Mar School ; of his spending the holidays with is bachelor uncle, Captain Robert Scott, who lived at Rosebank, mid who encouraged Walter to make himself familiar with all the historic ground—the cradle of his own elan-I;Torham Castle, Otterburn, Flodden field, Melrose and ,the rest—all the places he was to make so famous by his pen.</p>
        <p>HIS RESIDENCE IN . EDI:CRT:ROIL</p>
        <p>Though be still halted in his gait, Walter Was a healthy lad when. about 1782, he returned ....to Edinburgh to .• finish his education at the nigh School mid University. Scott chose his VZ•C home after encountering the one great aulsappointment in his life. For six years he bad cherished a deep passion for Miss Wiiliamina Belsches (afterwards Lady Stuart Forbes) ; When he declared it in 1790 she would none of him, and married another in January 1797. Scott, by this time an Edinburgh barrister, consoled himself by marrying Marguerite Charpentier in the following December, and ade his winter abode in South Castle Street, summer residence being Lasswade Cottage, aocut seven miles south-east of the city. Lass- Ivade Cottage is a quaint structure, which had 10a. g since been pulled down or remodelled, and ,"4 Peaked roof of thatch replaced by the hum- but necessary, slate, but for the praise- Worthy reference with which its present owner Illas • Preserved unaltered, what was Scott's home icr six of the happiest years of his life: Here 410 Made his first essay in poetry ; near at hand WaS the Lothian Esk, unpolluted in those days bj the refuse of industry which at the present ,p7e so sadly disfigures the fair stream, and .„,41ding its course among scenes endefired to the citing poet. " Who knows not Melville's beechy grove And 'login's rocky glen, Ualkeith, which all the virtues love, And classic Hawthornden 7" .15 t although Scott declared that "no stream 11 Scotland can boast such a varied succession Itg the most interesting objects, as well as of 0,6 most romantic and beautiful scenery " &amp;k, ne never forgot hi. own Border land, 1717 ear after year he used to spend his vacation desolate Liddesdale or the lonely vale of r„Eirrw, passing from one to another of the at'ess strongholds of the riders of yore. lor gently collecting the fragments of ballad e 9,. Which he gathered from the lips of wie,guerds and fanners, and published iu 1801, delightfu notes, at the Minstrelsy of the ?ttisil Border. Never was there a more laud- :Yie exercise of patronage than when the Duke " Bu ccleueh obtained for Walter Scott in 1799 ZPolnttneni of Sheriff at Selkirk. Hencefor- hrna duty and inclination combined to make '43 ePend his summers among the wethdoved</p>
        <p>II LI i in i s rttam, unn 81111 1 ItITOW. Lass- 'trade Cot rage was given. up and for eight years- -1804 to 181t2—be rented Ashestiel, a modernised border tower on Tweedside, about seven miles from Selkirk. It was here that his best poems were composect, and provect so remunerative that, in 1812, , Scott was able to carry into effect the dream of his life, by becoming the owner of some of the land with which his clan had been associated for so many centuries. He purchased Abbotsford, an unprofitable and vexatious investment, as it proved; but, by a strange freak of fortune, the immediate cause of both fame and fortune; for it was while pack- ing books and papets for removal to Abbotsford that he came across the unfinished manuscript of " Waverley," which he had thrown aside years before, because of the unfavourable opinion of a friend,</p>
        <p>SCOTT'S INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>Besides his office of Sheriff, Scott was a clerk of the Court of Session, which rendered his presence necessary in Edinburgh during the sitting of the Courts. His town house was 89 North Castle Street, a spot which must ever hold the attention of his admirers, because, if he went far afield for the material of his romances, this was the workshop in which so much of that material was wrought into literature. His study window was commanded by the back windows of a house in George Street, and Lock- hart has a curious anecdote arising out of this circumstance. A party of young men were dining together in George Street one summer evening in 1814; Lockhart was of the party, and as yet knew not Scott, though he was to become his son-in-law six years later. Noticing that his host seemed restless and worried, Lock- hart asked him if he felt unwell. _ _</p>
        <p>"No," he answered, "I shall be well enough presently, if you will only let me sit where you are, and take my chair. There is a confounded hand in sight of me here, which won't let me -El my glass with a -good will." " I rose," says Lockhart, "to change places with him, and he pointed out to me this hand, which, like the writing on Belshazzar's wall, disturbed his hour of hilarity. Since we sat down,' he said, I have been watching it--it fascinates my eye—it never stops—page after page is finished and thrown on that heap of manuscript, and still it goes on unwearied, and so it will be till candles are brought in and God knows how long after that. It is the same every night—l can't stand a sight of it when am not at my books." Some stupid, dogged, engrossing clerk, probably,' I exclaimed. No, boys,' said our host; I well know what hand it is—'tis Walter Scott's.' This was the hand," Adds Lockhart, " that, in the evenings of three summer weeks, wrote the two last volumes of Waverley.' " One loves to linger about that solid stone house in the northern street, for it is associated more than any other with Scott's great prose romances ; and it has been well said by Sir Leslie Stephen that, albeit Scott won his reputa- tion as a poet, his " poetical faculty may be more felt in his prose than his verse."</p>
        <p>HIS DARLING HOBBY-ABBOTSFORD.</p>
        <p>With Abbotsford one would fain speak ten- derly, for it stands in the dear Border land, and Scott'd whole affection was poured upon it; yet if it was not a failure, it was an egregious foible. " Perhaps the most incongruous pile that gentle- manly modernism ever designed" is . Ruskin's verdict ; yet that great critic s entire devotion to the designer led him to write many pages in ,describing the homelier Ashestiel. Abbotsford was a hobby, an elaborate freak; a laborious attempt at antiquity which *never deceived any- body, except perhaps Scott himself; "a place to see once, but never again," sighed sympathetic Dean Stanley. Spurious Gothic and plaster painted to resemble precious carving offend a cultivated taste, and one is depressed by the remembrance that, before the house was finished, its architect was overwhelmed in financial ruin. Still, the heroic struggle that followed must never be forgotten. When Scott died in 1832, the mountain of debt—M3o,ooo—M whioh he had been involved by the collapse of James Ballantyne and Co. had been reduced to £54,- 000 ; insurance and copyrights proved sufficient to clear off the whole of what remained. One more scene before the last. A few months before his death, Scott visited Douglas Castle to mark the scenery for his last and feeblest romance—' Castle Dangerous.' Lockhart was with him, and has described how the aged poet gazed silently awhile at the lands- cape, lying dark under a thundrous summer sky. Memories of old times crowded thickly upon him ; then striking his stick in the sod, his eyes dimmed with tears, he walked slowly away, re- peating the words of the dying Douglas at Otterburn. "My wound is deep—l fain would sleep , Take hou the vanguard of the three And hide me by the bracken-bush That grows on yonder lilye lea. Oh, bury me by the bracken bush, Beside the blooming brier, And never let living mortal km, . . . . .</p>
        <p>That e'er a kin* Scot lies here." On 26th. September 1832 a .ombre, eiler procession wound its way from Abbotsford, through Melrose across Leaderfoot, and so to the crest of Flemersyde Hill, where it halted a- while ; for here "the Shirr&amp; " used always to draw rein to enjoy the prospect of the winding Tweed, cloven Eildon, distant Rubislaw, and all that broad landscape, wherecf each feature told its own tale to him. After a short pause, the solemn march was resumed; the mourners descended the slopes to the plain which bears the ancient abbey of Dryburgh, of which Scott himself had sung— " Tweed loves it well, and turns aghin As loath to leave the sweet domain."</p>
        <p>Here they laid the true Wizard of the North in St. Mary's aisle, within sound of the most historic of Scottish rivers; and, albeit it may be no concern to the departed where their ashes be laid, surely those who cherished the memory of Scott could have found no fitter resting place for his mortal part.</p>
      </div>
