      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>DIAGRAM 92.</head>
        <p>DIAGRAM 92.</p>
        <p>ram</p>
        <p>IA running-side slow-screw in-off.—Red ball 8 inches from the side cushion and one inch from the top cushion. Cue ball 7 inches from the sid( cushion and 11 inche4 from the top cushion.</p>
        <p>Diagram 92 shows position for an easy in-ofl from the red into the left top pocket. This in. off (with the balls accurately placed to the measurements given under the diagram) is. how- ever, just not on by a plain half-ball stroke ei medium strength, and the ordinary player gener- ally plays such an in-off by means of a forcing stroke driving the red in and -out of baulk Played in this way, unles,s good direction is given he object ball, it May easily run safe. Better class players play this stroke by a slow screw keeping the object ball well out of baulk ' when the second object ball is in some bad position, and by driving the first ball through baulk- to the vicinity of the second ball, com- mand may be had over both again. The slow screw shown on Diagram 92 is quite an easy stroke owing to the red being so near the pocket and the cue ball being so short a distance from the red. Many players Always use cheek side for such a stroke, being under the impression that all screw in-offs from a ball near the cushion require check side. Such a rule holds good when- ever the object ball besides being near the cushier is also some considerable distance from th( pocket, for the pocket is then a blind one, and the cue ball after contact with the object ball haf to either run along or very close to the cushion and therefore requires the assistance of check side to take it into .the pocket off the opposing angle. In the slow screw in-off shown on Diagrarc 92 there is reallyino opposing angle, for the CUE ball cannot run along the cushion to the pocket With the ebject ball one inch from the cushion, the cue ball at the moment of contaci must be about 3 inches from the cushion, so that the short distance it. has to travel is not to very blind pocket. Should it be desired to play the in- off quite slowly, running side should be used instead of check side, for, as already explained screw strokes can be made with less strength when running side is employed than when cheek side is used. In the shot under discussion, ever though the ball touch the upper angle instead ol going straight into the pocket, running side will most likely carry it into the pocket, owing to this side being pocket side off the upper angle. _ .</p>
        <p>- Were both balls a few inches farther away from the pocket than as shown on Diagram 92, but stir forming about the 'same angle with the poeket, the pocket would be a blind one, and check side would have to be used in order to take the ball in off the opposing, angle.</p>
        <p>A screw-back into a very open pocket.—Red ball :1 inches from the top cushion and in a line with the pyramid spot and the centre pocket. Cue ball folio inches from the top cushion and 71 inches from the side cushion. This in-off should be played without any side.</p>
        <p>Diagram 93 shows a screw-back into a top pccket. The stroke is by no means a difficult one for any fair player. Most players, however. use side as well as screw for this particular stroke. But when the pocket is exceptionally</p>
