        <p>obvious that a high rate of interest is not the only criterion of efficient stewardship, and that it is of primary importance that there should be no doubt as to the securities themselves. Some point was made as to the terms of a resolution dealing with the powers of the trustees on making investments for the district, but I am not satisfied but that the minute referred to was correctly entered and no investment could be lawfully made by the trustees except in accordance with the registered rule (No. 4) wkich only gives the trustees power to invest in the securities named in the rule under the direction of the district committee, The district has 28 members on their committee. No evidence was tendered by them at the inquiry. Apart from the difficulty of identi- fying the particular investments of the district owing to joining together of the funds of the three different bodies, the present position has arisen from insufficient Icare in complying with the rules dealing with the posi- tion of the trustees and their powers of investment. It appeared that the district as a registered branch had no legal adviser. The trustees on their own authority consulted Mr. Roberts from time to time as to the investments, and, unfortunately, they omitted to pro- duce to him a copy of the rules or to acquaint him with the fact of the district being registered. This was a serious omission on their part. I need not refer speci- 1 cially to those investments which were ultra vires. "This inquiry will bring to the notice of the com- mittee the necessity of seeing that the rule (No. 4) is complied with and that the securities repre- senting the funds of the district are such as to come within the limits therein contained. Recently personal feeling appears to have entered into the management of the society. It is unnecessary for me to point out that in business matters conducted under recognized rules, this element should not be allowed to enter. Whichever party may be in the wrong, there is the 'gal remedy capable in the case of a registered society of enforcement. The cross-examination of one of the witnesses suggested s desire on the part of that officer to sever the district from the provisions of the Friendly Societies Act. This would be a policy of retro- gression, which it may be hoped that the committee will not adopt, but that they will rather point out to the members the advantage of registration, the freedom they have under it, and the protection in the case of any infringement of the rules. Under Section 76 of the Friendly Societies Act, as Inspector, lam required to examine and report on the affairs of the society or branch in question. I, therefore, submit the above report to you. lam not empowered under the terms of the section to suggest any recommendations. It is for the committee in the interests of the members to act upon the position disclosed as they may be advised.</p>
