acter for the sake of the revenue and , ! allusion whatever as to what opposition there might be, the votes which they derive from it It is surely but simply asked him, would he support a abour criminal that a small army of brewers should be exalted L at the awful cost of the impoverishment anddebasement candidate, nothing more, nothing less, of millions of their fellow creatures ! Fair play calls Putting the question in the form he has, it appears quite plain to rue, that there must be no Liberal out with a stentorian voice for the removal of this candidate in the field at all ere he supported Labour and national blighter and its unworthy authors in the interest of the reputation and welfare of the country. even then, the Labour man must be" honest" and bind e Nun. I Were the hundred and eighty • million now spent himself "to support certain measures." Well qualm "you may have him "honest " enough, but if every year on intoxicating drinks devoted to the legiti- you want a Labour candidate to support measures that I mate purposes of life what a change for the better would I soon take place. Foreigners would no longer regard us different individuals would demand of him at election i as a drunken people and the polluters of the heathen. times I am afraid you would haveto get one specially i And where rags, filth and destitution now prevail there -made to cope with so many different opinions and so i I would be*clemer dwellings and happier conditions of varying a programme. To his question." If a Liberal candidate is in the i life generally, and all the features of Society would be ! field for Warrington, and there is no Labour candidate so improved that men would wonder why they had so if not, why long allowed the government and the brewers to injure will he support the Liberal candidate, ' and disgrace the country. A government, strong in the not ?" My reply, under the conditions he names, is this, power of right, backed up with the vast volume of I would support the