y, so I will keep to what I think the most important point. In one of his remarks he says : "He cannot mean that it is to be a continual fight between capital and labour." I ask him, has it ever been anything else under our present system of industry ? The capitalists became so cppressive that labour had to form his unions to protect himself as far as possible from the unfair methods of those capitalists, and only by so doing was he enabled to keep up his standard of living. What has happened to the worker who has not this unity behind him? You usually find him working for a bare living wage, in fact, in many instances von cannot call it a livin,g wage, men earning 16, Is, or 20 shillings per week, with wives, families and homes to keep going; men whose lives are a continuous struggle to eep body and soul together. A week's sick- ness or out of work and they are in debt, it is such men as these, and even worse, that the Labour Party is desirous of helping. The Trades Unionist generally is at the better end of labour. It is the workers who are forced to live in narrow, dirty slums, ill-fed, ill-clothed; it is for the little children born under such conditions that I wish you to think of. We talk about the Chinese slaves in' South Africa, but let us cast a look on our white slaves at home. We hear of Home Rule for Ireland, let us have a little more Homo Rule in England. Any man " Nunqualm " if he has any grit in him, or love foe his fellows and sees this poverty all around him would rise in indignation against the present system of government and would demand, yes demand, social. reforms such as the Labour Party are working for, but when I see the utter indifference of those in comfortable circumstances towards the pooreehelpless class, it leads me to ask myself, is our present Christianity true? Socialism, if I have studied it rightly, is not to drag anyone down but it is an attempt to lift up those that are already down to a better and higher state of living and I wish them, God speed i i their good work. No," Nunqnahn" I do not look for" life as it should be through Trades Union windows," but I do look for it through the windows of common sense, love, and justice. We know too that there are good capitalists, hut alas, they are too few and are often forced into doing that which, under fairer condtions they would not do, but they are bound to keep up with competition created by men of the unscrupulous Penrhyn type,or go under entirely, and so their good intentions are crippled and become of little or no benefit to the workers. So long as the c3pitalist Class have their Employers Parliamentary Committee (composed of Liberals and Conservatives) pledged to oppose labsur reforms in the House and have their festers' Associations to oppose the workers outside the House, then I maintain that the worker his equal rights to form his Parliamentary party and his trade unions to protect and further his interests and only through them will he receive his due rights. You may say " God forbid" if you like " Nunquelm " but I don't think the worker need fear his God in this matter.—Yours, ac., April 25th, 1905.</p>
        <p>lONA.</p>
        <p>April, 1905.</p>
        <p>" FAIRPLAY."</p>
        <p>A Sure Remedy for Poverty. Sir,—The clamour which is now going on among the politicians as to Free Trade and tariffs as remedies for poverty is entirely misleading. Fiscal arrangements may increase and 'lntensify the disadvantages under which the working classes suffer, but they can neither remove nor even mitigate them. The history of the Corn Laws and the present condition of the industrial world conclusively Drove this. There is, however, one and only one way whereby the monopoly of wealth may be broken up, and a more equitable dittribntion made lor the benefit of all concerned. That remedy is at our doors, is easy of application, and so simple that the poorest mental capacity can grasp it at once, and its name is Fair Play. Now it gives me unfeigned satis- faction to record my belief that fair play is better understood and more applied by the great body of the English people than by any other civilized nation. But at the same time the unwelcome truth must not be con- cealed that not a few of us are true Episcopalians, inasmuch "we have left undone those things which we ought to have clone; and we have done those things which we ought not to have done" (General Confession). A few instances in point most suffice to-day. My first instance is the land laws. The land, like the I air, the sunshine, and the water, is thecommon property of all God's creatures, and was never intended to be j monopolised by any man or body of men for his or ' their special benefit. How unjust, how cruel then it is to allow a mere handful of men to lay hold, by force of law, of the greater part of the soil and minerals of England, and deal with them as if they , were their own property, although they have I really neither human nor divine rights to 11 them. But this huge wrong does not end with the I appropriation of the property, but is supplemented by I a still more enormous one—the levying and enforce- ment of black-mail from all who till their native earth 1 and use the ores beneath it. The amount of revenue thus brought into the coffers of the aristocratic idlers is, probably, not less than fifty millions a year, and to this extent tenant farmers, miners and labourers are deprived of the just reward of their industry. With these facts before us we need not be surprised that English agriculture is not as satisfactory as it might be, that farmers are oppressed, food dear, and the peasantry often in a worse plight than slaves. And what adds greatly to the ugliness of the conduct of the land monopolisers is the fact that the greater part of their enormous income is spirit, not at home among the people wholearned it, but in foreign capitals wnere "my lords and gentlemen," like prophets, are more honoured than in their own country. Is it possible to -ma^iner or even dream of a case where fair play is A a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, April 29, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, April 29, 1905.</p>
        <p>THE SEWER FATALITY.</p>
        <p>A Comparison in Letters.</p>
        <p>In response to a request for a subscription for the widow and family of the late James Baxter, the editor of the 'Review" received 10s. and .25 respectively from Messrs. It. Pierpoint, M.P., and A. H. Crostield, accom- panied by the following interesting letters—interesting because of the contrast in style.</p>
        <p>LETTER FROM MR. R. PIERPOINT, M.P. 8a Bickenhall Mansions Gloucester Place, London, W., April 19th, 1905.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir, lam sending you a postal order for the subscription which you are getting up in respect of the sad Sewer Fatality. You will probably think my subscription small. I can only say that everyone appears to think that the member for the Borough has a fund on which every one may draw.</p>
        <p>I am. Yours faithfully, ROBERT PIERPOINT. To the Editor of the "Warrington Review."</p>
        <p>LETTER FRONI MR. A. H. CROSFIELD. Hotel Belle Vne, Cannes, France, . _ . April 10th, 1905.</p>
        <p>The Editor of the "Review,"</p>
        <p>Warrington. Dear Sir,—ln accordance with my telegram the other day, I beg to enclose herewith .cheque for five pounds, which amount please include in your list of subscriptions for the widow and family of the late James Baxter, who lost his life in gallantly attempting to rescue the men who had been overcome by the poisonous gas accumu- lated in a sewer. It is indeed pleasing to me to see the response our townsfolk are making to your appeal, and I sincerely hope it may be the success it deserves. Yours faithfully, A. H. CROSPIELD.</p>
        <p>...--.4p..-.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. WILLIAM CART WRIGHT. .</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. WILLIAM CART WRIGHT. .</p>
        <p>We record with regret the death of Mr. William Cartwright of Moore whioh occurred at the Warrington Infirmary on Thursday of last week. It appears that on the 10th instant Mr. Cartwright, who was by trade a cabinet maker, was in his workshop when he thought he heard someone breaking glass in the room beneath the one in which he was working. He opened the door to look into the yard and in doing so dislodged a roll of linoleum, which struck him on the back and caused him to fall a distance of 25 feet. He sustained a compound fracture of the right leg and was at once conveyed to the Infirmary. The injured limb was amputated but lockjaw supervened and Mr. Cartwright passed away at the time stated. Mr. Cartwright, who was 41 years of age, was exceedingly well-known in Moore and War- rington. He was an enthusiastic member of the War- rington Field Club and frequently contributed to the debates of the Club. He was a competent naturalist and an authority on the management and keeping of bees, it being as an apiarist that he was most widely known. He was ever ready to give others the advantage of the knowledge which he possessed. The funeral took place at Daresbury Church on Mon- day,the large attendance at the graveside affoming excel- lent testimony to the respect in which he was held by all who knew him. Prior to the service at the grave- side the coffin was taken to the Moore Wesleyan Church, where deceased had from childhood been a constant worshiriper, and a brief memorial service was held, at which the Revs. John Gunnell, R. Wilkins Rees, and Joseph Manning officiated. A short address was delivered by the Rev. John Gunnell, who spoke of the high tone, purity, and estimable character of their deceased friend's life, of his lovableness and the hold he had on the affections of all who knew him, of his great abilities unfailingly well applied, and of the attractive modesty which was the characteristic of his life. At the conclusion of the service the boiy was conveyed to the church, where the burial service was read by the Vicar of Daresbury, the Rev. T. Lee Jones and the curate, the Rev. H. A. Lester. Many floral tributes were placed on the coffin, including wreaths from the con- gregation and school teachers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>INQUEST.</head>
        <p>THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>The inTilry into the accident was conducted by Mr. S. Brighouse at Warrington on Wednesday. The facts as above stated were related by Mrs. Cartwright, and a verdict of "Accidental death" was returned. The jury and coroner _both expressed their sympathy with Mrs. Cartwright.</p>
        <p>WORKS COMPETITION FINAL. Easy Victory for Fletcher's. The final for the Warrington Works Competition, under the Northern Union rules, was decided on Thurs- day evening before a fairly large crowd of spectators. Both of the competing teams—Fletcher's and Walker's Brewery—were at fulfstrength and a keen struggle was anticipated as each side had easily accounted for their opponents in the semi-final. The spectators were, however, disappointed as the match resulted in an easy. victory for Fletcher's who ran out winners by 19 points to nil. lathe competition Fletcher's havean excellent record having scored 95 points and not long had a single point recorded against them. The teams lined out as follows : HOUSES CONDEMNED. FLETCHERS.—CIegg, full back; Savage, Cartwright, The Health Committee has resolved that the Town Knight and Isherwood, three quarter backs; Fleming Clerk be instructed to take the necessary proceedings and Kay, halves; Boardman, Holbrook, Melton,Grainger under the Housing of the Working Classes Acts, 1800 and Walsh, forwards. to 1903. for the closing of the undermentioned houses all of which have been reported by the Medical 0 lice; WALKF,RS.—Brooks, full back ; Taylor, Miller, Hol- croft and Hay es, three quarters; Darden and Little- of Health to be unfit for human habitation .-6• , , Peers fair, halves ; Price, Howarth, Guest, Palmer and Malem, Yard, 1,3, and 5, Peers Court, 3, and 5, Wainwrights forwards. Yard, 4,6, 8, 10, and 12. Wainwrights Yard, 8. 14, and Fletcher's wan the toss and in the first half had the advantage of a strong wind. Flom the point of view 16, Pig Hill, 21, Barbauld-streat. of the spectators the start was not auspicious as A RE p:E3T TO MR. CARNEGIE. The Museum Committee has passed the following scrambling play followed the kickoff. Knight, however, resolution. That the Library Sub-Committee be put a different complexion on the game, scoring near the posts as the result of a good dribble. The kick at authorised to open