        <p>In a series of betting prosecutions at Grimsby in which Isaac Bennett, Charles Vinsen, William Mitchell, and Solomsn Hyman were fined, the police brought a snapshot taken by a policeman in concealment as evidence. The photograph showed one of the defendants receiving money fro in a man in the street. In a paper before the ltoyal Astronomical Society in London by Mr. T. C. Whitman, of Leeds, it was stated that a method of discovering the rotundity of the earth was obtained by examining the reflected image of the sun in still water when the orb was near the horison. The chairman said the method was a curious one and not widely known.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>EASTER VESTRY MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>EASTER VESTRY MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH. HARMONIOUS PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>Almost perfect harmony characterised the proceedings at the annual vestry meeting in connection with the Warrington Parish Church which was held in the National Schools, Church-street. on Tuesday evening. There was a large attendance, the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) presiding. Amongst those present were the Revs. T. W. Barber, T. A. J. Ridpath, and 0. A. Dearden (assistant clergy), Messrs. J. G. Craik and S. Taylor (church-wardens),_ and Mr. E._A. White (vestry clerk).</p>
        <p>THE FINANCIAL POSITION. - Mr. S. TAYLOR (people's warden) presented the state- ment of accounts. The meeting, he said, would probably ask how it was there should be a deficit of £2o,but when they examined the balance sheet he thought they would admit that it was not a bad one at all. When they took into consideration that they had had the task of working off the c:ebt on the Church restoration fund, and that money had also been raised by means of the parochial tea and other functions, it would readily be seen that the amount of collections to be devoted to various purposes must have fallen somewhat. Mr. Taylor instanced several funds to which a certain amount was given each year and pointed out that the collections had fallen short of those sums, thus incurrinr, additional calls on the ordinary offertories. Including a special grant to the choir of £ll 35., the total deficit brought about in the way he had named was £37 17s. 4d. The offertories were certainly improving and he sincerely hoped that they would very soon have a credit balance. He had just completed his first year of office, and the position had not, he could assure them, been altogether a bed of roses. At the same time he felt it a great honour to represent the congregation in that capacity and, if it was their wish, he would be pleased to serve again. With regard to the Rector and assistant clergy, he could not speak too highly of them. The Rector had been kindness itself,notw ithstanding the little differ- ence they might have had. (Applause.)ln making a slight allusion to what occurred at last year's vestry meeting, he would be very sorry indeed to see anything further take place. (Applauso). As the Rector or minister of the parish, they could not possibly have a better man than Canon Willis. (Applause.) The same remark applied to the assistant clergy who had left the parish and to those who had succeeded them.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. DAVIES moved the adoption of the balance sheet and remarked that Mr. Taylor had fully justified their expectations of him. (Hear, hear.) He (the speaker) was extreinely sorry there was a deficit of £2O, but considering the bad year of trade and other circumstances, bethought they had every reason to be thankful. He did not by any means despair of the ordinary collections reaching £lO per Sunday. Last Sunday week they approached to within two or three shillings of that amount. Mr. MASON seconded. THE SERVICES OF THE MIRFIELD CLERGY.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. DAINTITII put several questions concerning the administration of the Thomas May and james Morris charities.' He also remarked that £lO seemed to be a great deal to pay to!the Community of the Ressurecti on at Mir- field.Consideringlthey had such a difficulty in making ends meet would it not be possible to do without the services of a missioner from the Community and save the expense With regard to the collections he thought that consider- ing the large congregations, the amounts raised were very poor. Mr. J. G. CRAIK (Rector's warden) said he did not think it would give him much trouble to answer Mr. Daintith's "paltry charges." That gentleman appeared to be sadly in need of something to grumble about. (Laughter.) With regard to the May and Morris chari- ties, Mr. Craik explained that they were administered in accordance with the terms of the bequests. In con- nection with the latter fund £3O had to be dis- tributed to the poor of the parish at Christmas- time. This year the wardens exceeded that amount, but they paid it out of their own money, so he did not see that there was anything to find fault with. Mr. Daintith had also alluded to the £l6 offertory which went to Mirfleld. He (Mr. Craik) thought the huge congregations which assembled on Good Friday and Easter Day proved conclusively that the people of the parish and of Warrington appreciated the services of the clergy of Mirfield. (Applause.) So long as the people did appreciate those services and attended in such large numbers, the wardens would be prepared to invite the Mirfield clergy to come to War- rington. (Applause.) He did not agree that the offer- tories were miserably poor. Taking them all the year round, they were a credit to the Parish Church or to any other church. The congregation was almost entirely a working class congregation,and the wardens were very well satisfied with the collections.</p>
        <p>The balance sheet was then unanimously approved. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The RECTOR nominated Mr. J. G. Craik to serve as his warden for the ensuing year. He believed the appointment was just as acceptable to the congregation as it was to him (Canon Willis). He could not have a kinder friend or a better man for the office. With regard to the people's warden, it was a very great grief to him to lose from that office Mr. John Dewhurst because he was an old resident and had served the town and parish BO faithfully. He would be leaving Warrington towards the end of this year. Failing Mr. Dewhurst, he could not wish for a kinder, a more energetic, or more courteous people's warden than Mr. Samuel Taylor. (Applause.) On the motion of Mr. WI. ROGERS, seconded by Mr. LUST, Mr. Samuel Taylor was unanimously re-elected people's warden. Mr. Craik and Nlr. Taylor briefly returned thanks and nominated Mr. W. E. Atkin and Mr. James Wood res- pectively as their sidesmen. The other sidesmen were elected as follows :—Parish Church, Messrs. S. Davies, E. Isherwood, 11. Antwis, R. Collin, F. G. Taylor, W. Rogers, W. Daintith, and J. Dewhurst; St. George's Mission, Mr. Joseph Wilson; St. Clement's Mission, Mr._Charlesvvorth. .</p>
        <p>Mr. J D. Backton and Mr. James Woods were unani- mously re-elected lay representatives. In returning thanks Mr. BUCKTON interestingly detailed the duties of lay representatives. He referred especially to the part which the House of Lyman took in church affairs pointing out that while it had no authority it could express opinions on church questions which had weight with'the Houses of Convocation. On the motion of Mr. CRACK, seconded by Mr F. G. TAYLOR, Mr. E. A. White was re-appointed vestry clerk and suitably returned thanks. _</p>
        <p>--Res—s-rs—. -13.-6-. 15'rice and J. T. Whittle were re-appointed auditors. .</p>
        <p>CHANCELLOR'S VISITATION.</p>
        <p>The fiECTOR announced that Ci;;;Cell;Espin's visita- tion would take place at the Parish Church on Thurs- day, May 4, at</p>
        <p>THE RECTOR'S ADVICE.</p>
        <p>The S. DAvIES in moving a vivo of thanks to the Rector for presiding said that whatever differences they might have with the Rector they all recognised his sterling worth as a clergyman and leader of his people. (Applause.) Mr. W. L. WILLIAMS seconded, and the motion vas heartily agreed to. The ItmeTon, in reply, said he could not say how thankful he was that they had had such a happy and successful meeting. With reference to the balance sheet, if they looked at it they would see that the receipts were £OOO-2500 from offertories,and £lOO from other sources. Of that £6OO, £5OO had been spent on parochial needs, and f'loo given to deserving objects outsida. It was perfectly true that there was a deficiency on the year of £2O, but the meeting would be pleased to know that up to the present date, April 25, the receipts were £3O more than they were last year. The reNipts up to April 25, 1901, wereabout.6l6o, and at the present date, April 25, 1905, £l9O. (Applause.) The whole amount for the renovation of the church was raised in October. Last Easter he announced that £2,135 had been raised but subsequently they received about £l5, and at the parochial sale in October 2230 was received, making the cost of the renovation £2,380. A large proportion of that amount was raised in the parish. Only last week the receipts were £35. Did that not show, as Mr. Craik had said, that they had reason to be thankful, and to appreciate the services of the community at Mirfleld ? (Applause.) The profits on the parochial tpri were £l5. In conclusion, the Rector said he was full of the deepest thankfulness for the services which had been held, and the addresses given, and he was sure they would make them all better and braver men. Let them be brave and stand up for what they believed to be right, and they might he quite sure that in the end truth would prevail. (Applause). HOLY TRINITY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Perfect unanimity irked the proceedings at the Easter vestry meeting in connection with the above church which was held on Tuesday evening. The Vicar (the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton) presided, and there were also present :—Aessrs. J. Halton, Welsby, Dawson, W. Randall, A. Walsh, Pickles, Dunbabin, and Dyke. - -</p>
        <p>FINAMC I AL. The balance sheet, which was approved, showed that the year commenced with a balance in hand of Bs. 3d. and ended with it debit balance of ss. The total income was £2BB 2s. lqd. a decrease as compared with the income during the previous year. The VICAR remarked that in connection with the curacy fund there was a decrease in the income of „819 due to the fact that only three collections were made on behalf of the fund, whereas four collections were made in the previous year. Mr. RowseNT submitted the accounts for the Woodall charity which showed that the income had been £l9 Gd. and that after meeting all payments there was left a small balance in. hand. ELECTION OF OFFICERS. The VICAR said they had a rule at Holy Trinity Church that they should have periodical changes in the churchwardens. During the coming year he thought however that it was not advisable they should have a change and he had therefore pleasure in again nominating Mr. Welsby as his warden. Mr. Welsby was re-electe4l to the office of people's warden. Messrs. Rowson, Harding, Gandy, Dunbabin, J. Bowcock, E. Duke, H. Ferguson W. J. Forshaw, J. Gerrard, W. Gill, G. Gill, J. liesketh, Joseph Heslceth, J. Hignett, T. Hunt, H. Milling, junr., U. W. Naylor, Pickles, E. Rose, Shirlaw, and A. Walsh. were elected sidesmen. Messrs. Rovvson and W. Gill were appointed lay representatives. Mr. J. F. F. White was re-elected vestry clerk, and Mr. H. A. Naylor was re-appointed d AU itor. THANKS. , The VICAR expressed his thanks to the wardens and</p>
        <p>to the choir for their services during the year. They had sustained a great loss in the death which they all deplored of Mr. Randall. They all knew him and felt his loss keenly. Since his death they had had to make some fresh arrangements, i and he hoped things would go on as satisfactorily as in the past.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>LEGACIES TO THE- CHURCH.</head>
        <p>LEGACIES TO THE- CHURCH.</p>
        <p>In reply to a cordial vote of thanks to himself and the assistant clergy the Vicar referred to legacies which had been bequeathed to the parish. He said Miss Whittle left £2O for him to do as he wished with for the benefit of the church, and it had helped them to steer through the curacy difficulty, as he had given £lO of it to the Trinity curacy fund and £lO to the Bank Quay curacy fund. Their old friend Miss Garven, who was, he thought, the oldest member of the church, left £lOO to Trinity Church for a repair fund ; £lOO for a similar fund to St. Luke's ; and £5O for Trinity day schools. He understood St. Luke's would also receive a handsome sum as the residuary legatee under the will of the late Rea. E. D. Garven. The parish was admirably served by his assistant clergy, and he thought he had always been fortunate in securing good assistants. ST. ANN'S CHURCH. The VICAR (Rev. James Davies) presided at the annual vestry meeting which was held in the vestry on Tuesday afternoon. There were also present :—Messrs. H. H. Bretherton, W. Thomas, and Herbert Woods (vestry cleric).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>THANKS.</head>
        <p>THANKS.</p>
        <p>The VICAR returned-M.1175. for the help he had received from the church wardens, sidesmen, Sunday school teachers, choir, and other workers in connection with the parish.</p>
        <p>ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The VICAR nominated Mr. Joseph Bunch to act as his warden for the ensuing year. Mr. Bretherton resigned the office of people's warden and Mr. E. Winter was elected to succeed him. The sidesmen were elected as follows :—Messrs. G. Armstrong, G. Gleave, J. E. Harrison, A. Owen, Fred Taylor, H. H. Bretherton, H. Corbett, Frank Taylor, and Green.</p>
        <p>Messrs. G. Armstrong and E. Winter were appointed lay representatives.</p>
        <p>TfIE ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>The vestry then re--BC:I-ye-cilia-Ai into a meeting of com- municants and the statement of accounts was presented by Mr. Bretherton. The year commenced with a credit balance of 13s. 10d. The general offertories amounted to £3l. Ifis. 101 d. and the special offertories totalled £27 ss. Bd., the number of coins collected being 13,713. The total receipts were £9l 16s. 41d. and after deducting expenses there was a balance in hand of £2 11s. Old. Against this there was an outstanding account of £3 18s. Bd. It had also to be noted that Messrs. E. Forshaw and Sons, account of £3B 15s. Gd. for new boiler and fittings in connection with the church heating apparatus was advanced from the Vicar from funds under his control. As compared with last years accounts, the receipts were nearly £l3 less, but there had been a considerable redac- tion in items of expenditure. The liabilities at the beginning of the year were £l3 Os. sd, including the cost of the heating apparatus improvement which, as stated above, had been defrayed from special funds. The statement, which was considered very satisfac- tory, was adopted. The VICAR remarked that the position would have been much better if everyone contributed to the extent that some did, and if.those who were absent occasion- ally forwarded the sum which they usually gave to the offertory. ST. THOMAS'S STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>The Easter vestry meeting in connection with the above Church, was held on Tuesday. The vicar pre- sided and there were also present Messrs. Henry Greenall (vicar's warden), %. Richardson (people's warden), F. Pearson (vestry clerk), A. Down, S. W. Hallow, T. Sharrocks, F. Rose, F. Timperley and Spence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>SATISFACTORY BALANCE</head>
        <p>A SATISFACTORY BALANCE</p>
        <p>Mr. Richardson presented the statement of accounts which showed that the year commenced with a debit balance of £5 Is. and ended with a debit balance of £1 Bs. id. The receipts for the year amounted to £360 Bs. 10d. as against £353 Os. 6d. for the previous year. The general offertory amounted to £245 17s. Id. as against £227 Os. 6d. for the previous year. General satisfaction was expressed at the balance sheet which was adopted.</p>
        <p>ELECTIONS. The Rev. H. V. Pigot re-appointed Mr. Henry Greenall as his warden for the ensuing year. Although Mr. Greonall Was legally exempted from holding the office he pressed him to take it. Mr. Greenall consented to again accept the office though he thought it would have been better had some- one else been appointed. Mr. W. Richardson was unanimously re-elected people's warden. Messrs. T. J. Lee, A. Down, J. H. Clarke, J. Jeffes, T. R. Westbrook, J. A. McGregor, W. ;;Mclntvre, S. W. Dallow, T. Sharrocks, L. G. Davies, J. Parkes, and A. J. Willett were appointed sidesmen. Messrs. Sharrocks and McGregor were re-elected lay representatives.</p>
        <p>VOTE OF SYMPATHY. It was decided to send a vote of sympathy to the Rev. C. A. M. Evans (curate) in his prolonged illness.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANK 9. The Chairman desired to recognize the most efficient services which had been rendered during the year by the choir. They had every reason to be more than satisfied with the way the services were rendered in the Church.(Hear, hear.) He moved a vote of thanks to the choir for their services.</p>
        <p>Mr. Horrocks seconded and the motion was heartily carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rose briefly returned thanks. With regard to the work of the choir, Mr. Pigot said he had received a letter from a gentleman who desired to be anonymous, offering to take the choir boys to Blackpool or Llandudno for a holiday and to defray all expenses. (Hear, hear.) He (the Vicar) halil replied accepting the generous offer. The churchwardens and sidesmen were thanked for their past services, Mr. W. Richardson briefly responding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NEW FUND OPENED.</head>
        <p>NEW FUND OPENED.</p>
        <p>It was decided to Opf-M-sPOCTiti-ireachers'*fund, and several subscriptions were promised at the meeting. IVIRS. DOWN'S GENEROSITY. _</p>
        <p>The Vicar announced that new books had been pur- chased, and that Mrs. Downs had kindly defrayed the cost of the shelves in which to keep them, and she was thanked for her kindness. ST. PETER'S CHURCH. The Vicar (the Rev. B. Percival) presided over the Easter vestry meeting which was held on Wednesday evening. There were also present the Rev. D. James (curate), Councillor John Roberts, Messrs. J. Barnforth, J. W. Kay, W. Stanley, Joseph Taylor, J. H. Webb, J. Boardman, J. Cowdell, F. Webster, T.Reddish,J.Clayton, T. S. Steel, P. Green, John Taylor, F. Lashford, and P. Edgar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>FP,TANCIA.L.</head>
        <p>FP,TANCIA.L.</p>
        <p>The statement of accounts, which is adopted, showed that the year commenced with a balance in hand of 19s. 4d. and ended with a balance in hand of 6s. 3d. The income was £199 18s. ld.</p>
        <p>F.LECTION OF OFETCERS. MR. P. EDGAR RESIGNS TILE OFFICE OF ORGANIST.</p>
        <p>The VICAR said he would like to congratulate the churchwardens upon the very satisfactory balance sheet. the income had been larger than in any previous year and was £l6 higher than in 1903. Considering that in the past year the state of trade had not improved he thought the increase in the income was most satisfac- tory. Trade, if anything had been a little worse and as the parish consisted chiefly of workingmen they had consequently been rather badly hit. Last year he expressed the hope that they would be able to give £25 to the Liverpool Cathedral Fund, and they had actually given .£2O 7s. 100.—a very satisfactory. sum. (Hear, hear). At the last Easter vestry meeting he mentioned the fact that they required new kneelers, and during the year £l2 15s. had been raised and 200 kneelers recovered. A similar sum would be required this year to complete the work. Owing to the church being so spacious and lofty, it wal difilerilt to hear the preacher at the back of the building. He had thought of various schemes which they could adopt to remedy the defect, and he hoped they would be able to do something in that direction during the present year. (Hear, hear.) He expressed his sincere thanks to his colleague, Mr. James, and the Church officers for their hearty and earnest help daring the year. It was very pleasant to work with them on account of their loyal, hearty, and sympathetic support. He was glad to possess such excellent colleagues, and could aesnre them he had never a moment's uneasiness during the year. He also desired most heartily to thank the organist, choir and parish visitors. Their paid organist Mr. P. Edgar, had resigned the position which he had held for almost 25 years and he was sure they all regretted the step which Mr. Edgar had taken. (Hear, hear.) He however had promised to continue to act as voluntary organist to serve whenever he was called upon. He had known Mr. Edgar for 12 years and had always found him to be most faithful and punctual in the carrying out of his duties—(hear, hear,)—and he thanked him most heartily for his past services. He hoped Mr. Webster, who had taken Mr. Edgar's place would be able to fulfill his duties as well as Mr. Edgar bad clone. He had pleasure in again nominating Coun- cillor Roberts as his warden. (Hear, hear.) Councillor ROBERTS' thanked the Vicar for again putting him in office, though he would have liked some- one else to have beanappointed. He (Councillor Roberts) claimed to be the oldest member of St. Peter's parish and was one of the sub-committee who had charge of the work of building the church. He thought St. Peter's Church was a monument to the energy and perse- verance of those connected with the parish in olden times. He had always taken a deep interest in the church. He was sure everyone in the parish was proud , of their Vicar as he was sure they could not get a man who could be more faithful in the discharge of his duties. Councillor Roberts also spoke highly of the services rendered by Mr. Bamforth as people's warden and expressed the hope that he would be allowed to con- tinue in that office. Mr. KAY proposed the re-election of Mr. Bamforth as peoples warden. Mr. JOSEPH TAYLOR seconded, He contested the claim made by Councillor Roberts to be the oldest member of the church. He (Mr. Taylor) was connected with the Newton-street schools which were the real commence- ment of St. Peters Church. He therefore fclaisaed to have had a longer connection with the parish than Mr. Roberts.</p>
        <p>I ,had</p>
        <p>Mr. T. S. STEM. suggested that a committee he formed to consider the question of taking steps to im- prove the acconstic properties of the church. He pro- posed the committee be.cornposed of the Vicar, the Churchwardens and Messrs. Kay and Joseph Taylor. Mr. STEEL'S proposition was unanimously carried Mr. Steel's name being added to the committee. A vote of thanks Co the chairman concluded, the meeting.</p>
        <p>Mr. STEEL supported the nomination which was on- progress was again written largely across it. The total animously adopted, receipts amounted to £l4B Os. 4d., including collections The following wereappointed sidesmen: Messrs. Kay, for church expenses totalling £72 12s. 2d., and special Stanley, Joseph Taylor, J. Cowden. R. Knight, J. J. collections to the amount of £42 4s. 3d. After paving Hands, James Taylor, Armstrong, E. Clayton, P. Green, all expenses the wardens were able to hand over Z 2.5 to T. S. Steel, F. Lashforn, J. Heron. John Taylor, T. the curate's fund, ,E 5 to the decoration fund, and Reddish, W. H. Williams and Hebbs, Messrs, P. Edgar, Xi Hs. 2d. to the church extension fund. Mr. Proud and W. Kay, were appointed lay representatives. The pointed out that the income was nearly £l2 more than auditors, Messrs. Steel and Webster, were re-elected as last :7ear. was also the vestry clerk, Mr. P. Edgar. I The VICAR said they had been able to note slow but Mr. T. S. STEEL proposed a vote of thanks to the ' sure progress at every vestry meeting over which he choir for their services during the year and also moved had presided. Not only had there been a steady a vote of appreciation of the services rendered by Mr. growth in the congregations attending the ordinary Edgar during the past 25 years. He was sure the con- services, but the number of communicants had increased, gregation appreciated Mr. Edgar's work, it was a as had also the funds collected each year. matter of congratulation that Mr. Edgar would still 1 THE VICAR THANKED. be connected with the church and would be ready to I In proposing a vote of thanks to the Vicar for pre- perform the duties Of organist whenever° called siding, Mr. PROUD expressed the hope that the progress upon. (Hear, hear.) of the church would be maintained,and that eventually Mr. COWDELL seconded and Councillor ROBERTS sup- they would have the satisfaction of seeing the building. ported the motion which was heartily carried, completed. Mr. EDGAR said he heartily appreciated the vote of Mr. DOWLING seconded, and the motion was agreed to. thanks. He could assure them that it was no light task The VICAR acknowledged the compliment and said all for him to relinquish the duties of organist after having praise was due to the congregation, which was an served the church for 25 years in that capacity. He entirely working-class one, for the way in which it sup- however, felt that the time had come uthen he should ported the church. make way for a more active and enthusiastic man. He would continue to take a great interest in the church as ST. MARY'S CHURCH, GREAT SAN KEY. he lived. (Applause.) The Easter vestry meeting was held on Tuesday even- TICE ACCOUSTIC PROPERTIES OF THE CHURCH. ins. The Rev. J. B. Jones, vicar, presided id the-</p>
        <p>. am_ „aere were also present—Messrs. H. P. C. White and T. E. Shoobridge (wardens), Mr. W. J. Jeans, sen. (vestry clerk), and Messrs. H. Cartwright, N. Hatton, W. Woods, John Ray, P. Woolacott, H. Fairclough, F. W. Gilbert, J. Goulden, J. B. Aitken, W. A. Bingham, and J. Plumpton.</p>
        <p>THE BALANCE SHEET. The statement of accounts showed that the receipts amounted to £251 18s., and the expenditure £234 is. 4d. The Vicar announced that he had received a cheque for £lO from Miss Bolton for general expenses which amount would be carried over to next year's account. rhe balance sheet was passed, and a vote of thanks was accorded to Miss Bolton for her kindness, on the motion of Mr. Ray, seconded by "Ir. Hatton..</p>
        <p>A_PPOINTMENTS. FAIRCLOUGH was appointed vicar's warden. and Mr. Wm. Woods the people's warden and Messrs. H. P. C. White, Thomas E. Shoobridge. George Clarke, J. B. Aitken, P. Pownall, James Ray, Wm. Hodgkinson, H. Cartwright, H. Owen, W. A. Bingham, J. Millington, John Ray and W. Taylor were elected sidesmen : Mr. George Clarke was re-elected auditor, and Messrs. H. P. C. White and T. E. Shoobridge were elected lay representatives.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS. A vote of thanks was passed to the churchwardens, sidesmen, auditors, Sunday school 'teachers, organist. choirmaster, choristers, and all the church workers, for their services.</p>
        <p>CHOIRMASTER WANTED. Mr. AITKEN moved that a choirmaster lc* engaged to take the work off the Vicar's shoulders. They could easily afford to have a paid man as they had done in the past, and it was the church's duty to provide one. Mr. Ray seconded. The Rev. J. R. Jones remarked he had often said outside the Church Room that he would like to see a choirmaster appointed. He certainly had not enough time to devote to that work and he thought the choir would get on better if they had a choirmaster as they formerly had. The matter will be dealt with at a later date.</p>
        <p>THE CHAIRMAN THANKED. On the motion of Mr. Jeans,seconded by Mr. Fairclough a vote of thanks was passed to the Chairman. The Vicar said he was extremely thankful to the parishioners for their kind sympathy in the great sorrow he had sustained in the lois of his eldest son I (Ist September. He had received much strength and courage by their practical sympathy.</p>
        <p>STRE rTON.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHURCH, WALTON. The vestry meeting was held on Easter 'Monday, tho Vicar(the ,Rev. L. J. T. Darwall) presiding. The state- ment of accounts showed a satisfactory balance in hand The churchwardens (Sir Gilbert Greenall,Bart., and Mr. William Weir) were re-elected to their respective offices. Mr. William Yarmood was chosen to fill the vacancy amongst the sidesmen the others being re-elected. A vote of thanks was passed to the organist (Mr. Booth) and the members of the church choir, for the pains they had taken with the services.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>The Easter vestry meeting in connection with St. Matthew's Church was held on Monday evening. The Vicar, the Rev. and Hon. C. E. Cross presided. The churchwardens' accounts showed an adverse balance of £37, the deficiency for the year just ended being £3. During the past year special appeal was made to the parishioners, and others interested in the church, for donations] towards cleaning and thorough renovating the organ, painting various parts of the building, re- hanging the bells, and other necessary work to which 5 most liberal response had been given. It was stated that the work to the organ had been completed,the bells had been re-hung, and some of the painting had been done. The remainder would be finished next month if the weather was favourable. A balance of about £l6 remained of the special repair fund.—The retiring Vicar's warden was Mr. Hugh Darbyshire and the people's warden Mr. Oswald W. Willett. The latter was appointed Vicar's warden, and Mr. George Blackshaw was selected as people's warden. Mr. Joseph Hewitt and Mr. Herbert Brocklehurst were appointed sidesmen.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>ST. JAMES'S LATCHFORD. The vestry meeting was held in the schools on Thurs- day evening, the Rev. H. B. Firth (vicar) presiding. The ' oliicers appointed were :—St. James's church, Vicar's warden, Mr. J. Fargher ; people's warden, Mr. W. Eckersley : sidesmen, Alderman Hutchinson, Messrs.. , Astley, Calvert, Acton, A. Grounds, Brayshaw• Percival and Davies ; lay representatives, Messrs. H. N. Houghton and C. S. Burgess ; St. Hilda's Mission: Vicar's warden, Mr. Tonge ; people's warden. Mr. Rathbone ; sidesmen, Messrs. E. Lee and Joseph Grounds. Mr. Farghar presented the statement of accounts which showed that the year commenced with a balance in hand of £1 6s. Bd., and closed with a debit balance of 18s. 11d.</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>11 1 1 1 , 1 1 . , 1 1 I , , r</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Business of the Next Meeting.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tueschy evening next in the Town fiAll at 6 o'clock to consider the following and other business.</p>
        <p>FINANCg COMNIITTEE.-LOANS SANCTIONED.</p>
        <p>Tae Tows CLERK submitted the sanction of the Local Government Board to the borrowing of £6,130 for the purposes of the Technical School; to the borrowing of £3,252. for Private Street Works; and to the borrowing of the sum of £2,931 for the purchase of land near Wash-lane as a site for a Public Elementary School. The Committee also resolved that the Chairman, and failing him the Deputy-Chairman, with the Borough Treasurer, be appointed to attend the Annual General Meeting of the Institute of INlunicipal Treasurers and Accountants, to be held on tho 14th, 13th, and 16th June next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>ST..PAUL'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST..PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at the vestry meeting in connection with this church which was held in the Kenkrick-street schools on Thursday evening. The Vicar (Rev. W. Bracecamp) presided and was supported by Messrs. J. H. Smethurst and J. Rigby (wardens) ; and Mr. W. D. Jeans (vestry clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>TrEE ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>TrEE ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>Arr.R [GEV present-edil;c:;-S-Casie;-ent of accounts which disclosed a very satisfactory state of affairs. The special receipts amounted to £Bl 15s. 7d. and the offertories to £406 125. 9d. This, together with £l5 2s. 3d. from the Bible class special account,brought the total income to £503 10s. 7d. The donations to various outside and Parochial objects included £33 3s. 4d. to the Infirmary, £35 is. 7d. to the poor fund, £ll 9s. 6d. to the Waifs' and Strays' Society and £3161. to the District Nursing Association. The year commenced with a debit balance of ss. 6d. and, after paying all expenses, they had now a balance in hand of Is. 7d. He regretted that the receipts were not quite as large as last year. but he was glad to state that the offertories so far this year showed an increase of £l3 as compared with the same period in 1901. During the past month the collections amounted to .CB7 14s. 4d. Easter Day being a record in all respects. With regard to the endowment fund £lOO had been paid over to the Liverpool Diocesan Board and the balance due to the wardens had been reduced from £95 ss. Bd. at the beginning of the year to £67 18s. 2d. On the Church restoration fund there was a credit balance of £3 ss. sd. after carrying out repairs involving the expenditure of £74 ss.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. J. H. SMETHURST presented the accounts for All Saints' Mission, showing a balance in hand of Bs. 3d. He also stated with regard to the churchyard account that £2O 12s. 9d. had been spent on improvements, and there was a balance due to him of 10s. 3d. The Vlo.tit remarked that the £2O spent on the churchyard was money well spent. He did not think there was another churchyard in town which looked as well, and the improvement was a credit to Mr. J. H. Smethurst who had charge of the work. , Mr. J. W. PARKER, who has acted as auditor, con- gratulated the wardens on the admirable way in which the books were kept. On the motion of Mr. H. F. GREENOUGH, seconded by Mr. THomrso.N, the balance sheet was unanimously , adopted.</p>
        <p>THE VICAR AND TIIE YEAR'S WORK. The VICAR said he thought the balance sheet showed I that the church Naas in a very satisfactory condition., During the year they had collected in the church over £5OO. To that must be added the pew rents and £3OO raised by the sale of work. That meant that during the year they had raised over £l,OOO. it was I perfectly true that the receipts did show a small decrease, but he thought it could be explained by a slight falling off in the collections during the summer months, and the loss of the Christmas collection owing I to Christmas Day falling on Sunday. They had great cause to be thankful in the way the accounts had worked out. During the year they had done a great deal in connection with the endowment fund, with the result that the endowment had been increased by £lB. The amount received in the collec- tions for the purpose—£2oo—had been paid over to the Diocesan Board in Liverpool. That body made them a grant of £loo, sand the £3OO was forwarded to the Ecclesiastical Commissioners, who met it with £3OO. The endowment now stood at £l2B. They applied for another grant from the Commissioners this year, but they were not successful because the Commissioners thought their collections were so good that they did not need one. (Laughter.) He, however, thought that if a church tried to help itself, that was all the more reason why the Commissioners should assist it. He was greatly cheered by the attendances at the Easter Dayiservices, the communicants numbering 462. That was a larger number than they had had at any celebra- tions previously. He rejoiced in it the more, because it seemed to him as if a true revival was taking placekin their midst on Church lines. Having thanked the Rev. R. H. Bowen (curate), the officers, and congregation for their services, the Vicar remarked that St. Paul's had not the poorest congregation in Warrington, but the Church was certainly the poorest in regard to the endow- ment. He hoped the endowment fund would continue to be well supported in order to make it easier for the Vicars who had to follow him. The increase in the endowment resulted in the freeing of 54 additional sittings. The total seating accommodation of the Church was 905, and 564 of the seats were free. He hoped in the near future to be able to rai3o sufficient to clean the Church, and it would be exceedingly cheering if some kind friend would give them money to make a chancel. (Hear, hear). In conclusion the Vicar con- tradicted a statement which he said had been made that some of the offertories were helped by £lO or £5 note 'donations. The largest sum which had been put in the plate whilst he had had charge of the parish was a sovereign. Everyone who attended the church tried to do his level best. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The VICAR nominated Mr. J. H. Smethurst to act as his warden for the ensuing year. In Mr. Smethurst he had a true friend and the church a loyal worker. Mr. J. RIGBY was unanimously re-elected people's warden on the proposition of Mr. H. F. Greenough seconded by Mr. F. Bennett. Mr. SMETHURST nominated Mr. J. F. Lord SS his sidesman and Mr. Rigby nominated Mr. Henry Collin. The other sidesmen were elected as follows :—St' Paul's Church: Messrs. J. H. Hayes, T. D. Bate, P. ' Warburton, H. L. Clarke, J. Houghton, J. Pennington, J. S. Bate, H. F. Greenough, J. Hankinson, J. Lawton, J. W. Parker, E. Powell, J. Wainwright, E. Healey, A. C. Binks, J. W. Robinson, R. Hosker, F. Bennett, H. Broadhurst, R. S. Finney, W. E. Collins T. Locker, H. B. Houghton W. Flood, E. Andrews, .1. H. J. Hampson and T. Atherton; All Saints' Mission: Messrs. W. Kenwright, Thomas Addison, jun., a Darbyshire, J. Narroway and T. Spencer. Messrs. J. Richardson and A. J. Jolley were re-elected lay representatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>TIIANKS.</head>
        <p>TIIANKS.</p>
        <p>A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the church officers, choir-master and choir, on the proposition of Mr. O'BRIEN seconded by Mr. EVERETT. In returning thanks Mr. SMETHURST said they were all extremely proud of Belonging to the Church. The Vicar had made a slight mistake in his statement, as on Easter Day, for the first time in the history of the church, there was a £5 note in the collection. It had beeu said that the Hospital Sunday colle3tioos were helped up by notes &amp;c., but they were not. To prove that the money did not come from the few but from the many who attended, he would point out that the coins collected daring the year numbered 50,833. That, he thought, would want a lot of beating so far as this part of the diocese was con- cerned.</p>
        <p>THE VICAR'S EASTER OFFERING.</p>
        <p>Nrr.Rigbihanded the Vicar a cheq9e for £25 14s. Rd., being the amount of the Easter offering from the con- gregation. The VICAR thnnked the congregation most sincerely for their gift. He little thought he would receive so large an amount when he considered the expenses and the heavy collections they had, had during the year. He hoped he might prove worthy of their confidence in the future. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PARISIi CLERK.</head>
        <p>PARISIi CLERK.</p>
        <p>It was decided that 22 2s. be paid to the Parish Clerk for his services at baptisms, instead of making him an allowance from the offerings made at baptisms. The meeting closed with heatty votes of thanks to the Vicar and Vestry Clerk. ST. BARNABAS' CFIURCII. The Vicar (Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth) presided at the annual vestry meeting, which was held on Wednes- day evening. There were also present :—Nressrs. NNT. A. Proud, J. Iddon, C. Skinner, F. J. Dowling, E. Evans, E. Iddon, and W. P. James.</p>
        <p>ELECTIONS. The VICAR nominated Mr. J. Iddon to act as his warden for the ensuing year. On the motion of Mr..J I n oce.sr,seconded by Mr.E VANS, Mr. W. A Proud was unanimously re-elected people's warden. • The VICAR nominated as his sidesmen : Messrs. G. Rolfe, J. J. Siddall, and J. Tomkins ; while Mr. Proud nominated Messrs. Allen, F. J. Dowling and E. Evans. The other sidesrnen elected were :—Messrs. E. Iddon, W. P. James, C. Skinner, and P. Sudlow. Messrs. C. Skinner and E. Iddon were. appointed lay representatives. THANKS. The VICAR cordially thanked the officers and workers for their services during the year.</p>
        <p>SATISFACTORY' FINANCIAL POSTTION. In presenting the balance sheet Mr. PROUD said that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-04-29_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>ASSOCIATION CUP FINAL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION CUP FINAL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ALBIONS v. SUTTON COMMERCIAL. The final for the "Observer" cup on SaturdaF attracted some 4,000 spectators to the Walker's Brewery* Y. football ground in the rear of the King and Queen Hotel, Padgate-lane. The competing teams—Warring' ton Albions and Sutton Commercial—placed their strongest elevens in the field and a keen contest was witnessed. The Albions, who played with a stiff wind at their backs in the first half went off with a rusl, which promised them the victory. The game was ne' many minutes old however ere Sutton got their passing machinery in motion and Wyatt was called upon to deal with some good shots. Prom this period the possessed no features of special interest. Both teara in turn took up the attack but both however snffereo, from the same faults in decision and weakness in front: of the goal, and had either side possessed a man quicr' to take advantage of the many opportunities which Pre- sonted themselves, the game would not have resulted as it did in a pointless draw. The tie was re-played on Monday before 3,000 specta" tors and the result was no more satisfactory than it Was on the aturday. The Albions were handicapped by one of their full backs having early in tho game, to leave_ the field through injury. When the whistle sounded f.°' time neither side had scored and th game was Ion: longed for half an hour. The extra time was ,al unproductive and in the end it was decided that tbo _ team should become joint holders of the cup for the year, each team to hold it for six months.</p>
        <p>In the Grand Lodge of English Freemasons on Wednesday night, the Duke of Connaught was Pl° claimed as reinstalled Most Worshipful Grand Master, Earl Amherst was appointed Pro-Grand Master, and gr' T. F. Halsey, M.P. Deputy-Grand Master, The Grand Wards appointed were the Earl of Malmesbury and gr.. Allan Gibbs, M.P.</p>
