        <p>THE CHESHIRE CONSTITUENCIES, THE ELECTORAL ROLL FOR 1905. comprising four boroughs and eight county divisions. Of the boroughs, Birkenhead comes first with 16,588 electors and one member, while Stockport, with Growth of the Lancashire and Cheshire n electorate of 12,547 has two representatives. Chester an electorate are the remaining boroughs, the former Constituencies. having 7,799 electors and the latter 7,601. The County ' divisions are;—Wirral, 18,423; Eddisbury, 10,885; Macclesfield, 8,509; Crewe' 14,800 Northwich, 12,527; Altrincham, 15,877; Hyde, 11,079 ; and Knutsford 10,887. In 1885 the electorate of Cheshire was 112,345—nn average of 8,642 per member. To-day it is 147,622—an average of 11,355 per meniber. GLARING ELECTORAL ANOMALIES. Without wearying our readers with a mass of statistics, we may briefly indicate some of the more striking electoral inequalities in various parts of the kingdom. There are in all fifty constituencies with fewer than 5,000 electors and these have an aggregate electorate of 186,683, only 300 more than in the six greatest single- member constituencies. By way of comparison we show opposite to them the six smallest constituencies : GREAT CONSTITUENCIES. SMALL CONSTITUENCIES. Romford Division ... 44,012 Kilkenny Walthamston" „ ... 33,994 Newry Wandsworth 29,845 Durham 2,617 Harrow Division 27,368 Galway Cardiff District Handsworth Division 24,655 Sutherlandshire ... 2,821 -- 186,361 13.82 In the one group the average per 'member is 31,060, while in the other it is only 2,303. A vote in Kilkenny has 28 times the value of a vote in Rumford. A com- parison between the above small constituencies and several of the Lancashire divisions would be almost as striking, differing only in degree.</p>
