        <p>FRIENDLY SOCIETY SECURITIES. Druids' Investments. INQUIRY AT WARRINGTON, On the 15th March, an inquiry was held at the. Lion Hotel, Warrington, by Mr. 0. Dudley Barlow, an inspector appointed by the Chief Registrar of. Friendly Societies, into the investments of the Warrington Equalized District Branch of the Order of Druids Friendly Society. A request, signed by 100 members of the society, and countersigned by Mr. John \Vestal!, the Chief Secretary of the order, in accordance with the requirements of Section 76 of the Friendly Societies Act of 1896, had been,rnade to the Chief Registrar to order such an inquiry. The grounds of the application as set forth in the request were :—" The failure of the trustees to produce the branch securities to the duly appointed auditors at the proper time. After repeated requests it was found that some bonds amounting to £1,600 or thereabouts had been repaid and cancelled,and no notice given to the District Committee, nor had the money been placed to the credit of the branch. The District Secretary has requested to be furnished with particulars of the present securities, but the informa- tion had been refused." "A statutory declaration hereto annexed by David Houghton, Charles Bebbing- ton and Arthur Gourden, three of the present appli- cants, and by oral and documentary evidence, to prove that the present investments have not been authorized by the Branch Committee ; that the trustees have can- celled bonds without the consent of the Branch Com- mittee ; that the trustees have not credited the funds of the branch with the amounts received, but have invested (presumably) such moneys without the consent of the Branch Committee, and that both of the trustees and the solicitor who acted for them in relation to investments have refused to supply the District Secre- tary with the particulars of the present investments or the moneys in their hands, and that the trustees have been guilty of breaches of the rules of thebranch and of the Friendly Societies Acts." The Lords Commissioners of his Majesty's Treasury gave their consent to the appointment of the inspector. At the inquiry the applicants were represented by Mr. Thos. S. Steel, solicitor, Warrington, and the trustees, Messrs. William Bibby, George Roughley and Samuel Hankey, by Mr. F. R. Roberts, solicitor, War- rington, and the proceedings were Iratched on behalf of the Order of Druids by Mr. Verney Claytou,solicitor, Manchester. Witnesses were called, including Mr. , David Houghton, the late district secretary : Mr. Geo. W. Rigby and Mr. John Si. Connolly. auditors ; Mr. John T. 'Wilson, a former auditor ; Mr. Samuel Dun- bobbin, a former treasurer ; Mr. Ezra Eden, the present treasurer ; Mr. William Bibby, the senior trustee. After some discussion the Inspector ruled that it was inadvis- able that the Press should be represented, and that any particulars published should be obtained from his report. A copy of such report has been received by Mr. Steel, as representing the applicants and the particulars stated above, and following, have been forwarded to us for publication. The INSPECTOR states : "That the main ground of the application arose from the alleged non-production of the securities of the district to the auditors, and the failure of the trustees to satisfy the auditors arose primarily from the aggregation for the purposes of in- vestment of three funds, and the uncertainty of the officers as to the legal status of one of them. The following are the names of the three funds :—(1) War- rington Equalized District Branch of the Order of Druids ; (2) The Widow and Orphan Fund—Order of Druids ; (3) Warrington District Society of Druids Family Funeral Fund. When the district was registered the rules relating thereto provided under its objects:— Rule 2, for relieving the widows and orphans of deceased members ; rules 110 and 111 dealt with the objects applicable to this particular fund." Then follow copies of the rules. Continuing, the report says : "This widow and orphans fund was carried on under unregistered rules from its formation in IS6B,and it was the contention of the trustees that the original con- stitution was not affected by the rules registered as part of the district rules on sth January, 1893, and that the continuity of the society could be proved as dis- tinct from the district down to the present time. My answer to that plea was that prima facio the fund was identical with that under the registered rules, and that I could not impeach the accuracy of the annual returns made pursuant to the statute which had included the assets and liabilities and the investments of that fund. I understood the question had already been sub- mitted to the order. The total amount given on the annual return of the district for the year ending 31st December, 1903, as invested upon real securities, was £2,470, and it was the question of investments repre- senting this sum that I decided must be gone into, and that it would show to what funds this sum was applic- able." A list of the investments are then given. The Inspector further states :—" That the evidence showed that the auditors had during 1904 applied to the trustees for inspection and Sir. Connolly asked the Distrizt Secretary to arrange an appointment. Cern- munications in January and April were specially referred to, and the question was brought up and discussed at the September district meeting. On tho 11th of .Novem- ber, 1004, an appointment was made at Mrs. Bibby's -house, and the witness Connolly was shown securities to the value of £1,730. He had been furnished with a list of investments by the district secretary shown in the balance sheet, and the deeds produced did not agree with this list or with the total. By this time the annual return for 1901 was due, and the auditors, cot being satisfied as to the identity of the securities representing the fund under the registered rules, had declined to sign the certificates. In March, 1901, or three months after the application for inspection was made, a free- hold mortgage was found, and on the 9th of that month deeds were executed by the three trustees on behalf of the society for £1,645 on freehold securities. No valuation was obtained, the value accepted being that at which it, stood in the books of the company. Mr. Bibby appar- ently understood the expediency of valuations of property, as he stated in evidence that he always had one when advancing money of his own. He considered the greater margin required in the case of trust property was itself sufficient protection, but I think the com- mittee should, in the interests of the members whom they represent, very carefully consider this. It is</p>
